{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GJYjCddcQJgMCm7fo24Pgc","type":"tool_result","content":"      Exponential time hypothesis is false. . . but the verified\n    algorithm is slower than trying all 2n assignments at every\n                           practical size.\n\n                                             Alex Linhares\n                      Argo Lab AGI; on leave from the Getulio Vargas Foundation\n                           alex@argolab.org ORCID 0000-0002-4227-6879\n\n                                          November 5, 2026\n\n\n                                                 Abstract\n      In October 2026 OpenAI published a theorem, checked by the Lean proof assistant, that refutes\n      the Exponential Time Hypothesis: the 2001 conjecture that deciding whether a formula with\n      three variables per clause can be satisfied needs time exponential in the number of variables.\n      We read the verified statement exactly, rebuilt its proof in isolation, re-checked it with a\n      second, independent checker, and found no flaw. It does refute the hypothesis. What we add\n      is exactness about what is verified. The theorem is a guarantee, not a running time: it says\n      that a machine and some constants exist, and never computes them. Its exponent, the power\n      of two in the bound, is the one the manuscript itself calls weaker; the manuscript’s sharper\n      exponents are not in the Lean library. With every constant set to one, that exponent exceeds\n      the number of variables from 50 variables up to a number with 2,391 digits. So at every\n      practical size the verified guarantee is weaker than the guarantee of trying all 2n assignments.\n      We say what follows for the hypotheses, for the lower bounds proved from them, and for\n      anyone who runs a program on such formulas: nothing changes on any machine.\n\n\n1    Introduction\nIn late October 2026, OpenAI’s collection of machine-checked mathematics added a result dated\n25 October 2026 that it calls family 378, The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False (OpenAI, 2026).\nThe Exponential Time Hypothesis, ETH, is a conjecture from 2001 (Impagliazzo and Paturi, 2001).\nIt says that deciding whether a formula with three variables per clause can be satisfied needs time\nthat grows exponentially in the number of variables. The result comes with a 67-page manuscript\nand a proof checked by the Lean proof assistant, a program that verifies every step of a proof and\nrejects a proof with a gap. The verified theorem does refute the hypothesis. The title of this paper\nsays what else is true of it. Its sentence has four clauses. First, what is verified is a guarantee, not\na running time. The theorem says that a machine halts within a stated bound; it says nothing\nabout how long the machine actually takes on any input. Second, the bound contains constants\nthat the theorem only says exist. No value of any of them is computed anywhere. Where the\nproof does write a value down, the constant in the exponent is at least 257 (Section 2). Third,\nthe exponent, the quantity that two is raised to in the bound, is a fixed function of the number of\nvariables. With every constant set to one, it is larger than the number of variables at every size\n\n                                                     1\n\ffrom 50 variables up to a number with 2,391 digits (Section 2, Figure 2.1). Over that whole range\nthe verified bound is above 2n , the cost of trying every one of the 2n assignments of n variables.\nSo the verified guarantee is weaker, at every practical size, than the guarantee of the method\neveryone already has. Fourth, the manuscript states two sharper exponents, which would make\na different sentence. Neither is in the Lean library, and the manuscript itself calls the verified one\n“the weaker exponent” (Section 3).\n    This paper is a careful reading by people who checked, not a debunking. We read the verified\nstatement line by line. We inspected the proof’s source. We rebuilt the proof from source in\nisolation and re-checked it with a second proof checker that shares no code with Lean’s own. We\nfound no flaw: the statement means what the manuscript’s verified form says, and the proof rests\non the three standard axioms, the assumptions every ordinary Lean proof makes, and nothing\nelse. Every sentence that could read as “wrong” or “overclaimed” is replaced by what the Lean\nstatement and the manuscript actually say, with the file and line, or the page and line, beside it.\nNothing is quoted from memory. Every number has a script that regenerates it, named where\nthe number appears.\n\n1.1   The words we use\nEach section glosses its own terms where they first appear, so that a reader may enter anywhere.\nThe ones the whole paper needs are these, each in a sentence or two. A formula is a list of clauses,\neach clause an “or” of a few variables or negated variables. A choice of true or false for every\nvariable satisfies the formula when it makes every clause true. A k-CNF is a formula with at most\nk variables per clause, and k is called the clause width; 3-SAT is the problem of deciding whether\na 3-CNF can be satisfied. Trying all 2n assignments is the method of checking every one of the\n2n ways to set n variables; its cost is the yardstick in the title. ETH is the conjecture that 3-SAT\nneeds time exponential in n. SETH, its strong form, says that as k grows no algorithm for k-SAT\ndoes much better than trying all assignments. Several variants exist, and Section 6 names each.\nA guarantee is a bound a theorem promises; a running time is what a clock measures; the two\nare compared only with their own kind. Lean is the proof assistant. Its kernel is the small core\nthat does the final checking, and “verified” in this paper always means “accepted by the kernel”.\nMathlib is Lean’s community library of mathematics. The comparator is OpenAI’s checking tool. It\ntakes a challenge file, which states a theorem with the proof left blank, and a solution, which proves\nit. It checks that both state the same theorem and that the proof uses only the three standard\naxioms. nanoda is a second, independent checker for Lean proofs, written in another language.\nA replay is a rebuild of a proof from source on our own machine, inside a sandbox, an isolated\nenvironment that sees nothing else on the machine, at the pinned Lean version, the exact version\nthe repository names. A SAT solver is the program a practitioner runs to decide a formula, and a\nmodel counter the one that counts its satisfying assignments.\n\n1.2   What this paper does\nIn one paragraph. The verified statement (Section 2) says that for each fixed clause width there\nexist a finite machine of the textbook kind, a Turing machine with several tapes, and five positive\nconstants. On every formula of that width the machine halts with the exact number of satisfying\nassignments written on a tape. It does so within a bound: a constant, times a power of the input\nlength, times two raised to a constant times the exponent. The exponent is written out in Section 2.\n\n\n                                                  2\n\fIts ratio to the number of variables rises above one at 50, peaks at about 2.17 near a number\nwith 24 digits, and falls back below one only past a number with 2,391 digits. The manuscript\nclaims more than the statement (Section 3): two sharper exponents; a bound on the size of logic\ncircuits that depends on one of them; and a chapter of proofs by a technique of its own, tensor\ncontraction. We tag every claim verified, supported on paper by the verified statement, or paper-\nonly. We inspected the proof (Section 4): all 3,091 source files it depends on. They contain\nno axiom, no omitted proof, no step that trusts a compiled program, and no patch to Mathlib,\nand the statement they prove is the one the comparator posed. We replayed it (Section 5). The\n3,091 files built from source in a sandbox at the pinned Lean version in 33 minutes; the kernel\nreported the three standard axioms; and nanoda re-checked 95,176 declarations, the theorem\nand everything it depends on, with no error. We say what follows (Section 6). The statement\nalone refutes ETH, SETH, the gap form, the form for guessing (nondeterministic) machines, the\ncounting form, and the randomised forms of ETH, SETH and the gap form. A lower bound\nthat was proved from one of them is an “if . . . then” whose “if” is now false: it derives nothing,\nand the statement it concluded is not itself refuted. We say what it changes for a practitioner\n(Section 7): nothing on any machine. We show that by timing an ordinary SAT solver and two\nmodel counters on random formulas of up to 500 variables and drawing the two guarantees\nbeside the measurements. We restate the verified sentence in our own Lean library under one\nname, with its axioms audited (Section 8). And we place the paper beside what the collection, the\nhypotheses’ authors, a published human audit, and the two checking tools say about themselves\n(Section 9).\n\n1.3   What is claimed, and what is not\nClaimed, each with the section that establishes it:\n\n  1. The verified statement is a running-time guarantee with constants that exist and are never\n     computed, for each fixed clause width, with no lower limit on the number of variables\n     (Section 2).\n\n  2. With every constant set to one, the verified exponent exceeds the number of variables at\n     every size from 50 up to a number with 2,391 digits, so the verified bound is above 2n there.\n     The true constants are unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record, so with them the\n     bound is higher still (Section 2).\n\n  3. The manuscript’s two sharper exponents, and the circuit bound that rests on one of them,\n     are not in the Lean library (Section 3).\n\n  4. The proof’s source shows none of the known ways of weakening a verified proof, and its\n     statement is the challenge’s (Section 4). It builds from source at the pinned version with\n     the three standard axioms, and a second checker agrees (Section 5).\n\n  5. The verified statement refutes ETH, SETH, their gap, guessing and counting forms, and the\n     randomised forms of ETH, SETH and the gap form, by the parts of the statement named\n     in each case. A lower bound derived from any of them loses its premise and nothing more\n     (Section 6).\n\n\n\n\n                                                 3\n\f  6. On random formulas, the solvers a practitioner has are many orders of magnitude below\n     2n at every size measured, and the verified guarantee is above 2n from 50 variables on. The\n     verified result changes nothing that runs (Section 7).\n\nNot claimed:\n\n  1. No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. No implementation of it exists;\n     the title compares two guarantees.\n\n  2. No flaw in the statement, the proof, the Lean kernel, Mathlib, or the collection’s checking\n     tool. We found none and assert none.\n\n  3. No value of any constant beyond what the proof writes down, and nothing about what a\n     different proof of the same statement could achieve.\n\n  4. No judgement on the manuscript’s paper-only proofs, which were located and quoted, not\n     read for correctness.\n\n  5. No refutation of any lower bound’s conclusion, for any problem, and nothing about any\n     specific problem’s complexity beyond what the manuscript’s own remarks state.\n\n  6. Nothing about any problem outside satisfiability. This paper is about the limitation of the\n     ETH refutation and nothing else.\n\n1.4     How the paper is organised\nSection 2 reads the statement. Section 3 draws the line between it and the manuscript. Sections 4\nand 5 inspect and replay the proof. Section 6 says what follows. Section 7 is for the practitioner.\nSection 8 is our own Lean. Section 9 is related work. Section 10 concludes and lists the questions\nthis reading left for its author to decide. Every section ends with a list headed “what this does\nnot claim”. Appendix A lists every Lean name the paper cites. Appendix B holds the technical\ndetail behind the plain text of the body: the definitions with their line numbers, the theorem with\nits quantifiers, the spine of the proof. The OpenAI repository is read throughout at one commit,\n4eaee875, as untrusted data: with text tools only, never built or run in place. The one build is\nthe sandboxed replay.\n\n1.5     What this does not claim\n      • Nothing beyond the sections it summarises. Every sentence of this introduction is a sen-\n        tence of a later section said shorter; where the two differ, the later section, with its file and\n        line, is the one that counts.\n\n      • Nothing about the result’s importance. Whether the refutation matters is not a question\n        this paper asks; what the verified statement says, and at what sizes, is.\n\n      • No prior-art claim. “We found no earlier statement” of the title’s observation is bounded\n        to the six texts Section 9 names; the literature at large was not searched.\n\n\n\n\n                                                     4\n\f2     The verified statement, read exactly\n2.1   What we are reading, and the words we need\nOpenAI’s collection (OpenAI, 2026) holds, for the result it calls family 378, one machine-checked\ntheorem. It is named exact_count_and_sat. It is stated in the family’s challenge file, which\nis 220 lines long: lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean at commit\n4eaee875 of the collection. This section reads that file and says what the theorem asserts and\nwhat it does not. Line numbers are those of the file. We quote nothing from memory.\n     A few words first, each in one sentence. Lean is a proof assistant: a program that checks every\nstep of a proof and refuses a proof with a gap. The kernel is the small core of Lean that does the\nfinal checking; when we say a statement is verified, we mean the kernel accepted a proof of it. A\nformula here is a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form: a list of clauses, each clause an “or”\nof a few variables or negated variables, and the whole formula true when every clause is true. A\nk-CNF is such a formula whose clauses have at most k variables each; 3-SAT is the problem of\ndeciding whether a 3-CNF can be made true. A satisfying assignment is a choice of true or false\nfor every variable that makes the formula true; with n variables there are 2n assignments to try,\nand trying all 2n assignments is the method the title names. The comparator is OpenAI’s checking\ntool. It takes a challenge file, which states a theorem and leaves the proof blank, and a solution,\nwhich proves it, and checks that the two state the same theorem and that the proof uses only\nthree standard axioms. An axiom is a statement accepted without proof; the three standard ones\nare the ones every ordinary mathematical proof in Lean uses. The file we read is the challenge\nfile. It carries the statement with the placeholder sorry where the proof would go (line 218).\nThe solution proves the same statement in 3,091 files (Section 4). We rebuilt that proof ourselves\nand the kernel reported the three standard axioms and nothing else (Section 5).\n\n2.2   The definitions, in words\nThe theorem speaks about five kinds of object. Each is defined in the file before the theorem.\nAppendix B.1 gives every definition with its line numbers; here is what each one means.\n\nFormulas and the count (lines 13–51). A formula is a finite set of clauses over n variables, and\na clause is a finite set of signed variables. The formula F has width at most k when every clause\nhas at most k members (line 38). The number count F is how many of the 2n assignments make\nthe formula true (line 47). Because clauses and formulas are sets, a repeated clause collapses into\none; the empty clause is allowed and can never be satisfied; and a clause holding both a variable\nand its negation is allowed and counts as two members. The manuscript says the same thing in\nits own words: “Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k literals” (p. 1, line 44).\n\nThe input encoding (lines 53–81). The machine does not read a formula; it reads a string of\nbits. The file fixes how a formula becomes bits. Numbers are written in unary, that is, n is written\nas n ones and a zero. Each clause becomes a sorted list of such numbers, the formula becomes\na list of clauses, and the number of variables is written in front. Two things matter. First, the\nnumber of variables is part of the input, so a variable used in no clause still counts. Second, the\nstring is not long: its length is a polynomial in n and m, which means it grows like a fixed power\n\n\n\n                                                 5\n\fof them and not like 2n . A formula with m clauses of width k on n variables takes at most about\n8 k n m bits. In symbols, with L the length of the string,\n                                                           \u0001\n                           L ≤ n + 2 + m 2k (4n + 1) + 4 = O(k n m),\n\nwhere O(k n m) is read “at most a fixed multiple of k n m”. A formula with 125 variables, 500\nclauses and width 4 becomes 1,006,223 bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width 3\nbecomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that imitates the definitions, reports/\nscratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py. Nothing in the encoding grows exponentially.\n\nThe machine (lines 83–128). The machine is a Turing machine, the textbook model of a com-\nputer: a finite control that reads and writes symbols on tapes, one cell at a time. This one has\nseveral tapes: a finite table of states, a finite number of tapes, and a rule that, in each state, reads\none cell of one tape and then either stops with a yes-or-no answer, or writes one symbol, or moves\none head by one cell. Every part of it is finite. A step is one lookup in a table with three rows\nper state. No part of the machine can hide an unbounded computation inside a step. The tapes\nand the moves are Mathlib’s standard ones (Mathlib is the community library of mathematics for\nLean, at the revision the collection pins, d13f23b7).\n\nRuns (lines 87–90). A configuration is a snapshot: the control’s current state and the contents of\nevery tape. A trace of length t is a record that the machine, started in one configuration, reached\nanother in exactly t successful steps. The step rule is a function, so each run is determined by its\ninput; there is no randomness and no guessing.\n\nSpace monitoring (lines 147–189). Beside each configuration the file keeps, for every tape, the\nfurthest cells the head has reached to the left and to the right. The visited space is the total number\nof cells ever visited, summed over the tapes. The count word is a number written in binary, least\nsignificant bit first (line 147).\n\nThe exponent and the bound (lines 130–141). The theorem’s running-time bound is built from\none function of n, which the file calls runtimeScale and we write ρ(n):\n                                                               \u00012\n                                            n ln ln(n + 16)\n                                     ρ(n) =   p                     ,\n                                                ln(n + 16)\n\nwith ln the natural logarithm. In words: take n; multiply it by the square of the logarithm of the\nlogarithm of n + 16; divide by the square root of the logarithm of n + 16. The manuscript uses the\nsame logarithm: “Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm” (p. 1, lines 48–49; again at p. 7,\nlines 326–327). The +16 keeps the double logarithm defined and positive at every n, including\nn = 0, so the theorem needs no condition on n. The bound itself, called runtimeBound (lines\n136–139), is\n                                        C ( L + 1) d 2 A ρ ( n ) ,\nthat is, a constant C, times a power d of the input length L, times two raised to A times ρ(n). We\ncall ρ(n) the exponent and C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) the bound throughout the paper. The constants C, A\nand d are the subject of the next subsection.\n\n\n                                                   6\n\f2.3     What the theorem says, and in what order\nLines 194–217 state the theorem. Appendix B.2 displays it with every quantifier and the line of\nevery clause. In words, for every fixed width k of at least 2:\n\n        There exist a machine, a count tape, an answer tape, and five positive constants\n        C, A, S, d, e, such that for every number of variables n and every formula F on n vari-\n        ables of width at most k, the machine started on the encoding of F halts after some\n        number of steps, time, with the count of satisfying assignments written in binary on\n        the count tape, the bit “is the count positive” on the answer tape and in its halting\n        state, a count word of at most n + 1 bits, time at most C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) , and never\n        more than S ( L + 1)e cells visited.\n\n      Three features of the order of the words matter for everything that follows.\n\nThe machine and the constants come after k and before n. For each width k there is one\nmachine and one set of constants, and they serve every formula of every size. They may depend\non k and on nothing else. This is what complexity theorists write as Ok (1): a constant that\ndepends only on k.\n\nThere is no lower limit on n. The manuscript’s Theorem 1.1 and its Proposition 6.4 are stated\nfor n ≥ 20 (p. 2, lines 56–57; p. 43, lines 2474–2475). The verified theorem has no such condition;\nthe +16 in the exponent is what makes that possible.\n\nThe constants exist, and that is all. The theorem says that values of C, A, S, d, e exist. It names\nnone of them. The proof obtains them from other existence lemmas (a lemma is a small theorem\nproved on the way to a larger one) and never writes a number down. We read the proof for\nthe values it does pin: at the point where the main run is assembled, the exponent constant\nis set to A = 256 + At with At at least 1 (SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean, line\n51; SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean, lines 792–793). So the proof as written has\nA ≥ 257, and the theorem that the comparator exhibits adds one more (Section 4, A ≥ 258). No\nclosed value of any of the five constants exists anywhere. The machine is explicit in one respect:\nit is produced mechanically (“compiled”) from a program that uses 80 one-bit memory cells\n(“registers”) and 1,220 tapes (SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 98–101). How many states it\nhas is never computed.\n\n2.4     The exponent, in numbers\nEverything the title says rests on one comparison: the exponent ρ(n) against n itself. If ρ(n) is\nbelow n, the bound’s exponential part 2ρ(n) is below 2n , the cost of trying every assignment. If\nρ(n) is above n, it is not.\n                                                                              √\n    The ratio ρ(n)/n depends only on y = ln(n + 16): it equals (ln y)2 / y. That ratio starts\nat 0.64 for n = 1 and rises. It crosses 1 between n = 49 and n = 50: ρ(49)/49 = 0.9994\nand ρ(50)/50 = 1.0027. It keeps rising to a peak of 16/e2 = 2.165, reached when ln y = 4,\nwhich is at n ≈ 1023.7 , a number with 24 digits. Then it falls, and it is back below 1 only when\ny = 5503.7, which is at n ≈ e5504 ≈ 102390 , a number with 2,391 digits. Table 2.1 gives the values\n\n\n                                                  7\n\fand Figures 2.1 and 2.2 draw them. Every number is printed by paper3/scripts/runtime_\nscale.py, run under python3 -I; it imports nothing from the clone and works in y, so that\nno n beyond the range of floating-point arithmetic is ever formed.\n   Two facts are true at once, and the title is about the second. In the limit, ρ(n)/n goes to 0:\nthe exponent grows more slowly than n, which is what “subexponential” means, and that is a\ntheorem in our companion Lean (Appendix A, A.6). And on every size anyone will ever write\ndown, from 50 to a number with 2,391 digits, the exponent is above n.\n\nTable 2.1: The verified exponent compared with n. The second column is the ratio ρ(n)/n; where it is\nabove 1, the bound’s exponential part 2ρ(n) (with A = 1) is       n\n                                                          pabove 2 , the cost of trying every assignment.\n                                                        2\nThe third column is ρ(n) itself. ρ(n) = n(ln ln(n + 16)) / ln(n + 16), the comparator’s runtimeScale\n(lines 132–134), natural logarithms. Regenerate: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py.\n\n                                             n    ρ(n)/n           ρ(n)\n                                              1    0.6443          0.64\n                                             20    0.8606         17.21\n                                             49    0.9994         48.97\n                                             50    1.0027         50.14\n                                            100    1.1146        111.46\n                                            125    1.1495        143.69\n                                            103    1.4229       1422.88\n                                            106    1.8550    1.85 × 106\n                                           1012    2.0956   2.10 × 1012\n                                         1023.7    2.1654            —\n                                          10100    1.9497            —\n                                         101000    1.2490            —\n                                e 5504 ≈ 102390    1.0000            —\n                                         103000    0.9403            —\n\n\n\n2.5   The title’s claim, made precise\nThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2n assignments at every practical\nsize. Read against the theorem, that means the following four things, and nothing more.\n\n   1. What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time. The theorem says the machine halts\n      within C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) steps. It does not say how many steps the machine actually takes\n      on any input. No implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound above 2n\n      does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof promises nothing better than 2n there.\n\n   2. The constants are never computed. The theorem asserts that C, A, S, d, e exist. The proof\n      builds them from other existence lemmas and states no value, beyond the A ≥ 257 read\n      above. The machine’s number of states is likewise never computed.\n\n   3. With A = 1, the verified exponent is above n for every n from 50 up to about 102390 . That\n      is Table 2.1. Over that range the exponential part of the bound is at least 2n , and at its worst\n      it is 22.17 n . With the true A, whatever it is, the exponential part is 2 Aρ(n) , which is at least\n\n                                                    8\n\f                                           peak 2.17 at n 1023.7\n\n                                   2.0              verified exponent, (n)/n with (n) = n(ln ln(n+16))2/ ln(n+16)\n\n\n\nexponent relative to brute force\n                                   1.5\n                                               above 2n from n = 50         back below 2n only at n e5504 102390\n\n\n\n\n   (bound = 2 ratio n at A = 1)\n                                                                                                                    brute force: 2n","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"4dd7f887-11a9-423c-934b-0665e59d85ed","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:51:21.200Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GJYjCddcQJgMCm7fo24Pgc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"