lf calls ρ(n) “the weaker\nexponent” (p. 2, line 63), and its sharper exponents are not in the Lean library (Section 3). One\ndetail of bookkeeping: inside the solution the exponent is written with base-2 logarithms, and\na lemma shows the two forms differ only by a fixed multiplier, which can be folded into the\nconstant A (Appendix B.2); nothing above depends on it.\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                      10\n\f2.6     What the statement does establish\nFor each fixed width k ≥ 2, there is a deterministic machine that, given any k-CNF on n vari-\nables with m clauses in the fixed encoding, halts with the exact number of satisfying assign-\nments written in binary on a named tape, within C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) steps. Here L grows no faster\nthan a polynomial in n + m, and ρ(n) grows more slowly than n. A formula of width k on\nn variables cannot have more than about nk distinct clauses (2,604,376 at n = 125, k = 3; Ap-\npendix B.2), so the whole bound is subexponential in n alone. In the standard vocabulary, where\no (n) means a quantity that grows more slowly than n and poly a polynomial: a deterministic\n2o(n) · poly(n + m) algorithm for counting the solutions of k-SAT, and therefore for deciding it.\nThat refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis (Impagliazzo and Paturi, 2001), the conjecture that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in n, as stated. The verified theorem is the manuscript’s own\nverified form, the weaker exponent of its Proposition 6.4 (p. 43, lines 2474–2476), without the\nn ≥ 20 condition. We found no field, definition or quantifier through which the statement could\nbe accepted by the kernel and yet mean something weaker than this; the audit behind that sen-\ntence is reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md. Our own restatement of the\ntheorem as a named Lean sentence, over a verbatim copy of the machine model, is (Appendix A,\nA.2); it is never proved and never assumed there (Section 8).\n\n2.7     What this does not claim\n      • No running time of any machine. Nothing here measures or estimates how long the\n        verified machine, or any program, takes on any input. The title compares two guarantees.\n        Section 7 is the only place in the paper where anything is timed, and it times ordinary\n        solvers, not this machine.\n\n      • No statement about the proof. That the statement means what we say it means is a reading\n        of 220 lines. Whether the proof is sound is Section 4 and Section 5, which found no flaw.\n        The one proof-side fact quoted here, A = 256 + At , is a line of the solution read for the\n        value it assigns, not a claim about the argument.\n\n      • No value of the constants. A ≥ 257 is what the proof writes down, not what the machine\n        needs; a different proof of the same statement could have any A > 0. The statement itself\n        fixes nothing.\n\n      • Nothing about the manuscript’s sharper exponents, which are not in the Lean library and\n        are Section 3’s subject.\n\n      • No claim about sizes beyond e5504 . Past n ≈ 102390 the verified exponent is below n again\n        at A = 1; we say nothing about that range except that it is not a practical one.\n\n\n3      What the manuscript claims beyond the verified statement\nThe manuscript (OpenAI, 2026) is 67 pages long. The verified statement is 220 lines long. Sec-\ntion 2 read the statement. This section reads the manuscript’s claims one by one. For each claim\nit says one of three things: this is what the Lean theorem asserts; this follows from the Lean\ntheorem by an argument that exists only on paper; or this rests on a bound that the Lean library\ndoes not contain at all.\n\n                                                 11\n\f    The collection draws the line itself, in a short note on the family. We quote its two sentences\nin full (lean/docs/378.md, lines 9 and 11):\n        “For fixed k ≥ 2, Lean proves that a deterministic 