unts in a polynomial times 2Ok (ρ(n)) bit operations and polynomial\nspace, for n ≥ 20.\n\nWhich of the three is in the Lean. The verified exponent is the weaker one.            p The compara-\n                                                                                    2\ntor’s runtimeScale (lines 132–134 of the comparator file) is n(ln ln(n + 16)) / ln(n + 16), the\nsquared form. The solution’s own exponent, called rho (SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean,\nlines 10–11), is the same function with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma, runtimeScale_le_\nfour_rho (lines 190–191), bounds the comparator’s exponent by four times it for n ≥ 20.\nThe proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript’s Section 6.4, the three numbers\nthat set how the proof thins the formula before counting (its “sparsification”), to the letter: the\n                                                                     √\nmanuscript selects λn = ⌈log2 n⌉, νn = ⌈log2 λn ⌉ and s = ⌊ λn /νn2 ⌋, “the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying s2 νn4 ≤ λn ” (p. 43, lines 2480–2486; ⌈ x ⌉ is the smallest whole number at least x\nand ⌊ x ⌋ the largest at most x), and Asymptotics.lean lines 14–20 define sparsifyLambda,\nsparsifyNu and sparsifySchedule as exactly those three quantities. What Appendix D of\nthe manuscript provespis not in the library in any form we could find   p (Section 3.6). So the general\nexponent n log log n/ log n and the fixed-density exponent n/ log n are paper-only: proved\nat p. 57 (lines 3222–3246) and p. 54 (lines 3045–3064) respectively, by the contraction theorem of\nAppendix D.\n\nBoth bounds from one algorithm. The manuscript notes that a single algorithm attains both\npaper-only bounds, by switching between two methods according to the number of clauses (p. 57,\nlines 3247–3250). That algorithm is not the verified one either.\n\nThe three exponents, in numbers. Table 3.1 puts the three exponents side by side, each divided\nby n and each with its constant taken to be 1. A value above 1 means the exponent is above n,\nso the bound is worse than trying every\n                                      √ assignment. The verified ratio is the one of Table 2.1.\n                                                      √ 1: it peaks at 2/e = 0.736 at n ≈ 1,618\nThe general paper-only ratio, ln ln n/ ln n, never reaches\nand falls on both sides. The fixed-density ratio 1/ ln n falls from 0.578 at n = 20. So, at\nconstant 1, both paper-only guarantees sit below 2n at every size, and the verified one sits above\n2n from n = 50 to n ≈ 102390 . That is the whole content of the word “verified” in the title:\nthe manuscript’s exponents would make a different sentence, and they are not what the kernel\nchecked. Two cautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every exponent are","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"651baa58-0ef6-4120-97a5-bb1f8060f365","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:51:25.320Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BoQWrgoq5gjGkgizyyRkV4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter pass over paper 3 PDF"}
