{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MMYSoHjqXPsWviH3LtZTts","type":"tool_result","content":"checked. Two cautions apply to the table equally. The constants in front of every exponent are\nnot computed anywhere, in the manuscript or in the Lean. And the manuscript’s exponents\nare undefined below n = e and are stated for n ≥ 20, while the verified one is stated for all n.\nFigure 2.1 draws the same three curves.\n\nThe abstract’s form. The manuscript’s abstract states the running time as a polynomial times\n           1/2+o (1)\n2n/(log n)           (p. 1, lines 6–9). The small term o (1) in that exponent is a quantity that goes to zero\nas n grows. The verified exponent is of that form too, because the square of a double logarithm\ngrows more slowly than any fixed power of the logarithm (in symbols, (ln ln n)2 is (ln n)o(1) ), and\n\n\n                                                     13\n\fTable 3.1: The three exponents of the manuscript, each divided by n, with every constant taken to be\n1. A value above 1 means the bound     p is worse than trying every assignment at that n. Columns: the\nverified ρ(n)√/n = (ln ln(n + 16))2 / ln(n + 16) (comparator lines√ 132–134); Theorem 1.1’s general expo-\n nent ln ln n/ ln n (p. 2, line 59); its fixed-density exponent 1/ ln n (p. 2, line 62). Natural logarithms\n                                              2\n throughout (p. 1, lines 48–49). The row ee is where the general ratio peaks (2/e). Regenerate: python3\n-I paper3/scripts/beyond_claims.py.\n                                                                 √                    √\n                 n            verified ρ(n)/n    general ln ln n/ ln n      density 1/ ln n\n                 20                      0.861                      0.634             0.578\n                 50                      1.003                      0.690             0.506\n                 125                     1.150                      0.717             0.455\n                 103                     1.423                      0.735             0.380\n                   2\n                 ee ≈ 1,618              1.472                      0.736             0.368\n                 106                     1.855                      0.706             0.269\n                 1023.7                  2.165                      0.541             0.135\n\n\nthe manuscript says so at lines 64–66. We tag the abstract’s form verified, with the remark that\nits o (1) hides exactly the factor by which the three rows of Table 3.1 differ: at n = 106 that factor\nis 6.9 for the verified exponent, 2.6 for the general one and 1 for the fixed-density one.\n\n3.3   The corollaries\nThe corollaries speak of objects from the manuscript’s own fields: circuits, graphs, maps. We\ngloss each in a clause where it first appears and define none further; a reader who needs more\nshould go to the manuscript at the lines cited.\n\nFinding and sampling a satisfying assignment (p. 2, lines 68–71). “When #F > 0, the standard\nself-reduction finds a satisfying assignment by fixing one variable at a time and checking that the\nremaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two conditional counts instead\ngives an exactly uniform random satisfying assignment,” with polynomial expected overhead\nand a citation. Supported. The method calls the verified counter at most n times on formulas of\nthe same width, and the verified statement provides the exact count it needs. The method is two\nsentences of prose and is not in the library.\n\nCorollary 1.2, Max-k-SAT within εm (p. 2, lines 77–94). A randomised algorithm finds an\nassignment that satisfies at least OPT( F ) − εm clauses, that is, within a small fraction of the best\npossible, with high probability, in time\n\n                                         (n + m)Ok,ε (1) 2Ok,ε (ρ(n))\n\n(line 80). The corollary is stated with ρ(n) itself, the verified exponent. Its proof 