nt-locality                2974–2977                        same n variables, k = max{2, d} (lines 2984–2988)\nmaps, 2Od (ρ(n))\nAppendix D: Theorem D.1 (tensor                       p. 53,          paper-only       proofs at p. 54 (3045–3064) and p. 57 (3222–3250);\ncontraction), Lemma D.2, both proofs of               3001–3028                        D.4 uses base-2 logarithms (p. 57, lines 3251–3252)\nTheorem 1.1\n\n\n\n\n                                                                       19\n\f4     The proof, inspected\nSection 2 read the theorem. This section reads the proof, as far as reading can go. The question\nis not whether the mathematics is right. The kernel decides that, and Section 5 reports what\nthe kernel said when we ran it. The question is whether anything in the source could make the\nkernel’s acceptance mean less than it appears to mean. There are known ways for that to happen:\nan added axiom; a sorry, which is Lean’s word for a proof left out; a step proved by trusting a\ncompiled program rather than the kernel; a patched copy of the mathematics library; a statement\nin the solution that differs from the statement in the challenge; or a running-time bound proved\nagainst a cost model of the proof’s own choosing. We found none of these. What we did find is\nthat the repository offers no recorded evidence that the proof type-checks at all, that is, that Lean\naccepts it: no saved output of the axiom check, no automated build, and no second checker for\nthis family. That is why Section 5 exists. The last subsection joins the two.\n\n4.1   Method\nThe clone of openai/math at commit 4eaee875 is treated as untrusted data. Every file was\nread with cat, sed, grep and python3 -I, and nothing in it was built, imported or executed\nfor this section. Two scripts from the audit of 2026-11-05 do the mechanical part. closure.py\nfollows the import lines from the solution’s main module, strips comments, and counts how\noften each keyword of interest occurs in what remains. defdiff.py compares each defini-\ntion in the challenge file with the library’s definition of the same name, ignoring differences\nin spacing. Both were re-run for this section, and every number below is from that fresh run;\npaper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py re-runs them, performs the remaining checks, and\nregenerates Table 4.1. The inspection reads the spine of the proof, that is, the final file, the\ncompiler, the entry lemmas and the constants, and sweeps the whole closure by keyword. The\narithmetic beneath the spine was not read (Section 4.9).\n\n4.2   The closure\nThe closure of a module is the set of all files it depends on, directly or through other files. The com-\nparator’s JSON names one solution module, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main, a\nsixteen-line file that imports SqrtLog.ExecutionNativeFinal and SqrtLogSpace.Final\nand declares nothing (Main.lean). Its closure inside the repository is 3,091 modules, ev-\nery one in the family’s own directory, totalling 550,551 lines; the largest file is 2,873 lines\n(SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean). Outside the family,\nthe closure reaches 50 modules of Mathlib and one module of Lean’s own library, Lean.\nElab.Tactic.Omega, imported by SqrtLogSpace/FiniteFallback.lean (line 14) and\nSqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparseCharging.lean (line 5). That one module is a\nproof tactic, a tool for writing proofs; the closure defines no tactic of its own (Section 4.3). Noth-\ning is imported from any other family of the collection or from any of the repository’s other\ndependencies.\n    The family’s directory holds 3,977 .lean files, so 886 are outside the closure. They are\nnot reachable from the collection’s root module either: lean/OAI.lean imports the family\nonly at its line 49, and the build’s default target is that root (lean/lakefile.lean, line 89,\n@[default_target] lean_lib OAI). They are never built by the default target and bear on\n\n\n                                                  20\n\fnothing: dead files, not a hole. For completeness, a raw search of the 886 for sorry, axiom,\nnative_decide and admit finds nothing, with or without comments.\n\n4.3     The keyword sweep\nTable 4.1 gives the counts over the 3,091 files with comments stripped. The ones that matter are\nthe zeros, and each zero rules out one way of weakening a verified proof.\n\n      • No axiom, no sorry, no admit: nothing is assumed and no proof is left out.\n\n      • No native_decide and no ofReduceBool: nothing is proved by trusting a compiled\n        program’s answer instead of the kernel.\n\n      • No implemented_by, extern, unsafe or partial: no definition has a hidden imple-\n        mentation, and every recursion is one the kernel checks for termination.\n\n      • No elab, macro, syntax or simproc: no custom programming of Lean itself that could\n        rewrite a goal outside the kernel’s view.\n\n    The non-zero counts are ordinary. Nine declarations are opaque, a keyword that hides a\ndefinition’s body from the proof-writing layer; every one of the nine has a body, and the kernel\nchecks that body against its type, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 noncomputable declara-\ntions, the eight open Classical and the four explicit uses each of Classical.choice and\npropext are the classical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 instances and 40 local in-\nstances are standard bookkeeping declarations (“typeclass instances”) that tell Lean, for example,\nthat a type is finite or a property is decidable; they are harmless because such a declaration can\nonly make a false claim by way of an axiom, and there is none. The only options set in the closure\nswitch off a check on notation in two files and have no bearing on soundness. Appendix B.4 lists\nevery one of these with its file and line.\n\n4.4     The statement is the challenge’s\nThe solution does not import the challenge file. Instead the library declares again, under the same\nnames, every definition the statement uses, spread over seven files. So one has to check that the\ntwo copies agree. The definition diff compares 35 declarations: 33 are identical character for\ncharacter once spacing is ignored, and the remaining two, runtimeScale and runtimeBound,\ndiffer only in that the challenge marks each definition noncomputable by itself where the\nlibrary marks a whole section so (SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean, line 15), a difference the\nkernel does not see. The theorem statement itself, from its first word to the start of its proof, is\nidentical in the two files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at line 194 of the\nchallenge and line 74 of SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean; the challenge’s proof is the single word\nsorry at its line 218. Appendix B.3 names the seven files.\n    This textual match is a sanity check and not the guarantee. The comparator (Lean FRO, 2026)\ncompares the two statements after exporting them from Lean, with every definition the statement\nmentions matched by name and content, so a library definition that differed from the challenge’s\nunder the same name would make the comparator fail rather than pass silently. What the text\nconfirms is that there is no such difference to find, and that the shape Section 2 read is the shape\nthe library proves.\n\n                                                21\n\fTable 4.1: The inspection in numbers. Each row is one count over the 3,091 files of the closure, comments\nstripped where a keyword is counted; the script column says which script produced it. Regenerate:\npython3 -I paper3/scripts/proof_inspection.py.\n\n quantity                                                value               script\n modules in the closure of Main.lean                     3,091               closure.py\n raw lines in those modules                              550,551             closure.py\n external imports: Mathlib modules / core modules        50 / 1              closure.py\n .lean files under the family / outside the closure      3,977 / 886         this script\n axiom,       sorry,    admit,      native_decide,       0, 0, 0, 0, 0       closure.py\n ofReduceBool\n implemented_by, extern, unsafe, partial                 0, 0, 0, 0          closure.py\n elab, macro, syntax, simproc                            0, 0, 0, 0          closure.py\n set_option (both quotPrecheck false)                    2                   closure.py\n opaque (all with bodies)                                9 (9)               closure.py, this script\n noncomputable                                           1,048               closure.py\n explicit Classical.choice / propext / open              4/4/8               closure.py\n Classical\n instance / local instance                               148 / 40            closure.py\n termination_by / decreasing_by                          9/5                 closure.py\n statement definitions compared / identical / identi-    35 / 33 / 2         defdiff.py, this script\n cal up to noncomputable\n theorem statement, characters (normalised / raw),       1,124 / 1,353       this script\n identical\n #print axioms commands: closure files / lines;          8 / 19; 17 / 35     this script\n family files / lines\n recorded depends on axioms outputs in the clone         0                   this script\n challenge JSONs: total / enable_nanoda false /          418 / 413 / 2 / 3   this script\n true / absent\n CI workflow files (lock-prs.yml only)                   1                   this script\n manifest packages / patch files / patches for Mathlib   42 / 23 / 0         this script\n\n\n4.5   The spine of the proof\nWe followed the proof from its last file down to the lemmas that connect it to the machine of the\nstatement. Appendix B.3 gives every name and line; here is what the spine shows.\n\nThe machine is a concrete table. The theorem’s machine is compiled from a program over 80\none-bit registers and 1,220 Boolean stacks, with twelve kinds of instruction (BitProgram.lean,\nlines 10–26). The compiler (SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean, line 131) builds the\nmachine’s transition table case by case from the program and then renames the states to numbers\nby an appeal to a choice principle that names no particular numbering. The theorem only says\nsuch a machine exists, so this non-constructive step (one that proves something exists without\nproducing it) costs it nothing; it is also the reason no running time can be read off the statement\n(Section 2).\n\nThe constants, as far as the proof pins them. For inputs above a cutoff, the main run supplies\na trace within the bound with 1 ≤ C and 1 ≤ A (SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean,\n\n\n                                                   22\n\fline 12). Its exponent constant is 256 + At (line 51), where At is at least 1 and comes from\na contract on the leaf program (SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean, line 793). The\ncutoff itself is max 20 (max Nleaf Ns) (line 50), with Ns obtained from a statement that\nsomething holds for all large enough n, which yields no particular number, and never computed.\nBelow the cutoff, a finite scanner gives time ≤ 28700 ( L + 1)2 (SqrtLogSpace/NativeEntry.\nlean, line 92). The two branches are folded together, and the final theorem’s constants are\nthose of the two branches added up: C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2\n(SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, line 41). So the proof as written has an exponent constant of at\nleast 258 in front of ρ(n), a polynomial degree that depends on k, and a leading constant of at\nleast 28,701. None of these is in the statement, which asserts only that they are positive, and none\nof the underlying quantities is computed anywhere. This confirms, from the proof side, what\nSection 2 said from the statement side: the constants are unknown, and the smallest they could\nbe is already far from 1.\n\nWhere a hidden cost model would live, and why it cannot. A proof of a running-time bound\nfor a machine that is compiled from a higher-level program could, in principle, prove the bound\nagainst a cost function of its own and leave the kernel to check only that function’s arithmetic.\nThat is not what happens here. The lemmas that connect the program to the machine conclude\nin the challenge’s own words: a source run of n steps becomes a run of the compiled machine of\n7n steps, with the overhead 7 proved as a theorem (SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean,\nlines 152–162), and the space lemma (SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean, lines 125–143)\nproduces a trace of the challenge’s own step function with its visited space bounded on every\nprefix. The time in the final theorem is literally the length of a trace of the challenge’s step\nfunction (its line 201). No cost function of the compiler’s appears in the statement; a wrong cost\nmodel would make these lemmas unprovable, not the theorem false. This is the structural point\non which the rest of the paper leans: whatever the proof’s internal accounting, the number the\ntheorem bounds is the number of steps of the comparator’s machine.\n\nCorrectness of the count. The outer lemma (SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuter.lean, lines 50–\n51) yields the count word and the answer bit from the canonical search, and a transport lemma\n(line 73) carries both to the machine’s tapes. These are theorems about the concrete step function.\nThe arithmetic that makes them true, the sparsification, the ring and moment computations, the\nsearch, is the content of the other three thousand files, and the only claim made about it here is\nthe one the kernel makes: Lean accepts it (Section 5).\n\n4.6   Toolchain, Mathlib, and the build hooks\nThe Lean version is fixed (lean/lean-toolchain: leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1; lean/\nlakefile.lean line 8: fixedToolchain := true). The manifest (the list of the pack-\nages the build depends on, each at an exact version) lists 42 packages; Mathlib is pinned\nto upstream revision d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612 at leanprover-\ncommunity/mathlib4, and the audit of 2026-11-05 confirmed that revision on GitHub as the\nupstream commit that moves to v4.34.1. The repository carries 23 compatibility patches under\nlean/patches/, none for Mathlib; every one is for a third-party package that this closure does\nnot import. The build file has two hooks, pieces of code that run automatically when the build\n\n\n                                                23\n\ftool configures or updates the package: one clones and patches eleven dependencies, the other\nverifies or applies the patches for the rest (Appendix B.4). They touch only the dependency di-\nrectory, and only non-Mathlib packages. They are one reason the inspection did not build, and\nthe reason the replay of Section 5 runs inside a sandbox with the network on.\n\n4.7     What the repository does not record\nThree things one would expect beside a verified refutation of a twenty-five-year-old hypothesis\nare absent. Their absence is a fact about evidence, not about the proof.\n\n      • No saved axiom check. SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean ends with five #print axioms\n        commands (lines 197–201), and seven other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nine-\n        teen commands in eight files; the family’s directory holds seventeen such files, but nine of\n        them are among the 886 never built). Their output exists only while the build runs and is\n        recorded nowhere: a search of the whole clone for the string depends on axioms finds\n        nothing.\n\n      • No automated build. .github/workflows/ holds one file, lock-prs.yml, which locks\n        pull-request conversations. Nothing builds or checks the Lean.\n\n      • No second checker for this family.                  The comparator’s JSON (lean/\n        ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json) names the one theorem,\n        an empty definition_names list, the three permitted axioms propext, Quot.sound,\n        Classical.choice, and enable_nanoda: false.                   Nanoda is an independent\n        checker written in another language (Section 5); with it off, the collection’s own check of\n        this family rests on one implementation of the kernel. Of the collection’s 418 challenge\n        JSONs, 413 set enable_nanoda to false, two to true, and three omit the key. The\n        comparator’s README describes the check as: build challenge and solution in a sandbox,\n        export both, match each named theorem’s statement with every declaration it uses, confine\n        the proof to the permitted axioms, and have the Lean kernel accept it; with nanoda on,\n        the exported environment is replayed through the independent checker as well (ammkrn,\n        2026). Section 9 records the versions.\n\n    Two smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript’s source is dated “17 Octo-\nber 2026, revised 25 October 2026” (preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-\nFalse-October-25-2026/build/main_full_version.tex, line 25); the collection’s his-\ntory file mentions the family once, in the sentence that names the working group at whose\nrequest the materials were released (history.md, line 18), and its only “Formalization Fix” en-\ntry (line 7) concerns another\n                           pfamily. And the scope note lean/docs/378.md states the verified\nexponent as n(log log n)2 / log n (line 9), which is what the library proves and what Section 3\ncompares with the manuscript’s.\n\n4.8     The trust boundary, joined with the replay\nBefore anything was run, the proof’s trust rested on five things: (i) OpenAI’s unpublished build,\nsince nothing showed the closure type-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output; (iii)\nthe Lean kernel of v4.34.1 and Mathlib d13f23b7 being what upstream publishes; (iv) the\n\n\n                                                  24\n\fTable 4.2: The trust boundary before and after the replay of Section 5. “Moved” means the item no longer\nhas to be taken on trust; “not moved” means it still does.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"9e8763d1-70d8-4012-90bd-4f4deccf34ba","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:51:28.879Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EMXpijkzdx7mdGQDDdFJEP","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter pass over paper 3 PDF"}
