{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EM8P2BUGMea5XijJo2GZ6v","type":"tool_result","content":"has to be taken on trust; “not moved” means it still does.\n\n item                               status after the replay\n (i) the build                      moved: the 3,091-module closure built from source in our sandbox\n                                    at the pinned toolchain, exit 0, no error, no sorry.\n (ii) the axiom output              moved: #print axioms of the comparator’s theorem printed\n                                    [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound], and the nine-\n                                    teen in-build axiom lines say the same; the output is on record.\n (iii) kernel and Mathlib           moved in part: Mathlib was fetched from upstream at the pinned\n                                    revision and not patched, so “as upstream” is now observed rather\n                                    than assumed; the Lean kernel of v4.34.1 is still trusted as up-\n                                    stream, now beside a second checker (v).\n (iv) the comparator run            not moved: the statement-level match through lean4export was\n                                    not performed; the match this paper relies on is the textual one of\n                                    Section 4.4.\n (v) a second checker               moved: nanoda_lib checked the exported dependencies of the\n                                    theorem, 95,176 declarations, with no errors and the three axioms;\n                                    what this adds and what it does not is stated in Section 5.\n\n\ncomparator run, not reproduced; (v) no second checker for this family. Table 4.2 says which of\nthe five Section 5 moved.\n    After the replay, then, the proof’s trust rests on four things: the Lean kernel of v4.34.1 and\nnanoda agreeing on the exported dependencies of the theorem; the export tool lean4export\nwriting out what the built environment (the set of all checked declarations) holds; upstream\nMathlib at the pinned revision; and the textual, not export-level, match between the solution’s\nstatement and the challenge’s. Everything the inspection could find by reading, the build and\nthe two checkers confirmed. Nothing the inspection could not settle by reading has been settled\nby anything else.\n\n4.9     What this does not claim\n      • The arithmetic was not read. The inspection followed the spine from SqrtLogSpace/\n        Final.lean down to the compile lemmas and swept the whole closure by keyword; the\n        content of the other three thousand files was not read. The argument above is that its\n        correctness is enforced by the kernel, not that we verified it.\n\n      • A text search is necessary, not sufficient. Zero sorry and zero axiom by text search\n        cannot show that a file type-checks; only the build does (Section 5). And a keyword count\n        is over source text at one commit; a proof can be weakened only through the mechanisms\n        searched for, and we searched for the ones we know.\n\n      • The textual statement match is not the comparator’s. The comparator matches exported\n        constants; we matched source text after normalising spacing and found it identical. A\n        difference the normalisation hides would have to be invisible to the kernel as well, but that\n        is an argument, not a run.\n\n      • Nothing about the constants’ values. The bounds A ≥ 258 and C ≥ 28,701 are what the\n\n\n                                                    25\n\f        proof’s witnesses are built from; they are lower bounds on what this proof exhibits, not\n        statements about any machine’s running time, and a different proof of the same statement\n        could exhibit different ones.\n\n      • Nothing about the Lean kernel, Mathlib or the toolchain. All three are trusted as up-\n        stream at the pinned versions; the second checker of Section 5 is evidence about the first,\n        not a proof of either.\n\n      • Nothing about what the theorem says. That is Section 2; a sound proof of a weak bound\n        is a sound proof of a weak bound.\n\n\n5      Independent replay\nThe repository holds no saved output of the axiom check for the family, no automated build of\nits Lean, and enable_nanoda: false in the comparator’s settings (Section 4). We therefore\nbuilt the proof ourselves, in isolation, at the Lean version the repository pins, and recorded what\nthe kernel reports. This section says exactly what was run, where, and what came out. The\nscripts are quoted verbatim in paper3/replay/README.md, and the logs are under paper3/\nreplay/logs/.\n\n5.1     What was run, and where\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit 4eaee875) was built inside a sandbox on the project’s ma-\nchine. The sandbox tool is bwrap (bubblewrap). Inside it, the system directories are read-only;\nthe home directory is a private, empty one (a tmpfs), so nothing of the real home or of this\nrepository is visible; processes, inter-process communication and host names are kept apart from\nthe host’s (separate “namespaces”); the whole sandbox is confined to eight cores (taskset -c\n0-7) at the lowest scheduling priority; and the network is on, because the repository’s build file\n(its lakefile) clones its 41 dependencies at build time and the compiled Mathlib comes from a\ndownload cache. Inside the sandbox, the Lean installer elan was installed into the private home\nand fetched the pinned Lean version from the repository’s own lean-toolchain file. Then,\nin the clone’s lean/ directory, three commands ran: lake exe cache get, which downloads\nthe compiled Mathlib; lake build OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main, which\nbuilds the solution’s 3,091 files and not the collection’s default target, which would build all 721\nfamilies; and #print axioms of the one theorem the comparator names, OAI.Algorithms.\nSubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat, through lake env lean.\nOnly text left the sandbox; nothing built inside it was executed outside it.\n     Two runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed at once at the build step\nbecause its command line carried -j 8, an option this version of the build tool does not have\n(the whole of its build log is the one line error: unknown short option ’-j’); nothing\nof the family was compiled. The second, started at 01:16 with that option removed and the core\ncap moved to taskset, is the run reported below. Both scripts, both versions, and the launch\ncommand are in the README; the first run’s logs are kept beside the second’s.\n\n\n\n\n                                                 26\n\f5.2   What came out\nThe build finished with exit code 0 in 33 minutes of wall clock on eight cores (01:16:25 to 01:49:31).\nIt compiled 3,091 modules with the prefix OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT: exactly the\nset the inspection of Section 4 enumerated, the same 3,091 names with nothing missing on either\nside (paper3/replay/counts.sh). The build tool’s own job total, 12014, counts the cached\nMathlib and dependency modules as well, none of which was compiled. The log has no line\ncontaining error and none containing sorry. Its eleven warning lines are the build tool noting\nthat the repository’s compatibility patches leave eleven dependencies with “local changes”, and\nnone of the eleven is Mathlib.\n    Eight source files of the closure carry nineteen #print axioms commands (seventeen files\nof the family’s directory do, but nine of those are never built), so the build log records nineteen\naxiom lines as it goes. Every one reads [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound], the\nthree standard axioms. Among them, SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 197–201, prints the\naxioms of compiled_exact_count_and_sat, exact_count_and_sat, exact_count_eth,\nsat_polynomial_space and original_sat_main. The separate #print axioms run after\nthe build gives, for the comparator’s theorem,\n      ’OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat’\n      depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]\n\n(paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms.txt).                      The     versions    are   Lean    4.34.1\n(the       log       line      reads        Lean (version 4.34.1, commit 5045d005...,\nRelease)),          Lake      5.0.0-src+5045d00,             and       Mathlib      at     revision\nd13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612 from the clone’s own manifest, with\nits compiled files from the Mathlib cache (8,908 files). The replay copy is at commit 4eaee875,\nthe same as the pristine clone, and the comparator file and the solution’s files have the same\nhashes in both (paper3/replay/logs/run2/hashes.txt; the comparator statement’s\nSHA-256 begins 4183b205). The full build log is kept out of the repository by size; its SHA-256\n(d313f594...) and its last 200 lines are committed.\n    So the sentence every later section relies on reads: replayed. The solution builds from source\nat the pinned Lean version with unpatched Mathlib, and the kernel reports the three standard\naxioms and nothing else for the theorem the comparator names. This moves the first three items\nof the inspection’s trust boundary (Section 4): the build is no longer OpenAI’s unpublished one,\nthe axiom output is now on record, and the Mathlib revision was fetched from upstream and not\npatched.\n\n5.3   The second checker\nThe comparator’s settings for the family have enable_nanoda: false: OpenAI’s check of\nthis family ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a second checker, in the same sandbox, after the\nbuild (paper3/replay/nanoda.sh, recorded in the README before it ran). Two tools are\ninvolved. lean4export (tag v4.34.0, commit 076e8e57, built with the pinned v4.34.1\nLean so that it reads the compiled files the build produced) wrote out exact_count_and_sat\nand everything it depends on from the built environment, as a plain-text file: 2,355,666,449 bytes,\n43,633,742 lines, format version 3.1.0, in 68 seconds. Then nanoda_lib (ammkrn, 2026) (version\n0.4.31, commit 8de7e0c4, 2026-10-29), a checker for Lean 4 proofs written in Rust that shares no\n\n\n                                                 27\n\fcode with the Lean kernel, read that file and re-checked every declaration in it, with propext,\nClassical.choice and Quot.sound as the permitted axioms and the Nat and String literal\nextensions on. Its output (paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda_check.txt):\n        Checked 95176 declarations with no errors\nin 110 seconds on eight cores, exit code 0, after printing back the theorem’s statement and the\nthree axioms from its own reading of the file. The export contains exactly three axiom dec-\nlarations (grep -c ’\"axiom\"’ on the file), so nothing else could have been permitted. The\nexport file (SHA-256 bdbc9afe..., paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/export_sha256.txt)\nstays in the sandbox directory by size; the configuration, the build logs and the checker’s output\nare committed.\n\n5.4     What this does not claim\n      • The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned version (v4.34.1) and upstream Math-\n        lib at the pinned revision. It moves the proof’s trust from “OpenAI’s unpublished build”\n        to “a build we ran”, not from the Lean kernel to anything else. The second checker re-\n        moves the Lean kernel from the list for the theorem’s dependencies as exported, but adds\n        lean4export (that it writes out what the environment holds) and nanoda_lib (that it\n        checks what it reads) to it. Two independent checkers agreeing is evidence, not a proof that\n        either is correct.\n\n      • It is not the comparator run. The comparator checks that the solution’s exported statement\n        matches the challenge’s; we did not run that tool chain, and the statement-level match the\n        paper relies on is the textual one of Section 4 (the 35 definitions and the theorem statement,\n        identical after normalising spacing, two of them up to the noncomputable keyword).\n\n      • The counts above are counts of lines in one build’s log on one machine with the named\n        versions; a different build tool would print different job totals (the 12,014 includes the\n        cached Mathlib modules), and a different machine a different time.\n\n      • Nothing here bears on what the theorem says (Section 2) or on the manuscript’s unverified\n        exponents (Section 3); a replayed proof of a weak bound is a replayed proof of a weak\n        bound.\n\n\n6      What follows, in general terms\nSections 2–5 read the verified statement, drew the line between it and the manuscript, inspected\nthe proof and replayed it. This section asks what follows from the verified statement alone. Two\nthings: which of the standard hardness hypotheses it refutes, and by what step; and what a lower\nbound that was derived from one of those hypotheses has lost. The manuscript answers both\nquestions itself, in its §1.1.1–§1.1.2 (p. 4–5) and §1.1.3 (p. 5), and we quote it. What we add is\nthe exact logical form of each hypothesis, the one line of the comparator statement that denies it,\nand the distinction, stated in general terms, between a theorem whose premise has become false\nand a statement that has been refuted. Nothing here names a problem the manuscript’s §1.1.1\nand §1.1.3 do not name, and nothing here is about any problem’s complexity beyond what those\ntwo sections state.\n\n                                                   28\n\f    A hypothesis, in this section, is a conjecture about how hard satisfiability is. The Exponential\nTime Hypothesis, ETH, says that 3-SAT needs time exponential in the number of variables. Its\nstrong form, SETH, says that as the clause width k grows, no algorithm for k-SAT does much\nbetter than trying all 2n assignments. Several variants exist, and the manuscript lists them. A\nlower bound derived from a hypothesis is a theorem of the form “if the hypothesis holds, then\nproblem X has no fast algorithm”; hundreds of such theorems exist in the literature.\n\n6.1   The one property every refutation uses\nEvery refutation below rests on the same parts of the verified theorem (comparator file lines 194–\n217, Section 2). For each fixed width k ≥ 2 there is one deterministic machine which, given any\nformula of width at most k on n variables, halts with the number of satisfying assignments on\nits count tape (lines 203–204), the answer “is that number positive” on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205–207), within the bound C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) (lines 210–211). Four features of\nthat property do the work.\n\n  1. It is deterministic. The step rule is a function; there is no random choice and no guess.\n     A deterministic algorithm is also a randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err,\n     and also a nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each refuta-\n     tion of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a refutation of its randomised and\n     nondeterministic forms by the same machine.\n\n  2. The bound is subexponential in n alone. A formula of width k on n variables has at most\n     about nk distinct clauses (2,604,376 at n = 125, k = 3), so the input length L is at most a\n     polynomial in n, and the whole bound is a polynomial in n times 2 Aρ(n) . Since ρ(n)/n goes\n     to zero (Appendix A, A.6), for every rate δ > 0 there is a size beyond which the bound is\n     below 2δn ; below that size the bound is a constant depending on k and δ. In the standard\n     notation, the running time is Ok,δ (2δn ) for every δ > 0 (Appendix B.5). This is a fact about\n     the limit, and the hypotheses are statements about the limit. It coexists with Section 2’s\n     observation that ρ(n) > n on every 50 ≤ n < e5504 , which is about where the limit begins.\n\n  3. One machine per k, for every n. The machine and the constants are chosen after k and\n     before n (Section 2); there is no n ≥ 20 condition in the comparator statement, though the\n     manuscript’s Theorem 1.1 carries one (p. 2, lines 56–57). So the verified form is the strong\n     form of the denial, one algorithm for every rate at once: a single algorithm that runs within\n     2δn times a polynomial for every δ at once, where the denial of a hypothesis of the form\n     “for every δ there is an algorithm” would have allowed a different algorithm for each δ.\n\n  4. The machine model is the conservative one. The comparator’s machine is a multitape\n     Turing machine that moves one head one cell or writes one symbol per step. Every step of\n     such a machine is a bounded amount of work on any of the standard models of computation\n     (random-access machines, other kinds of Turing machine) in which the hypotheses are\n     stated, so the bound transfers to those models with at most a constant factor per step,\n     which the polynomial absorbs. The input is the manuscript’s own encoding, of length\n     polynomial in n + m; converting from any other reasonable encoding is polynomial too.\n\n\n\n\n                                                 29\n\f6.2   The hypotheses, one by one\nThe manuscript’s own sentence is (p. 4, lines 187–191):\n      “Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing whether the ex-\n      act count is positive or zero decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability in determinis-\n      tic 2o(n) (n + m)Ok (1) time for every fixed k; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH\n      promise problem. The deterministic algorithm also refutes the bounded-error randomized\n      versions of ETH [DHM+ 14, Section 1] and SETH [CDL+ 16, Definition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH.”\n\nand, for counting (p. 5, lines 211–212): “Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves #3-SAT determinis-\ntically in 2o(n) poly(n, m) time and therefore refutes #ETH.” We take each hypothesis in the form\nthe manuscript states it, with the manuscript’s own source for that form, and say which part of\nthe verified theorem denies it. Table 6.1 is the summary, and Appendix B.5 gives each hypothesis\nand its denial with the quantifiers written out. In every case the only thing used is the property\nof Section 6.1; nothing is used from the manuscript’s sharper exponents, which are not verified\n(Section 3).\n\nETH. As stated (p. 4, lines 165–166): “The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) asserted that\n3-SAT requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time in the worst case, and hence so does k-SAT for every fixed\nk ≥ 3 [IP01, Theorem 1],” citing Impagliazzo and Paturi (2001). In words: there is some fixed\nrate δ > 0 such that no deterministic algorithm decides 3-SAT within 2δn times a polynomial, on\nall inputs. Its denial: for every rate δ > 0 there is such an algorithm. The verified statement\nat k = 3 supplies, for every δ, the same machine, whose answer bit is the decision, within that\ntime (item 2 above). This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis whether\n“requires 2Ω(n) time” is read as a lower bound at infinitely many sizes or at all large sizes. In the\nvocabulary of Impagliazzo and Paturi (2001), where sk is the smallest rate δ such that k-SAT has\nan algorithm within 2δn and ETH is the statement that s3 > 0: the verified statement gives sk = 0\nfor every k ≥ 2, by one algorithm per k.\n\nSETH. As stated (p. 4, lines 166–169): “The Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) made\nthe sharper prediction that k-SAT requires 2n(1−ok (1)) time as k grows. Precisely, for every ε ∈\n(0, 1), there is a fixed k for which no deterministic algorithm solves k-SAT in 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1)\ntime,” with the motivation attributed to Impagliazzo and Paturi (2001)’s open problems (lines\n169–171). In words: however small a saving ε one asks for over trying all 2n assignments, some\nclause width k makes that saving impossible. Its denial: there is a saving ε that every fixed width\nk allows. The verified statement gives more: for every saving ε and every k ≥ 2, the machine for\nk runs within 2(1−ε)n times a polynomial for all large n, because ρ(n) grows more slowly than\nn; below that size the exponential factor is a constant. The polynomial’s degree depends on k,\nand that is exactly what the hypothesis’s “for a fixed k” permits. The refutation does not pass\nthrough ETH; it reads the statement at each k. It is also standard that SETH implies ETH, through\nthe Sparsification Lemma of Impagliazzo et al. (2001), a tool that trades one formula with many\nclauses for a few formulas with few clauses (Appendix B.5); so the refutation of ETH alone would\nalready refute SETH. We note the implication for the record; the direct reading above is what the\npaper rests on.\n\n\n\n\n                                                    30\n\fGap-ETH, and its randomised form. As stated (p. 4, lines 172–174): “The Gap Exponential\nTime Hypothesis (Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants δ, γ > 0 for which no\nO(2γn )-time algorithm can distinguish a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment\nviolates at least a δ fraction of the clauses, even when m = O(n) [Din16, MR17],” citing Dinur\n(2016) and Manurangsi and Raghavendra (2017); and (lines 175–176) “The randomized version\nrules out an algorithm with the same running time that distinguishes the two cases with proba-\nbility at least 2/3 on every promised input [CCK+ 20, Conjecture 2.5],” citing Chalermsook et al.\n(2020). In words: it should be hard even to tell a satisfiable formula from one that is far from\nsatisfiable. Its denial: for every rate γ there is an algorithm within O(2γn ) that tells the two\napart, already when the number of clauses is proportional to n. Exact decision tells them apart,\nsince a formula in which every assignment violates some clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified\nmachine at k = 3 decides within O(2γn ) for every γ, at every number of clauses (item 2). The\npromise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every input, promised or not, with","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"e68274b4-8c9f-48c0-a298-0aa2c04a9cf2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:51:30.595Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EM8P2BUGMea5XijJo2GZ6v","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter pass over paper 3 PDF"}
