{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WXi6JjrrAwezRN958qXGt6","type":"tool_result","content":"promise is never used: the machine answers correctly on every input, promised or not, with\nprobability 1, which refutes the randomised form by item 1.\n\nNSETH. As stated (p. 4, lines 181–183): “The Nondeterministic Strong Exponential Time Hy-\npothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every ε > 0, some fixed k admits no nondeterministic 2(1−ε)n\n(n + m)O(1) -time algorithm for deciding whether a k-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether\na k-DNF is a tautology [CGI+ 16],” citing Carmosino et al. (2016). (A k-DNF is the mirror image\nof a k-CNF, an “or” of small “and”s, and a tautology is a formula true under every assignment.)\nIn words: even an algorithm allowed to guess should not be able to certify unsatisfiability with\na saving over 2n . Its denial: there is a saving that every fixed k allows, for a guessing algorithm.\nThe verified machine’s answer bit is false exactly when the count is zero (lines 205–207), so it\ndecides unsatisfiability deterministically within the SETH bound of the previous paragraph for\nevery ε and every k; and a deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never guesses, so\nevery convention for nondeterministic time is met. The manuscript’s “decides both satisfiability\nand unsatisfiability” (line 188) is this.\n\n#ETH. As stated (p. 4, lines 204–205): “The counting Exponential Time Hypothesis (#ETH)\nasserted that exactly counting the satisfying assignments of a 3-CNF requires 2Ω(n) deterministic\ntime [DHM+ 14, Section 1],” citing Dell et al. (2014). Its denial: for every rate δ > 0 there is\na deterministic algorithm within O(2δn ) that outputs the exact count. The verified machine at\nk = 3 halts with the count on its count tape, a binary number of at most n + 1 bits (lines 203–204,\n208–209), within the same bound. This is the one hypothesis for which the yes-or-no bit is not\nenough and the count tape is what refutes it. It is also the hypothesis the comparator statement\nwas written for: the count is the theorem’s first conclusion and the decision its corollary.\n\nThe bounded-error randomised ETH and SETH. The manuscript names them by citation only\n(p. 4, lines 190–191): “[DHM+ 14, Section 1]” for ETH (Dell et al., 2014) and “[CDL+ 16, Definition\n2.1]” for SETH (Cygan et al., 2016). In each, the algorithm may toss coins and must be right with\nprobability clearly above one half on every input. A deterministic algorithm is such an algorithm\nthat is right with probability 1 (item 1), so the refutations of ETH and SETH above refute these\nforms by the same machine, with no change to the bound. We have not re-read the two sources’\ndefinitions; we state the forms as the manuscript cites them, and a reader who needs the exact\nerror threshold or the exact model of the source should go to the cited section and definition.\n\n\n                                                 31\n\fTable 6.1: The hypotheses the manuscript’s §1.1.1–§1.1.2 list, each in the form the manuscript states\nit (page of paper.pdf, lines of the committed extract), with the manuscript’s own source for that\nform, the logical form in symbols, and the part of the verified theorem that denies it (lines 194–217\nof the comparator file at 4eaee875). A, Ck , dk are the never-computed constants of Section 2. Gen-\nerated, with every page and fragment checked against the extract and the clone, by python3 -I\npaper3/scripts/consequences.py.\n\nhypothesis   stated      source           form                                    negated by\nETH          p. 4,       IP01, Theorem    ∃ δ > 0: no deterministic 2δn poly      the answer bit at k = 3: deterministic,\n             165–166     1                algorithm decides 3-SAT                 2 Aρ(n) poly, ρ(n) = o (n)\nSETH         p. 4,       IP01, Open       ∀ ε ∈ (0, 1) ∃ k: no deterministic      the answer bit at every k: for each k,\n             166–169     Problems, item   2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) algorithm for       Ak ρ(n) ≤ (1 − ε)n for all large n\n                         2                k-SAT\nGap-ETH      p. 4,       Din16, MR17      ∃ δ, γ > 0: no O(2γn ) algorithm        exact decision separates the two cases;\n             172–174                      separates satisfiable 3-CNFs from       at m = O(n) the bound is\n                                          δ-far ones, even at m = O(n)            2 Aρ(n) poly(n) = O(2γn ) for every γ\nrandomised   p. 4,       CCK+20,          as Gap-ETH, for algorithms correct      a deterministic algorithm is correct\nGap-ETH      175–176     Conjecture 2.5   with probability ≥ 2/3 on every         with probability 1\n                                          promised input\nNSETH        p. 4,       CGI+16           ∀ ε > 0 ∃ k: no nondeterministic        the answer bit is false iff\n             181–183                      2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) algorithm decides   count F = 0; a deterministic machine\n                                          k-UNSAT                                 is a nondeterministic one with no\n                                                                                  guess\nrandomised   p. 4, 190   DHM+14,          ETH for bounded-error randomised        a deterministic algorithm is a\nETH                      Section 1        algorithms                              bounded-error randomised one with\n                                                                                  error 0\nrandomised   p. 4,       CDL+16,          SETH for bounded-error randomised       as above\nSETH         190–191     Definition 2.1   algorithms\n#ETH         p. 4,       DHM+14,          ∃ δ > 0: no deterministic 2δn           the count tape at k = 3 holds\n             204–205     Section 1        algorithm counts the satisfying         countWord (count F ) within the same\n                                          assignments of a 3-CNF                  bound\n\n\n\n\n                                                      32\n\fWhat the manuscript places beside the list. Two sentences follow the refutation paragraph\n(p. 4, lines 191–194): the result “does not conflict with unconditional exponential lower bounds\nfor restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13],” and “In 2016, Ryan Williams\nrefuted the Merlin–Arthur version of SETH (MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies k-CNF\nunsatisfiability in 2n/2 poly(n, m) time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3]” (Williams, 2016). Both are context.\nThe first says that a lower bound proved outright for one restricted way of writing proofs of\nunsatisfiability (“regular resolution” is one such way) is not a statement about algorithms in\ngeneral and is not touched. The second says that one member of the family, the version in\nwhich the proof of unsatisfiability may be checked with the help of randomness (a Merlin–Arthur\nprotocol), was already false before this result. Neither is used below.\n\n6.3   What a derived lower bound loses\nThe manuscript’s §1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were proved from ETH or from\nSETH (p. 5, lines 213–225), for problems with exponential algorithms and for problems with\npolynomial ones, and then says what its result does to them (lines 226–227):\n      “Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness claims above. Some of\n      the same lower bounds follow from weaker hypotheses that may still hold.”\n\nWe say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the two standard shapes,\n\n                     (E)   ETH ⇒ no 2o(n) algorithm for X,\n                     (S)   SETH ⇒ no (c − ε)k algorithm for Y for any ε > 0,\n\nwhere n and k are whatever measure of the input the theorem names and c is its constant. In\nwords: (E) says that if ETH holds, problem X has no subexponential algorithm; (S) says that if\nSETH holds, no algorithm for problem Y beats the base c. Nothing below depends on what X\nand Y are.\n\nThe theorem stands; its premise is false; it derives nothing. A theorem of shape (E) or (S) is\nan “if . . . then”. It is proved by a reduction: a recipe that turns a fast algorithm for X or Y into a\nfast algorithm for k-SAT. The recipe is unaffected by anything in the verified statement; the “if . . .\nthen” remains a theorem. What has changed is that its “if” is now known to be false (Section 6.2),\nso the theorem can no longer be applied: it derives nothing about X or Y. That is the whole of\nwhat “removes one basis” means.\n\nThe conclusion is not refuted. A false “if” does not make the “then” false. The statement “no\n2o(n) algorithm for X” and the statement “no (c − ε)k algorithm for Y” are untouched by anything\nin the comparator statement, which speaks about k-CNF formulas and nothing else. To refute\neither, one would need an algorithm for X or Y, and the verified statement supplies one only\nthrough a reduction in the opposite direction to the one the lower bound used. The manuscript\nsays exactly this (p. 5, lines 237–238): “To obtain a faster algorithm for a target problem from our\nSAT algorithm, we need a reduction in the other direction, from that problem to fixed-width SAT,\nthat controls the number of variables.” And it places the general caveat on itself at p. 2, lines\n95–99:\n\n\n                                                  33\n\f     “Note that runtime bounds of the form 2o(n) do not transfer under general polynomial-time\n                    o (1)\n     reductions (2n       would be needed for this), so our result does not imply that general NP\n     problems admit subexponential time algorithms.”\n\nSo for shape (E): a subexponential algorithm for X follows only from a reduction of X to fixed-\nwidth SAT in which the number of SAT variables is proportional to X’s own size measure, and\nthe manuscript gives one such transfer (below). For shape (S): an algorithm for Y with base below\nc would need the SAT instance to be solved with an exponent a fixed fraction below k log2 c; the\nverified exponent is A times ρ of the SAT instance’s variable count, A is unknown with no value\nbelow 257 on record, and nothing of the kind follows (Appendix B.5). No statement of shape\n“no (c − ε)k algorithm” is refuted by anything in this paper or, so far as we can read, in the\nmanuscript.\n\nHardness as a classification. A statement of the form “Y is SETH-hard” (or ETH-hard) records\nthat a reduction exists from k-SAT to Y with the stated control of the size measure. The reduction\nstill exists, and the statement is still true as a statement about reductions. What it used to convey,\nthat a fast algorithm for Y is unlikely because it would refute SETH, it no longer conveys: a fast\nalgorithm for Y would refute SETH, and SETH is refuted. The classification is now a fact about\nreductions and no longer a fact about Y’s running time.\n\nThe weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched. The manuscript gives two (p. 5,\nlines 229–236). The first is the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates\nto SETH: “The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies OVC [Wil05,\nVW19]” (lines 231–232). That implication is a theorem with a false premise, like (E) and (S): it\nsays nothing about OVC either way, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal\nvectors. The second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which we quote\nwith the manuscript’s list of the problems it covers elided (lines 233–236):\n     “The lower bounds for [. . . ] also follow from the hypothesis that unrestricted CNF-SAT admits\n     no 2(1−δ)n poly(n, m)-time algorithm for any constant δ > 0 [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n     1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width k, while this hypothesis allows\n     the width to grow with n.”\n\nThe verified statement cannot reach this hypothesis, and the reason is visible in the order of its\nwords (Section 2): the machine and the constants C, A, S, d, e are chosen after k, and nothing in\nthe statement bounds how they grow with k. For a formula whose width grows with n there\nis no single machine and no single bound in the statement at all; even the trivial observation\nthat every k-CNF is a CNF runs the wrong way, since the hypothesis speaks about all widths\nat once. So the unrestricted-width hypothesis, and every lower bound that follows from it, is\nexactly where it was.\n\nWhat a transferred algorithm inherits. The manuscript’s one positive transfer is Set Splitting\nwith sets of size at most k (given small sets over a universe of elements, colour the elements with\ntwo colours so that no set is all one colour; p. 5, lines 239–243): one variable per element and\ntwo clauses per set give a k-CNF with “one variable per universe element, so for fixed k Theorem\n1.1 gives subexponential time in the universe size, up to a polynomial input-size factor,” citing\nCygan et al. (2016)’s Observation 3.6. Section 3 tags this supported: the encoding is on paper,\n\n                                                   34\n\fthe counter is the verified one. What such a transfer inherits is the verified guarantee on the\n                                          ′\nSAT instance it builds, C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n ) with n′ the number of SAT variables, and therefore the\n                                                                         ′\ntitle’s limitation with it: at A = 1 the exponential factor is above 2n for every 50 ≤ n′ < e5504\n(Table 2.1), with C, A, d unknown. In general terms: an algorithm obtained from the verified\nstatement through a variable-controlling reduction is a subexponential algorithm whose verified\nguarantee is weaker, at every practical size, than trying every assignment of the SAT instance it\nconstructs. That is not a defect of the transfer; it is the same sentence as the title, read through a\nreduction.\n\n6.4     What this does not claim\n      • No statement “no (c − ε)k algorithm” is refuted, for any problem, any size measure or any\n        constant c. Such a statement, proved from SETH, has lost its proof and kept its truth value,\n        which is unknown.\n\n      • No statement “no 2o(n) algorithm for X” is refuted either, unless X has a reduction to\n        fixed-width SAT that controls the number of variables; the manuscript names one such\n        problem (Set Splitting) and we add none.\n\n      • The unrestricted-width hypothesis is untouched, and so is the Orthogonal Vectors Con-\n        jecture, and so is every lower bound proved from either; the verified statement is for each\n        fixed k and says nothing about widths that grow with n.\n\n      • Nothing about any specific problem’s complexity beyond what the manuscript’s §1.1.1\n        and §1.1.3 state and we quote. The problems the manuscript lists at lines 213–225 and 233–\n        235 are not discussed here, and no example from any other setting is given, by the scope of\n        this paper.\n\n      • No re-reading of the sources. Each hypothesis is stated in the form the manuscript gives it,\n        with the manuscript’s citation; we did not check that the cited sections and definitions state\n        it in the same words. If a source’s form is stronger than the manuscript’s, the refutation of\n        the manuscript’s form may not reach it, and the reader should check the source.\n\n      • About the limit only. Every refutation here denies a statement about the limit and uses\n        only that ρ(n) grows more slowly than n. None says that any machine decides or counts\n        anything fast at any size; the constants are as unknown here as in Section 2, and the verified\n        bound is above 2n on every 50 ≤ n < e5504 .\n\n      • Nothing beyond the verified statement. The manuscript’s sharper exponents (Section 3)\n        are not used; where the manuscript derives a consequence from them, it is not a conse-\n        quence of what is verified and is not claimed here.\n\n\n7      For the practitioner\nThe title compares two guarantees. One is the verified bound, C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) , with C, A and d\nnever computed (comparator file lines 136–139 and 210–211, Section 2). The other is the trivial\nbound for trying every assignment, 2n times a polynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs.\nWhat runs on a real machine, when the question is whether a formula can be satisfied, is a SAT\n\n                                                   35\n\fsolver; the standard kind is called CDCL, for conflict-driven clause learning, a search that learns\nfrom each dead end it reaches. When the question is how many satisfying assignments there are,\nwhat runs is a model counter, exact or approximate. No implementation of the verified machine\nexists to run beside them. This section measures the tools a practitioner has, on random formulas,\nand draws the two guarantees next to the measurements as shapes, so that the reader can see\nwhere each sits. The point is small and the section says it once: the verified guarantee is not the\nbinding one on any machine, because it is above 2n for every n ≥ 50 (Section 2), while the solvers\nare many orders of magnitude below 2n at every size they were run at.\n\n7.1   Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements\nThe verified statement says a machine exists (comparator line 195 opens with the existence quan-\ntifier, Section 2). The solution’s 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no #eval, no\ndef main and no input-output (IO) action. The script of this section checks that, reading every\nfile of the family with open() and counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0. The library proves that\na machine with the stated bound exists and exhibits one as a mathematical term, inside a de-\nvelopment with 1,048 noncomputable declarations and no way to execute anything (Section 4);\nnothing in the clone runs it on an input, and we found no implementation elsewhere. So the\nverified algorithm’s actual running time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing\nsays it is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its guarantee, and a guarantee is\ncompared with other guarantees. The measurements below are of other programs entirely. They\nare in the section because the question a practitioner asks is not “which guarantee is smaller”\nbut “what would I run”, and the answer to that has not changed.\n\n7.2   What was run\nInstances. Random 3-CNF on n variables with m = ⌊4.26 n⌉ clauses, each clause three dis-\ntinct variables with random signs, generated by the script from (n, seed); twenty seeds per size;\nn = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 400, 500. The ratio 4.26 clauses per variable is where\nrandom 3-SAT switches from mostly satisfiable to mostly unsatisfiable (Selman et al., 1996); ran-\ndom formulas are hardest to decide there (a method from statistical physics puts the threshold\nat 4.2667, Mertens et al., 2006). The sizes are chosen so that the smallest ones are where the title’s\ncrossing is (n = 50) and the largest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas begins to take\nseconds. These are random instances only; nothing is claimed about formulas with structure.\n\nTools. CaDiCaL 1.9.5 (Biere et al., 2024), a CDCL solver, through python-sat 1.9.dev15 (Ignatiev\net al., 2018), one solve() call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0 (Sharma et al., 2019), an\nexact model counter, through the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called Arjun\nand then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 (Chakraborty et al., 2013; Soos and\nMeel, 2019), an approximate counter, at its defaults ε = 0.8, δ = 0.2 (an answer within a factor 1.8\nof the truth with probability 0.8), with the count taken over all variables (its “projection set”); and,\nat n ≤ 30 only, exact counting by listing every solution with CaDiCaL, one at a time, which is not\na counter at any larger size (its time grows with the count, and the count grows exponentially\nin n) and is here as the independent check of the two counters where it can reach. Ganak and\nApproxMC were installed from PyPI for this section; no sharpSAT, d4 or other counter binary\nis on the host.\n\n                                                    36\n\fProtocol. Each call runs in its own interpreter under nice -n 19, at most eight at a time on the\nthirty-two cores of an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was shared\nwith other work, load average at the start in tables/practitioner_meta.json), with a wall-\nclock budget of 60 s per call. The time recorded is the wall clock (perf_counter) around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child process, with the interpreter’s\nstart-up and the import outside it. A call over budget is killed and recorded as censored, that is,\nas “more than 60 s”. A size at which a tool finishes fewer than ten of its twenty instances ends\nthat tool’s climb to larger sizes. The whole run took about twenty minutes (1,172 s).\n\n7.3   What came out\nTable 7.1 gives, per size, the number of satisfiable instances, CaDiCaL’s median and maximum\nover the twenty seeds, the number of instances each counter finished with its median and maxi-\nmum (a median over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the budget, so a median is\na number whenever more than ten finished), and the two guarantees in the common scale of the\nnext subsection. Figure 7.1 draws the same.\n\nTable 7.1: Random 3-CNF at m = ⌊4.26 n⌉, twenty seeds per size, one machine, 60 s per call. Columns:\nn variables; m clauses; “sat”, the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL finished; for each\ntool, “fin”, the number of instances it finished within budget, and “med” and “max”, its median and\nmaximum seconds over all twenty seeds with a censored run counted as > 60. The last two columns\nare the two guarantees as shapes: 2n (trying every assignment) and 2ρ(n) (the verified bound), both\nwith constant 1 and both multiplied by one common factor t20 /220 that puts 2n through CaDiCaL’s me-\ndian at n = 20 (t20 = 1.28 × 10−4 s). They are shapes, not running times. Regenerate: python3 -I\npaper3/scripts/practitioner.py.\n\n                            CaDiCaL                Ganak             ApproxMC           guarantee, s\n        n    m    sat       med        max fin     med     max fin     med    max          2n          2ρ ( n )\n       20   85 12/20 1.3 × 10−4 1.6 × 10−4    20 0.0015 0.0047    20 0.0016 0.0073 1.3 × 10−4 1.9 × 10−5\n       30 128 12/20 1.6 × 10−4 1.9 × 10−4     20 0.0014 0.0295    20 0.0031 0.0074     0.1310     0.0254\n       40 170 11/20 2.0 × 10−4 2.6 × 10−4     20 0.0018 0.0466    20 0.0058 0.0117        134      53.18\n       50 213 16/20 2.2 × 10−4 3.6 × 10−4     20 0.0041    9.48   20 0.0044 0.0148       105.1      105.2\n       60 256 12/20 3.5 × 10−4 6.9 × 10−4     20 0.0288    9.60   20 0.0099 0.0151       10 8.1     108.7\n       80 341 12/20 6.4 × 10−4      0.0016    20 0.0518 11.76     20 0.0133 0.0245      10 14.2    1016.1\n      100 426 9/20       0.0025     0.0038    20   8.34 10.97     20 0.0219 0.1987      10 20.2    1023.6\n      150 639 10/20      0.0169     0.0443    20   6.16 16.11     20 0.0595    1.01     10 35.2    1043.3\n      200 852 15/20      0.0578     0.3906    20   2.37 29.05     20 0.5104 25.21       10 50.3    1063.5","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"a4fa9a42-6256-46a4-a944-48bb3d60ca89","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:51:32.678Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WXi6JjrrAwezRN958qXGt6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter pass over paper 3 PDF"}
