{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VEkCqsgnjXVD3SesXTv9eF","type":"tool_result","content":"paper3/scripts/related_work.py.\n\nSource                       Where it is on disk        When, and at what      SHA-256 (first 12)\n                                                        commit\nOpenAI collection,           clone, commit 4eaee875     2026-10-29 (clone)     –\nhistory.md line 18\nOpenAI collection,           clone, commit 4eaee875     2026-10-29 (clone)     –\nlean/docs/378.md\nOpenAI collection, lean/     clone, commit 4eaee875     2026-10-29 (clone)     –\nComparatorChallenges/\nREADME.md and\nSubexponentialSAT.\njson\narXiv 2608.14673, API        related/fetched/           fetched 2026-11-05     2737bacdb870\nrecord (v3)                  arxiv_2608.14673_api.\n                             xml\narXiv 2608.14673, abstract   related/fetched/           fetched 2026-11-05     9047b0010cc3\npage                         arxiv_2608.14673_abs.\n                             html\nleanprover/comparator        related/fetched/           fetched 2026-11-05;    39e03761a9b2\nREADME (master)              comparator_README_         commit 865edc14 of\n                             master.md                  2026-10-14; archived\nammkrn/nanoda_lib            related/fetched/           fetched 2026-11-05;    d4d3f3be41ba\nREADME (master)              nanoda_README_master.      commit 8de7e0c4 of\n                             md                         2026-10-29 (the\n                                                        replay’s)\nImpagliazzo & Paturi 2001;   not held; manuscript       –                      –\nImpagliazzo, Paturi & Zane   extract lines 3733–3736\n2001\n\n\n\n\n                                                   44\n\fThree things in this sentence matter for us. First, the group it names is led by two of the three\nauthors of the papers that stated the hypotheses (Section 9.2). Second, the verb is “will”: the log\nannounces an examination to come. Nothing from that group is in the clone, and we fetched noth-\ning from it, so this paper has no finding of theirs to cite, agree with or disagree with. Whatever\nthat group publishes about the proof will carry more weight on the proof than Section 4 does,\nwhich only inspected and replayed. Third, the log says “the paper gives . . . subexponential time”\nwith no exponent; the exponent is in the scope note.\n    That scope note is lean/docs/378.md, seventeen lines, quoted in full in Section 3. It is\nthe collection’s own statement of the gap between what Lean proves (the exponent with the\nsquared log log factor) and what the manuscript claims (two sharper exponents). Its table\n(line 17) links the result to the one comparator statement, lean/ComparatorChallenges/\nSubexponentialSAT.lean. So the collection itself records that the verified exponent is the\nweaker one. What it does not record is what the weaker exponent means at any size: that ρ(n),\nthe verified exponent, is larger than n for every n between 50 and e5504 , so that the verified bound\nwith constant one sits above 2n at every size anyone will ever run (Section 2). That is the title’s\nobservation, and the note does not make it.\n    The third document is lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md, ten lines. It says how\nthe collection’s checks are run: “Install comparator, landrun, and lean4export, and make\nthem available on PATH.” (line 3), then from the lean/ directory lake update, lake exe\ncache get, and lake env comparator on a family’s JSON (lines 7–9; the example is another\nfamily’s). The JSON for this family has six fields (Section 9.4 reads them).\n\n9.2   The two papers that stated the hypotheses\nETH, the Exponential Time Hypothesis, says that 3-SAT (deciding whether a formula whose\nclauses have three literals each can be satisfied) has no algorithm whose running time grows\nslower than every exponential in the number of variables. SETH, the Strong form, says that\nas the clause width grows, the best possible base of the exponential tends to 2, the base of\ntrying every assignment. Both are from Impagliazzo and Paturi (2001); the standard setting of\nSETH, and the lemma that lets one move between the number of variables and the number of\nclauses, are from Impagliazzo et al. (2001). The manuscript cites both as [IP01] and [IPZ01]\n(extract page 66, lines 3733–3736) and states each hypothesis in its own words in its §1.1.1, which\nSection 6 quotes with page and line. We do not hold either paper: literature/ has no file for\nthem, and literature/MISSING.md now records both with their DOIs. Every statement of a\nhypothesis in this paper is therefore the manuscript’s statement of it, not the original’s, and we\nquote nothing from the two originals. The manuscript’s own account of what motivated SETH is\none sentence we can locate (page 4, lines 170–171): the improvements of the known algorithms\n“over exhaustive search diminish as k grows [IP01, Open Problems, item 2]”. Exhaustive search\nis the “trying all 2n assignments” of the title.\n\n9.3   A human audit of an earlier batch of results\nSienicki and Sienicki (2026) is a published human audit of OpenAI’s AI-generated proofs. We\nfetched its arXiv abstract page and API record (v1 of 3 August 2026, v2 of 25 August, v3 of 9\nSeptember 2026; 13 pages; primary class cs.AI). It is not about this family. Its abstract says what\nit covers: “We assess 18 chapter-specific reviews of the ten mathematical results announced by\n\n\n                                                 45\n\fOpenAI on 1 August 2026, alongside review standards, Lean formalizations, subsequent research,\nand mathematical references.” The family this paper reads is dated 17 October 2026, revised 25\nOctober, and entered the collection on 26 October (Section 9.1); it is not one of the ten, and the\nabstract mentions neither it nor ETH (the script checks). So we cite the audit for its method of\nreview and for nothing about the proof.\n    Four sentences of the abstract bear on how this paper was written. On restraint: “The ar-\nticle audits this review record without claiming a complete reconstruction of all ten proofs.”\nOn the finding: “No confirmed substantive mathematical error in a principal result remains in\nthe examined assessments, although review depth varies and some dependencies remain partly\nchecked.” On a hazard of reading proofs through text extraction: “In Chapter 6, an apparent\npolarity error was withdrawn after an overbar lost during PDF extraction was recovered from\nthe typeset source.” And on what confidence should rest on: “We argue that confidence should\ncombine formal checking, human reconstruction, independent mathematical use, and a public\nrecord supporting correction of both proofs and reviews.”\n    The third of these is a warning we took. This paper reads the manuscript through a\npdftotext extract, the same kind of text in which that overbar was lost. That is why every\nquotation from the manuscript carries the page of the PDF beside the line of the extract, why\nthe Lean is read from its source files and never from a rendering, and why no claim in this\npaper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the formulas that matter are the Lean’s,\nre-typed in Section 2 from the .lean file and re-checked by the kernel in Section 8. Against\nthe fourth sentence, this paper is one of the four things it lists, formal checking, replayed by us\nand by a second kernel, together with a reading of what the checked statement says. It is not a\nhuman reconstruction of the proof: Section 4 inspected the proof’s files and did not re-derive the\nargument. We fetched the abstract only, and quote nothing from the paper’s body.\n\n9.4   The comparator\nThe comparator is the program the collection used to check that the solution file proves the\nchallenge statement (Lean FRO, 2026). We fetched its README from the master branch of\nleanprover/comparator on 2026-11-05 (latest commit 865edc14 of 2026-10-14, “doc: migra-\ntion notice”; the repository is archived and under the Apache-2.0 licence). Its first lines say the\ntool has moved: “This project has been merged into core as of Lean release v4.35.0 in the form\nof lake comparator and the accompanying lake check” (line 3). The collection pins Lean\nv4.34.1 (Section 4), one release earlier, so the stand-alone tool is the one its README names. The\nREADME opens: “Comparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs.” (line 8).\n    What it checks is stated as three guarantees for the theorems a JSON configuration names\n(lines 52–55):\n      “All theorems in Solution that are listed in theorem_names are guaranteed to:\n      1. Prove the same statement as provided in Challenge\n      2. Use no more axioms than listed in permitted_axioms\n      3. Be accepted by the Lean kernel”\n\nIn plain words: the solution proves exactly the sentence the challenge posed, uses only the agreed\naxioms (the three standard ones in the example configuration, line 27), and passes the kernel,\nthe small core of Lean that checks proofs. How it checks them is in its “Internals” (lines 184–\n200), six steps: build the challenge inside a sandbox; export its declarations with lean4export;\n\n\n                                                46\n\fdo the same for the solution; “Verify that all declarations used in the statement of all relevant\ntheorems in Challenge are the same as in the Solution environment” (lines 191–192); “Verify\nthat the body of all relevant theorems in the Solution environment only uses axioms listed in\npermitted_axioms” (lines 195–196); and “Replay the Solution environment into the Lean\nkernel” (line 197). The first guarantee is therefore a match of the statement’s declarations as\nexported, not a textual comparison of the two files. That is the check Section 4 says we did not\nreproduce: our statement match is textual (Table 4.2, row (iv)).\n    The guarantees hold under six stated assumptions (lines 34–45). The one that concerns the\nkernel is the fifth: “The Lean kernel is correct (with external_kernels this can be reduced to\n‘At least one of the Lean kernel or the external_kernels is correct’)” (lines 43–44). The sand-\nbox is landrun (line 10), a tool that confines the build so that a malicious solution file cannot\ntamper with the checker; the README notes that its trusted base “naturally includes the oper-\nating system and hardware it is running on, plus its sandboxing mechanism” (line 58). External\nkernels are optional: “Comparator can additionally check solutions with external kernels” (line\n70), and “For backwards compatibility reasons users may instead set enable_nanoda: true\nto obtain a config that calls nanoda_bin” (lines 86–87). The tool “was originally developed by\nLean FRO, with feedback from the AIMO team, in support of the AIMO series of competitions”\n(lines 207–208).\n    For this family the configuration has six fields (lean/ComparatorChallenges/\nSubexponentialSAT.json): the challenge module; the solution module, OAI.Algorithms.\nSubexponentialSAT.Main; one theorem name, SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat;\nan empty definition_names; the three standard axioms as permitted_axioms; and\nenable_nanoda: false. There is no external_kernels field. So the check the collection\nconfigured for this family is the Lean kernel alone, under the fifth assumption in its unweakened\nform. Section 5 is the weakened form, supplied by us: the same theorem’s dependencies through\na second kernel. Our bwrap sandbox there played the role landrun plays here, for the same\nreason, and we did not run the comparator itself.\n\n9.5   nanoda\nnanoda is the second checker (ammkrn, 2026). Its README, fetched from the master branch of\nammkrn/nanoda_lib on 2026-11-05, says what it is in one sentence (line 3): “This is an external\ntype checker for the Lean 4 programming language and theorem prover.” The repository’s de-\nscription line is “Library implementing type inference/checking functionality based on the Lean\ntheorem prover”; the licence is Apache-2.0. It reads the file lean4export writes: “Export files\ncan be created with lean4export” (line 13), and “Given that lean4export already uses json as its\nexport format, adding a json parser as a dependency doesn’t increase the overall complexity of\nthe project” (line 23). Its axiom rule is the comparator’s: “The average Lean user will want to per-\nmit the three semi-official axioms, which are Quot.sound, Classical.choice, and propext,\nand most export files will also rely on Lean.trustCompiler” (line 33); the export we checked\ncontains the first three and no fourth (Section 5). Two kernel extensions for arithmetic on literals\nare off by default and “enable or disable the Nat and String kernel extensions” (line 39); the\nreplay turned both on.\n    The latest commit of the repository at the time of the fetch is 8de7e0c4 of 2026-10-29, and\nthat is the commit the replay built and ran (version 0.4.31; the script checks the replay’s log\nagainst the fetched record). The checker is written in Rust and shares no code with the Lean\n\n                                                47\n\fkernel, which is what makes it a second opinion. Its author’s book on external type checkers,\nlinked from line 3 of the README, was not fetched.\n\n9.6     Where we looked for an earlier statement of the title’s observation\nThe title says the verified guarantee is weaker than trying all 2n assignments at every practi-\ncal size. We looked for an earlier statement of that observation in the texts this paper rests\non, and in no others: the collection’s history.md, its scope note lean/docs/378.md, the ab-\nstract of Sienicki and Sienicki (2026), the comparator README, the nanoda README, and the\nmanuscript extract. The script searches each for the words such a statement would be likely to\nuse (“practical”, “brute”, “trying all”, “exhaustive”, “2n ”, “constants are”, “concrete”). Outside\nthe manuscript, none occurs. In the manuscript, “practical” occurs once (page 4, line 153), in a\nsentence about the practical value of SAT solvers on industrial formulas, a different sense; “ex-\nhaustive” occurs eleven times, as the motivation of SETH quoted above (line 170) and as the\nproof’s own fallback for small inputs (lines 2480 and 2487, where the algorithm uses “exhaus-\ntive search” below the cutoff n ≥ 20). The nearest earlier statements are the manuscript’s own\nsentence that Lean verifies “the weaker exponent” (page 2, lines 63–67; Section 3) and the scope\nnote: both say the verified exponent is the weaker one, and neither says at what sizes the verified\nbound exceeds 2n , which is the title’s point. Within these texts we found no prior statement of it.\nThe search is bounded by that list: we did not search the literature at large, the working group’s\noutput, or the press.\n\n9.7     What this does not claim\n      • Nothing about the working group’s findings. The collection’s log says the group “will\n        examine and explain the proof”; we hold nothing it has written, and we neither anticipate\n        nor contradict it.\n\n      • The human audit does not cover this family. It is cited for its method and its stated lesson\n        about PDF extraction; its body was not fetched, and no sentence of it beyond the abstract\n        is quoted.\n\n      • The two hypothesis papers were not read. They are cited as the manuscript cites them,\n        and the hypotheses are stated as the manuscript states them (Section 6).\n\n      • We did not run the comparator. What we reproduced is stated in Section 4’s Table 4.2; the\n        comparator’s statement match by exported declarations is not among it.\n\n      • No claim that landrun, lean4export or nanoda is correct. Each is quoted for what its\n        README says it does; the replay’s evidence is that two independent checkers agreed on\n        this export, and the trust left in lean4export is stated in Section 5.\n\n      • “No prior statement” is bounded by the list above. Six texts, read with a script; not a\n        literature search.\n\n\n\n\n                                                  48\n\f10     Conclusion\nOpenAI’s family 378 holds a theorem the Lean kernel accepts, and the theorem refutes the Expo-\nnential Time Hypothesis as stated. We read it, inspected its proof, rebuilt the proof from source\nand re-checked it with a second checker, and found no flaw. That is the first thing this paper says,\nand it is said without qualification. The second thing is what the title says, and it is a reading of\nthe statement, not of the proof. The statement is a guarantee: a machine exists and halts within\na bound. The bound’s constants exist and are never computed; where the proof writes values\ndown, its construction has at least 257 for the exponent’s constant, and the theorem it exhibits has\nat least 258 there and at least 28,701 in front (Sections 2 and 4). The exponent is the manuscript’s\nweaker one, and with its constant set to one it exceeds the number of variables at every size from\n50 up to a number with 2,391 digits (Section 2). So at every practical size the verified guarantee\nis weaker than the guarantee of trying every assignment. The manuscript’s sharper exponents,\nwhich would change that sentence, are not in the Lean library (Section 3). What follows from\nthe verified statement is exactly what the manuscript says follows: the hypotheses fall, the lower\nbounds derived from them lose their premise, and the conclusions of those lower bounds are\nuntouched (Section 6). For anyone who runs a solver, nothing changes (Section 7). The two facts\nare true at once, and neither diminishes the other: in the limit the exponent is smaller than the\nnumber of variables by any factor one names, which is a theorem in our own Lean (Section 8);\non every size anyone will write down, it is larger.\n\n10.1   What is claimed, and what is not\nThe introduction’s two lists (Section 1.3) are the paper’s claims and non-claims, and each later\nsection repeats its own under “what this does not claim”. In one sentence each: the verified\nstatement is a guarantee with uncomputed constants, above 2n at every practical size with the\nconstants set to one, and refuting ETH and its relatives nonetheless; the proof shows no flaw by\ninspection, builds from source, and passes two checkers; the sharper exponents are paper-only;\nand no running time of any machine, no flaw in anything, no value of any constant beyond\nthe proof’s own, and no refutation of any derived lower bound’s conclusion is asserted. Every\nnumber in the paper has a script under paper3/scripts/ that regenerates it, every quotation\nhas a page and line or a file and line, and the replay’s logs and hashes are under paper3/\nreplay/.\n\n10.2   The questions this reading left open, by entry\nThe paper was written under a rule that no question stops the work: a choice between two\nsound ways of saying or doing a thing is made, recorded in paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD as\none numbered entry with what was ambiguous, what was chosen and what the author may\nwant to change, and the writing goes on. Forty-four entries were written before this section\nand four by it. We reviewed all forty-eight and sort them here. Fifteen need no decision: seven\nwere resolved by the author in writing (S1, the author line; S3, where the companion Lean lives,\nwith 07a following it; S4, no width references; S6, the model counters; S7, the phrase “for every\n50 ≤ n < e5504 ”; S8, the replay’s Lean version) and eight are procedural or are corrections already\ncarried by the text (S5, the per-item time cap of the writing driver; 01a, the sandbox’s core cap;\n03b, the manuscript has 67 pages; 04b, 04d and 04e, three counts corrected from an earlier audit;\n\n\n                                                 49\n\f06b, a counter installed for Section 7; 08xb, which sections the plain-English rewrite covered).\nThe thirty-three below affect the text and are open: in each case the text stands as chosen, and\nthe entry says what would change.\n\nThe title and its figures. S2: 2n is typeset with a superscript and the ellipsis as three dots; the\n     plain text is in the PDF’s metadata. 02a: Section 2 carries both the two-panel Figure 2.1\n     and Figure 2.2, three drawings of one function. 02b: the central figure’s peak is placed\n     analytically and its legend carries the +16.\nThe statement (Section 2). 02c: a proof-side fact, the constant 256 + At , is quoted in the state-\n     ment section for its value. 02d: the Lean theorem is transcribed as a display, not shown\n     verbatim, because the house build cannot typeset its symbols. 08xd: Section 2 says the\n     proof’s constant is at least 257 and the exhibited theorem’s at least 258, two readings of two\n     lines, both kept.\nBeyond the statement (Section 3). 03a: a third tag, “supported”, between verified and paper-\n    only. 03c: what the verified exponent would give through Corollary 1.4’s argument is not\n    worked out. 03d: Corollary 1.2 is stated with the verified exponent and tagged supported,\n    not verified.\nThe proof and the replay (Sections 4 and 5). 01b: the second checker is nanoda_lib at the\n     commit named, with lean4export at the nearest tag. 01c: that checker was run with\n     the three axioms permitted and unused axioms ignored. 01d: “replayed” means the build,\n     the axiom output and the second checker, not the comparator run. 04a: thirty-five defini-\n     tions compared, thirty-three identical, two up to a keyword; the audit’s “33” is explained,\n     not kept. 04c: the exhibited constants, A ≥ 258 and C ≥ 28,701, are stated with their lines.\nWhat follows (Section 6). 05a: the manuscript’s list of problems is elided from one quotation,\n    by the paper’s scope. 05b: SETH is refuted by reading the statement at each width, and\n    “SETH implies ETH” is cited for the record. 05c: the hypotheses’ sources are copied from\n    the manuscript’s reference list and not re-read. 05d: two paragraphs are ours and not the\n    manuscript’s, and say so.\nFor the practitioner (Section 7). 06a: the two guarantee curves share one scale factor, so that\n      they cross where the statement puts the crossing. 06c: seven references for the tools and\n      the threshold are from memory, with no DOI checked. 06d: the counters are reported at\n      their defaults, including a plateau that looks like a preprocessing budget.\nOur Lean (Section 8). 07b: the axiom audit prints two lines with fewer than the three axioms,\n     and “every line on the three” is read as “nothing beyond the three”. 07c: the re-diff also\n     matches the restatement’s body to the theorem’s, beyond what was asked.\nRelated work (Section 9). 08a: the audit paper’s title is typeset with an apostrophe the arXiv\n     record lacks. 08b: four small public records were fetched beside the three allowed pages,\n     for their dates and commits. 08c: fourteen references carry no checked DOI. 08d: “we\n     found no earlier statement” is bounded to six texts.\nPlain English, and this section. 08xa: the dense passages were moved into Appendix B, not cut.\n     08xc: the glosses are repeated in each section and now also in the introduction. 09a: the in-\n     troduction and abstract state the title’s third clause in words, with no formula beyond 2n , by\n     the plain-English rule rather than by the item’s brief. 09b: the abstract carries two numbers,\n\n                                                50\n\f       both from Section 2’s script, and no count from the replay. 09c: this sorting of the entries\n       into resolved, procedural and open is the author’s to redo. 09d: “people who checked” in\n       the stance sentence is read as the author and the assistant the acknowledgements disclose.\n\n   Thirteen of the thirty-three are bibliographic or typographic (S2, 02b, 02d, 05c, 06c, 08a, 08b,\n08c and the five spelling, placement and tagging choices of 02a, 03a, 03d, 04a, 08xa); the rest are\nchoices about what the text says, and each is a sentence that can be struck without touching any\nnumber.\n\n10.3    What this does not claim\n   • Nothing new. This section states no fact that a previous section does not state with its\n     source; the numbers in it are Sections 2, 4 and 5’s.\n\n   • The list of open questions is complete to the day it was written. Entries added after it, by\n     the reading of the whole paper that follows this draft, are in the file and not here.\n\n   • No question is answered here. Each entry records a choice and what would change; the\n     author’s answer, where there is one, is in the file’s “Owner resolutions” and in the text.\n\n   • No claim about the working group. The collection says a group of computer scientists will\n     examine the proof (Section 9); nothing of theirs is on record here, and their reading of the\n     proof will carry more weight on the proof than our inspection does.\n\n\nData, code and proofs\nPlaceholder. Item 11 completes this statement: the repository, the scripts under paper3/\nscripts/ that regenerate every number, the replay’s commands and log hashes under\npaper3/replay/, the Lean names and the axiom audit under paper3/lean/, and the read-\nonly clone of https://github.com/openai/math at commit 4eaee875.\n\n\nAcknowledgements\nPlaceholder. Item 11 writes the acknowledgements, with the disclosure of AI use in the form\nof paper1/latex/sec8_ack.tex.\n\n\nReferences","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"b009b105-dbff-4df4-b5bc-c7a9bbddc06b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:51:36.395Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VEkCqsgnjXVD3SesXTv9eF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent