{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CSxcaN1UERqXHSZ7Dh4n1T","type":"tool_result","content":"The body of the paper is written so that a reader with no background in complexity theory or in\nproof assistants can follow it. The passages that could not be made plain without losing precision\nare collected here, each under the heading of the section that cites it. Nothing in this appendix is\nnew; it is the dense form of what the body says in words.\n\n\n\n\n                                                55\n\fB.1   The definitions of the comparator file, line by line\nThis is the detail behind Section 2.2.  Line numbers are those                             of   lean/\nComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean at commit 4eaee875.\n\nFormulas and the count (lines 13–51). A Literal V is a variable with a sign (lines 19–22); a\nClause V is a Finset of literals and a CNF V a Finset of clauses (lines 24–25). An assignment\nis any function V → Bool (line 17). A literal evaluates to the Boolean “the assignment agrees with\nits sign” (lines 29–30), a clause to “some literal is true” (lines 32–33), a formula to “every clause is\ntrue” (lines 35–36). WidthAtMost k F says every clause has at most k literals as a set (line 38).\nOver a finite variable type, satisfyingAssignments F filters the universe of all assignments\n(lines 44–45), and count F is its cardinality (line 47): the number of satisfying assignments\namong all 2n when V = Fin n. The Finset choices change nothing in the meaning: a repeated\nclause or literal collapses, the empty clause is allowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause\n{ x, ¬ x } is allowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the manuscript’s\n“Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k literals” (p. 1, line 44).\n\nThe input encoding (lines 53–81). encodeNat n is n ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\nframe doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and ends it with a zero (lines 57–58).\nencodeList enc prefixes each element’s frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines\n60–62). A literal becomes the number 2 · var + [positive], at most 2n − 1 (lines 64–65); a clause\nis its sorted list of literal numbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67–71). encodeCNF\nF is encodeNat n followed by the formula’s list of lists of unary numbers (lines 73–77), and\nformulaBitLength F is the length of that bit string (line 79). Two things follow. The number\nof variables n is part of the input, so a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the\nlength is polynomial: a clause of j ≤ k literals costs 2j(4n + 1) + 4 bits, so\n\n              L = formulaBitLength F ≤ n + 2 + m (2k(4n + 1) + 4) = O(k n m)\n\nfor m clauses. The solution itself proves ( L + 1)d ≤ (8k + 7)d (n + | F | + 1)2d (SqrtLogSpace/\nStatement.lean, lines 9–14). Emulating the definitions (reports/scratch/eth_audit/\ncheck_encoding.py), a formula with n = 125, m = 500, k = 4 encodes to L = 1,006,223 bits\nand one with n = 1,000, m = 4,000, k = 3 to 47.9 million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in n.\n\nThe machine (lines 83–128). PrimitiveMachine (lines 92–99) is a finite object: a number\ntapeCount of tapes and a number stateCount of states, an input tape and a start state, a\ntable scan saying which tape each state reads, and a table transition from Fin stateCount ×\nOption Bool—a domain of 3 · stateCount points—into either a halting answer (a Bool) or a\nnext state with one action. The action type is Mathlib’s Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool):\nmove the head left or right, or write one of the three symbols none, some false, some true,\nfive values in all (Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean\nlines 118–120 and Tape.lean lines 421–423 at Mathlib d13f23b7, the clone’s pinned revision).\nA configuration Cfg (lines 103–105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib Tape per tape index\n(a head symbol and two blank-padded halves, Tape.lean lines 408–414, blank = none). step\n(lines 107–119) returns none in a halted state; otherwise it reads the head of the one tape the\n\n                                                  56\n\fstate scans, looks the pair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one symbol\nor moves that one head by one cell and changes state. init (lines 121–124) puts the input on the\ninput tape with the head at its first bit and every other tape blank (Tape.mk1 , Tape.lean line\n494). A Lean function on a finite domain is, whatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is\none lookup in a table of 3 · stateCount rows, and no field of the structure can carry unbounded\ncomputation.\n\nTraces (lines 87–90). Trace step t a c is an inductive proposition with two constructors:\nrefl, a trace of length 0 from a to a itself, and cons, which extends a trace from b by one\nsuccessful step a 7→ b. So it holds exactly when c is reached from a by t successful applications of\nstep, and a trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since step is a function,\nthe run is deterministic.\n\nSpace monitoring (lines 147–189). LocatedCfg (lines 149–153) pairs a configuration with, per\ntape, the head’s current position and the least and greatest positions seen so far, as integers;\nlocatedInit starts every head at 0 with the input tape’s range covering the input (lines 155–\n159); headDisplacement is −1, +1 or 0 for a move left, a move right or a write (lines 161–164);\nmonitor (lines 166–182) applies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\nminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else; locatedStep is step on the\nconfiguration followed by monitor (lines 184–186). visitedSpace (lines 188–189) is the sum\nover tapes of upper − lower + 1: the number of cells whose head position was ever visited.\ncountWord a is the binary expansion of a, least significant bit first, with [false] for 0 (line\n147), and toNat (lines 7–9) decodes such a word.\n\nThe exponent and the bound (lines 130–141).\n      runtimeScale n = n * (Real.log (Real.log (n + 16)))ˆ2 /\n      Real.sqrt (Real.log (n + 16))\n\n(lines 132–134, transcribed; the casts to R omitted), that is\n                                                                2\n                                              n (ln ln(n + 16))\n                                    ρ(n) =       p              ,\n                                                   ln(n + 16)\n\nwith Real.log the natural logarithm. The manuscript fixes the same base: “Throughout, log\ndenotes the natural logarithm” (p. 1, lines 48–49; again at p. 7, lines 326–327). The shift by 16\nkeeps ln ln defined and positive at every n ≥ 0 (ln 16 = 2.77), so the theorem can be stated for all\nn with no proviso. Then\n\n      runtimeBound C A d L n = C * (L + 1)ˆd * 2ˆ(A * runtimeScale n)\n\n(lines 136–139, transcribed), a real number, with the real-exponent power.\n\n\n\n\n                                                 57\n\fB.2   The theorem, with its quantifiers\nThis is the detail behind Section 2.3. Lines 194–217 of the comparator file state, for every k with\n2 ≤ k:\n       ∃ machine, countTape, answerTape, C, A, S ∈ R, d, e ∈ N,\n         0<C ∧ 0<A ∧ 0<S ∧ 0<d ∧ 0<e∧\n         ∀ n ∈ N, ∀ F : CNF (Fin n), WidthAtMost k F →\n           ∃ time ∈ N, final : LocatedCfg machine,\n              Trace (locatedStep machine) time (locatedInit machine (encodeCNF F )) final\n                     (lines 201–202)\n              ∧ the count tape holds countWord (count F) (203–204)\n              ∧ the answer tape holds the bit [0 < count F ] (205–206)\n              ∧ the halting state carries that bit (207)\n              ∧ toNat (countWord (count F )) = count F (208)\n              ∧ |countWord (count F )| ≤ n + 1 (209)\n              ∧ time ≤ C ( L + 1)d 2 A ρ(n) , L = formulaBitLength F (210–211)\n              ∧