x D proves both bounds in Theorem 1.1 by tensor contraction.\n==================== lines 68-71: page(before-rule)=2..2  page(incl-rule)=2..2\n   68|    When #F > 0, the standard self-reduction finds a satisfying assignment by fixing one variable at\n   69| a time and checking that the remaining count is positive. Choosing each bit according to the two\n   70| conditional counts instead gives an exactly uniform random satisfying assignment, with polyk (n, m)\n   71| expected overhead [JVV86, Theorem 3.3(1)].\n==================== lines 77-81: page(before-rule)=2..2  page(incl-rule)=2..2\n   77| Corollary 1.2. For every fixed k ⩾ 2 and ε > 0, there exists a randomized algorithm that, given a\n   78| k-CNF F on n ⩾ 20 variables with m clauses, finds an assignment satisfying at least OPT(F ) − εm\n   79| clauses with probability 1 − 2−Ω(n) , in\n   80|                                         (n + m)Ok,ε (1) 2Ok,ε (ρ(n))\n   81| time and (n + m)Ok,ε (1) space.\n==================== lines 82-94: page(before-rule)=2..2  page(incl-rule)=2..2\n   82| Proof. We may assume m > 0. Let f (x) be the fraction of clauses satisfied by x, and let fb(x) be\n   83| that fraction in a sample of O(n/ε2 ) clauses chosen independently and uniformly with replacement.\n   84| Hoeffding’s inequality and a union bound over all 2n assignments give, with probability 1 − 2−Ω(n) ,\n   85|                            |fb(x) − f (x)| ⩽ ε/2    for every x ∈ {0, 1}n .\n   86| Thus, if x                       x) ⩾ maxx f (x) − ε. Given an integer threshold b, a Boolean circuit\n   87|          b maximizes fb, then f (b\n   88| with at most two inputs per gate and size Ok,ε (n) tests whether an assignment satisfies at least b\n   89| sampled clauses. The standard circuit-to-3-CNF reduction [Bar23, Lemmas 15.9–15.10] produces\n   90| a formula with Ok,ε (n) variables and clauses that is satisfiable exactly when this threshold can be\n   91| attained. Theorem 1.1, binary search on the threshold, and self-reduction then find an exact sample\n   92| optimizer within the stated bounds. Truncating rejection sampling after O(n + log(n/ε2 )) trials\n   93| per sample gives worst-case poly(n, m, ε−1 ) sampling time and adds at most 2−Ω(n) to the failure\n   94| probability.                                                                                       □\n==================== lines 95-99: page(before-rule)=2..2  page(incl-rule)=2..2\n   95|    Note that runtime bounds of the form 2o(n) do not transfer under general polynomial-time\n   96|               o(1)\n   97| reductions (2n     would be needed for this), so our result does not imply that general NP problems\n   98| admit subexponential time algorithms. However we do obtain subexponential time algorithms for\n   99| counting graph colorings and perfect matchings.\n==================== lines 100-103: page(before-rule)=2..2  page(incl-rule)=2..2\n  100| Corollary 1.3. On simple graphs with v vertices and e edges, the number of independent sets and,\n  101| for each fixed q ⩾ 2, the number of proper q-colorings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v) time\n  102| and polyq (v, e) space. The number of perfect matchings can be computed deterministically in 2o(v+e)\n  103| time and poly(v, e) space.\n==================== lines 107-120: page(before-rule)=3..3  page(incl-rule)=3..3\n  107| Proof. For G = (V, E), introduce a Boolean variable xu for each u ∈ V . Satisfying assignments of\n  108|                                         ^\n  109|                                              (¬xu ∨ ¬xw )\n  110|                                         {u,w}∈E\n  111| \n  112| correspond bijectively to the independent sets {u ∈ V : xu = 1}. For proper q-colorings, use one\n  113| Boolean variable per vertex and color, require exactly one color at each vertex, and forbid equal\n  114| colors at adjacent vertices. This gives a counting-preserving q-CNF encoding with qv variables and\n  115| Oq (v + e) clauses.\n  116|    For perfect matchings, use one Boolean variable per edge and a linear-size circuit checking that\n  117| exactly one selected edge meets each vertex. The standard circuit-to-3-CNF reduction [Bar23,\n  118| Lemmas 15.9–15.10] gives O(v + e) variables and clauses. The gate variables have unique values\n  119| once the edge bits are fixed, so this encoding also preserves the count. Theorem 1.1 gives the stated\n  120| time and space bounds.                                                                             □\n==================== lines 121-127: page(before-rule)=3..3  page(incl-rule)=3..3\n  121|   The fixed-density case of Theorem 1.1 gives a circuit lower bound for ENP , the class of languages\n  122| decidable in deterministic time 2O(n) with an NP oracle.\n  123| Corollary 1.4. Let CL (n) be the minimum number of noninput gates in a circuit computing L on\n  124| {0, 1}n , allowing arbitrary Boolean gates of fan-in at most two and unrestricted fan-out. There exist\n  125| a language L ∈ ENP and a constant c > 0 such that\n  126|                                          p\n  127|                              CL (n) > c n log n      for infinitely many n.                      (1.2)\n==================== lines 153-153: page(before-rule)=3..3  page(incl-rule)=3..3\n  153| Experiments on formulas from circuit synthesis and diagnosis demonstrated the practical value of\n==================== lines 165-171: page(before-rule)=4..4  page(incl-rule)=4..4\n  165|    The Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH) asserted that 3-SAT requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time\n  166| in the worst case, and hence so does k-SAT for every fixed k ⩾ 3 [IP01, Theorem 1]. The Strong\n  167| Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH) made the sharper prediction that k-SAT requires 2n(1−ok (1))\n  168| time as k grows. Precisely, for every ε ∈ (0, 1), there is a fixed k for which no deterministic algorithm\n  169| solves k-SAT in 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) time. The running-time bounds for Schöning’s algorithm and\n  170| PPSZ helped motivate this prediction: their improvements over exhaustive search diminish as k\n  171| grows [IP01, Open Problems, item 2].\n==================== lines 172-176: page(before-rule)=4..4  page(incl-rule)=4..4\n  172|    The Gap Exponential Time Hypothesis (Gap-ETH) strengthens ETH by postulating constants\n  173| δ, γ > 0 for which no O(2γn )-time algorithm can distinguish a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which\n  174| every assignment violates at least a δ fraction of the clauses, even when m = O(n) [Din16, MR17].\n  175| The randomized version rules out an algorithm with the same running time that distinguishes\n  176| the two cases with probability at least 2/3 on every promised input [CCK+ 20, Conjecture 2.5].\n==================== lines 181-183: page(before-rule)=4..4  page(incl-rule)=4..4\n  181|    The Nondeterministic Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every ε > 0,\n  182| some fixed k admits no nondeterministic 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) -time algorithm for deciding whether\n  183| a k-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a k-DNF is a tautology [CGI+ 16]. NSETH has\n==================== lines 187-194: page(before-rule)=4..4  page(incl-rule)=4..4\n  187|    Theorem 1.1 refutes ETH, SETH, Gap-ETH, and NSETH. Indeed, testing whether the exact count\n  188| is positive or zero decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability in deterministic 2o(n) (n+m)Ok (1) time\n  189| for every fixed k; deciding satisfiability also solves the Gap-ETH promise problem. The deterministic\n  190| algorithm also refutes the bounded-error randomized versions of ETH [DHM+ 14, Section 1] and\n  191| SETH [CDL+ 16, Definition 2.1], and of Gap-ETH. It does not conflict with unconditional exponential\n  192| lower bounds for restricted proof systems such as regular resolution [BI13]. In 2016, Ryan Williams\n  193| refuted the Merlin–Arthur version of SETH (MA-SETH) by giving a protocol that certifies k-CNF\n  194| unsatisfiability in 2n/2 poly(n, m) time [Wil16, Theorem 3.3].\n==================== lines 204-205: page(before-rule)=4..4  page(incl-rule)=4..4\n  204|    The counting Exponential Time Hypothesis (#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\n  205| assignments of a 3-CNF requires 2Ω(n) deterministic time [DHM+ 14, Section 1]. Under #ETH,\n==================== lines 211-212: page(before-rule)=5..5  page(incl-rule)=5..5\n  211| in Corollary 1.3 applies in particular to these graphs. Our algorithm (Theorem 1.1) solves #3-SAT\n  212| deterministically in 2o(n) poly(n, m) time and therefore refutes #ETH.\n==================== lines 213-227: page(before-rule)=5..5  page(incl-rule)=5..5\n  213| 1.1.3. Implications for fine-grained complexity. ETH and SETH became standard assumptions for\n  214| parameterized and fine-grained complexity [IPZ01, VW19]. ETH-based reductions yielded lower\n  215| bounds for the dependence on solution size in Clique [CHKX06], and on treewidth in constraint\n  216| satisfaction and partitioned subgraph isomorphism [Mar10]. SETH supported sharp exponential\n  217| bounds for Hitting Set and Set Splitting [CDL+ 16], and for Independent Set and Dominating Set\n  218| parameterized by treewidth [LMS18]. It also gave bounds on possible speedups of pseudopolynomial\n  219| algorithms for Subset Sum and Bicriteria Path [ABHS19].\n  220|    For problems in P, SETH was used to rule out polynomial-factor speedups over quadratic running\n  221| times for edit distance [BI18], longest common subsequence and dynamic time warping [BK15],\n  222| Fréchet distance [Bri14], and regular-expression matching [BI16]. Other applications concerned\n  223| approximating graph diameter [RV13] and maintaining reachability counts or the size of the largest\n  224| strongly connected component under graph updates [AW14]. Together these results made SETH a\n  225| common basis for explaining the apparent optimality of algorithms across quite different problems.\n  226|    Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness claims above. Some\n  227| of the same lower bounds follow from weaker hypotheses that may still hold. For example, the\n==================== lines 229-243: page(before-rule)=5..5  page(incl-rule)=5..5\n  229| Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture (OVC) [BK15, BI16]. OVC asserts that for every fixed ε > 0, no\n  230| O(N 2−ε poly(d))-time algorithm can decide whether two sets of N Boolean vectors of dimension\n  231| d contain an orthogonal pair, one vector from each set. The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal\n  232| Vectors shows that SETH implies OVC [Wil05, VW19]. The lower bounds for Fréchet distance,\n  233| graph diameter approximation, and the treewidth dependence of Independent Set and Dominating\n  234| Set also follow from the hypothesis that unrestricted CNF-SAT admits no 2(1−δ)n poly(n, m)-time\n  235| algorithm for any constant δ > 0 [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem 1.1 gives a running-time guarantee","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"a29ed36e-8fed-4225-b898-f94c9412900e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:54:48.929Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012sixTbEGv8RYMa27Cvq2Vi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter pass over paper 3 PDF"}
