{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SqqqZnRepororBFibCjqV1","type":"tool_result","content":"packages dir count:\n42\n===== comparator README selected lines\n     1\t# Comparator\n     2\t## Deprecation\n     3\tThis project has been merged into core as of Lean release v4.35.0 in the form of `lake comparator`\n     4\tand the accompanying `lake check`. Users are advised to migrate their validations setups to these\n     5\ttools as they are the only ones guaranteed to receive further security and performance improvements.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Introduction\n     8\tComparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs. It relies on having an existing Lean installation as\n     9\twell as:\n    10\t1. [`landrun`](https://github.com/Zouuup/landrun), compiled from the `main` branch's source, present in `PATH`\n    11\t2. [`lean4export`](https://github.com/leanprover/lean4export/), at a version that is compatible with whatever Lean version your project is targeting, present in `PATH`\n    12\t3. (optional) [nanoda](https://github.com/ammkrn/nanoda_lib/), compiled with a recent version of Rust.\n    25\t    \"solution_module\": \"Solution\",\n    26\t    \"theorem_names\": [\"todo1\"],\n    27\t    \"permitted_axioms\": [\"propext\", \"Quot.sound\", \"Classical.choice\"]\n    28\t}\n    29\t```\n    30\tWhere `Challenge.lean` contains at least a theorem named `todo1` that has a `sorry` (or any other proof)\n    31\tand `Solution.lean` is provided by a party trying to convince you that they have proven `todo1` by\n    32\twriting out the same theorem but with a proper proof attached.\n    33\t\n    34\tGiven the following assumptions:\n    35\t1. The transitive closure of imports of `Challenge.lean` as well as `lakefile.toml`/`lakefile.lean`\n    36\t   are controlled by you or trustworthy.\n    37\t2. You have not previously tried to compile the `Solution` file or any other potentially adversarial\n    38\t   files (as that might compromise your `Challenge` file to make it seem like you are looking for a\n    39\t   different proof than you actually are)\n    40\t3. You have the `landrun` and `lean4export` binary in `PATH`\n    41\t4. `landrun` works correctly on your system and `Solution.lean` does not\n    42\t   exploit any bugs in `landrun` that allow a process to escape its sandbox\n    43\t5. The Lean kernel is correct (with `external_kernels` this can be reduced to\n    44\t   \"At least one of the Lean kernel or the `external_kernels` is correct\")\n    45\t6. You are not running this under a privileged user\n    46\t\n    47\tIf the following command succeeds:\n    48\t```\n    49\tsystemd-run --property=RestrictAddressFamilies=~AF_UNIX --user --pty -E PATH=\"$PATH\" --working-directory $(pwd) -- bash -c 'lake env path/to/comparator/binary path/to/config.json'\n    50\t```\n    51\t\n    52\tAll theorems in `Solution` that are listed in `theorem_names` are guaranteed to:\n    53\t1. Prove the same statement as provided in `Challenge`\n    54\t2. Use no more axioms than listed in `permitted_axioms`\n    55\t3. Be accepted by the Lean kernel\n    56\t\n    57\t> [!NOTE]\n    58\t> The Trusted Code Base of Landrun naturally includes the operating system and hardware it is running on, plus its sandboxing mechanism.\n    59\t> The systemd-run part explicitly guard against a vulnerability in landrun, Comparator's current sandboxing solution, that will be fixed in Linux 7.1\n    60\t\n    68\t\n    69\t## Checking with Additional Kernels\n    70\tComparator can additionally check solutions with external kernels. To do this you must register them\n    71\tin the `external_kernels` list:\n    72\t```\n    84\tfile, containing the solution export to the kernel, in this case: `kernel_bin --threads=4 --paranoid export.ndjson`\n    85\t\n    86\tFor backwards compatibility reasons users may instead set `enable_nanoda: true` to obtain a config\n    87\tthat calls `nanoda_bin`. Furthermore, comparator currently attempts to detect `nanoda`-style kernels\n    88\tby checking whether the name contains the string `noda` and instead passing a `nanoda`-style\n    89\t`config.json` to them. This is only intended as a migration path while the kernel ecosystem\n    90\tmoves toward having an option to receive the input file as a `CLI` argument.\n   182\tspace and then dereferenced and are as such a potential point of attack for sophisticated adversaries.\n   183\t\n   184\tThe comparator performs the following steps to ensure these properties:\n   185\t1. Build `Challenge` using `lake` in a `landrun` sandbox that has:\n   186\t   - read access to the entire file system and write access to `/dev`\n   187\t   - write access to the `.lake` directory of the project\n   188\t2. Run `lean4export` on the produced `Challenge.olean` in a `landrun` sandbox that has:\n   189\t   - read access to the entire file system and write access to `/dev`\n   190\t3. Repeat the same build sandboxed and export sandboxed steps with `Solution`\n   191\t4. Verify that all declarations used in the statement of all relevant theorems in `Challenge`\n   192\t   are the same as in the `Solution` environment.\n   193\t   This always includes the declarations from `Init` with special meaning to the kernel. Both `Challenge`\n   194\t   and `Solution` therefore need to import the default prelude.\n   195\t5. Verify that the body of all relevant theorems in the `Solution` environment only uses axioms\n   196\t   listed in `permitted_axioms`\n   197\t6. Replay the `Solution` environment into the Lean kernel. Doing this within the same process as the\n   198\t   comparator should be safe as the worst thing that can happen at this point is an exploit that\n   199\t   makes the kernel accept when it should reject and that same exploit should also be applicable\n   200\t   from within an external process.\n   201\t\n   202\tNote that as `Challenge` is trusted, both the sandbox and lean4export step for `Challenge` are not\n   203\tnecessary to the best of our knowledge. We still adopt these rather free measures as additional\n   204\tparanoia in case an adversary comes up with a means of attack anyway.\n   205\t\n   206\t## Acknowledgement\n   207\tComparator was originally developed by Lean FRO, with feedback from the AIMO team, in support of the\n   208\tAIMO series of competitions and their goal of enabling trustworthy LLM Lean evaluation on Kaggle.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"55424952-3198-4d11-97a1-eabaf01aa3a3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:56:46.487Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SqqqZnRepororBFibCjqV1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter pass over paper 3 PDF"}
