{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014WUNjjH5xgxKieFB6KdEQ3","type":"tool_result","content":"AINTLIB\nAbsorptionCutoff\nBibtexQuery\nClassFieldTheory\nCli\nLeanArchitect\nLeanSearchClient\nMD4Lean\nPrimeCert\nPrimeNumberTheoremAnd\nQq\nSphereEversion\nStrongPNT\nTauCeti\nUnicodeBasic\nZeta3Irrational\naesop\nbatteries\nbelyi\ncarleson\ncheckdecls\ndoc-gen4\nelliptic-curves\nfixed-point-theorems\nformal-schemes\ngenl\ngromov\nheights\nimportGraph\niut\nleancert\nleansqlite\nmathlib\noka\norbicurve-cores\npi1\nplausible\nproofwidgets\nrellich-kondrachov\nschoenflies-lean\ntate-curves-theta\ntempered-fundamental-groups\n===== nanoda README\n     1\t# About\n     2\t\n     3\tThis is an external type checker for the [Lean 4](https://lean-lang.org/) programming language and theorem prover. You can read more about what an external type checker is and why you might want to use one in [this book](https://ammk\n     4\t\n     5\t# Usage\n     6\t\n     7\t## Building and running the binary\n     8\t\n     9\tEither run the binary directly through cargo `cargo run --release -- <path_to_config>`, or build the binary using `cargo build --release`, then run the built binary, passing a path to a [configuration](#configuration-file). Building \n    10\t\n    11\tThe binary takes a single argument, which is a path to a json configuration file describing the resource locations and desired options needed to run the type checker. Export files can be streamed via stdin rather than by file path by\n    12\t\n    13\tExport files can be created with [lean4export](https://github.com/leanprover/lean4export).\n    14\t\n    15\t## Using the library\n    16\t\n    17\tThe library component can be imported and used as a normal rust crate by specifying it as a dependency in a `Cargo.toml` file.\n    18\t\n    19\t# Configuration file\n    20\t\n    21\tThe execution conditions of `nanoda_bin` are determined by a json configuration file, there is no old school CLI. This was a conscious design choice to accommodate modern development practices (checking in to version control, use wit\n    22\t\n    23\tGiven that lean4export already uses json as its export format, adding a json parser as a dependency doesn't increase the overall complexity of the project.\n    24\t\n    25\t## Configuration options\n    26\t\n    27\tUsers can either specify a path to the export file to be checked, or set `\"use_stdin\": true`, in which case the program will wait for the export file to be passed via stdin. If the `\"export_file_path\"` key is not defined, then `\"use_\n    28\t\n    29\t`\"num_threads\"` sets the number of checking workers. If omitted or set to 0 or 1, checking runs serially in one worker.\n    30\t\n    31\t`\"stack_size_bytes\"` sets the requested stack size in bytes for each checking worker, including serial checking. It must be a positive integer and defaults to 32 mb. For example, `\"stack_size_bytes\": 1073741824` requests 1 GB per wor\n    32\t\n    33\tThe `\"permitted_axioms\"` list is where users specify the axioms an export file is permitted to use. For axioms to be permitted, they must be identified in the config file. The average Lean user will want to permit the three semi-offi\n    34\t\n    35\tif `\"unpermitted_axiom_hard_error\"` is set to `true`, the presence of an axiom in the export file that is not in the `permitted_axioms` list will cause a hard error and abort checking, regardless of whether it's used by any later dec\n    36\t\n    37\tIf `\"unsafe_permit_all_axioms\"` is set to `true`, all axioms will be admitted to the environment (when using this option, you must also explicitly set `\"unpermitted_axiom_hard_error\": false` to avoid a hard error on startup). This is\n    38\t\n    39\t`\"nat_extension\"` and `\"string_extension\"` enable or disable the Nat and String kernel extensions. While we expect most users to opt into the Nat and String kernel extensions, they are disabled by default.\n    40\t\n    41\t`\"pp_declars\"` is a list of declarations to be printed back by the pretty printer.\n    42\t\n    43\t`\"pp_options\"` are the options to be used by the pretty printer.\n    44\t\n    45\t`\"pp_to_stdout\"` determines whether the pretty printer output is written to stdout. This is not mutually exclusive with `\"pp_output_path\"`; if both options are set, the pretty printer output will be written to both stdout and the spe","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"092b1599-8e57-4014-8ea5-9462a5b575c8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:56:56.248Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014WUNjjH5xgxKieFB6KdEQ3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter pass over paper 3 PDF"}
