{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FfeHoNq6rfHKZ8Sok23HTM","type":"tool_result","content":"===== Frame.lean\n     1\t/-\n     2\tCopyright (c) 2026 Alexandre Linhares. All rights reserved.\n     3\tReleased under the MIT license as described in the file LICENSE.\n     4\t\n     5\t# The formal frame of loop0014: `SubexpCount`, `PathwidthSubexp`, `PathwidthSubexpOn`\n     6\t\n     7\tItem 02 of `reports/subexp_pathwidth_plan.md` (§0.4.1, §0.5); `reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §2.\n     8\tEverything the loop proves about a subexponential exact algorithm for pathwidth is stated\n     9\tagainst the three `Prop`s of this file:\n    10\t\n    11\t* `SubexpCount k` — the comparator statement `exact_count_and_sat k` of OpenAI's family 378\n    12\t  (*The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, 2026-10-25), **restated as a `def` of a `Prop`,\n    13\t  never proved and never an axiom**: one fixed primitive multi-tape machine exactly counts the\n    14\t  satisfying assignments of every CNF of width `≤ k` on `n` variables, in the collection's\n    15\t  canonical encoding of bit length `L`, in time `≤ C (L + 1)^d · 2^(A · ρ(n))` with\n    16\t  `ρ(n) = n (log log(n + 16))² / √log(n + 16)`, visiting at most `S (L + 1)^e` tape cells at\n    17\t  every step. The body is the comparator's statement character for character (the binder\n    18\t  `hk : 2 ≤ k` becomes the hypothesis of `ExactCountAndSat`); the machine model, the encoding\n    19\t  and `runtimeBound` are the verbatim copy in `PrimitiveMachine.lean`. This is the *verified*\n    20\t  exponent, the weaker one (`reports/subexp_pathwidth.md` §1.0): the paper's\n    21\t  `n log log n / √log n` and `n / √log n` are not in the Lean library and are not restated.\n    22\t* `PathwidthSubexpOn C` — \"some primitive machine decides `pathwidth G ≤ k` for every graph\n    23\t  `G` in the class `C` and every `k`, in time `2^{o(n)} · poly(L)`\": a machine, constants\n    24\t  `c > 0`, `d`, and an exponent `f = o(n)` (`Asymptotics.IsLittleO`, so `2^(f n)` is\n    25\t  `2^{o(n)}` in the number of vertices), such that on the canonical input `encodeGraph G k`\n    26\t  (`n` and `k` in the collection's unary code, then the `n²` adjacency bits) the machine halts\n    27\t  in the answer state `decide (pathwidth G ≤ k)` within `c (L + 1)^d 2^(f n)` steps. A class is\n    28\t  `GraphClass := ∀ n, SimpleGraph (Fin n) → Prop` (the item's `SimpleGraph → Prop` is not a\n    29\t  type; nothing requires the class to be closed under isomorphism).\n    30\t* `PathwidthSubexp` — the same over every graph.\n    31\t\n    32\tThe parameter is the number of vertices `n`, never the input length (`TASK.md`: on dense\n    33\tgraphs `L = Θ(n²)`), which is why `f` is `o(n)` and the polynomial factor is in `L`.\n    34\t\n    35\tThe machine model is the one the counting theorem is stated in, so a transfer theorem\n    36\t`SubexpCount k → PathwidthSubexpOn C` (item 09, if any) is a statement about one machine\n    37\tmodel and needs no translation between models; the price, recorded in the report, is that\n    38\tsuch a theorem needs a composition fact about primitive machines (a polynomial-time encoder\n    39\tfollowed by the counting machine), which the comparator does not state and which would be\n    40\tcarried as a further named `Prop`. An abstract notion (\"a function with a time function\")\n    41\twas rejected because without a cost model `∃ f, ∃ T, …` is provable outright and the\n    42\thypothesis would be vacuous.\n    43\t\n    44\tProved here:\n    45\t\n    46\t* `SubexpCount.anti`: `SubexpCount k → k' ≤ k → SubexpCount k'` (a width-`≤ k'` formula has\n    47\t  width `≤ k`; the same machine and constants).\n    48\t* `PathwidthSubexpOn.mono`: a machine for a class is a machine for every subclass.\n    49\t* `pathwidthSubexp_iff`: `PathwidthSubexp ↔ PathwidthSubexpOn (fun _ _ => True)`.\n    50\t* `pathwidthSubexpOn_iff_vertexSeparation`: the same `Prop` with `vertexSeparation` in place\n    51\t  of `pathwidth` (Kinnersley, `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`), for item 08, whose statements\n    52\t  are against `VertexSeparation.lean`.\n    53\t* `Trace.config_of_locatedStep`: a trace of the located (space-monitoring) step projects to a\n    54\t  trace of the plain step, so the time bound of `SubexpCount` is a bound on `machine.step`.\n    55\t* `runtimeScale_isLittleO`, `isSubexpExponent_runtimeScale`: the verified exponent\n    56\t  `A · ρ(n)` is `o(n)`, so `runtimeBound C A d L n` is of the form `PathwidthSubexpOn` asks\n    57\t  for (`runtimeBound_eq`). The analytic content is `(log y)² / √y → 0`.\n    58\t\n    59\tEvery statement here is a definition or a trivial lemma; nothing was brute-force checked\n    60\tbecause nothing is a reduction or an encoding of pathwidth (TASK.md's check applies to those;\n    61\t`encodeGraph_length` is the one computation, proved). What this file does **not** claim:\n    62\tthat `SubexpCount k` holds for any `k`; that any `PathwidthSubexpOn C` holds; that the\n    63\t`PrimitiveMachine` model is robust (polynomially equivalent to any other); or that the\n    64\ttheorem of the paper beyond the comparator statement is restated.\n    65\t-/\n    66\timport MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth.PrimitiveMachine\n    67\timport MOSPFormalization.VSEquivPW\n    68\t\n    69\tset_option linter.unusedSectionVars false\n    70\t\n    71\tnamespace MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth\n    72\t\n    73\topen OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT\n    74\topen OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Computation\n    75\topen OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace\n    76\topen OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLog.Statement\n    77\topen Asymptotics Filter\n    78\t\n    79\t/-! ### The hypothesis: the comparator statement as a `Prop` -/\n    80\t\n    81\t/-- **The counting hypothesis, for width `k`**: the body of `exact_count_and_sat k` in\n    82\t`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean` of the OpenAI collection, character for\n    83\tcharacter. Never proved here, never an axiom. -/\n    84\tdef SubexpCount (k : ℕ) : Prop :=\n    85\t  ∃ (machine : Computation.PrimitiveMachine)\n    86\t    (countTape answerTape : Fin machine.tapeCount)\n    87\t    (C A S : ℝ) (d e : ℕ),\n    88\t    0 < C ∧ 0 < A ∧ 0 < S ∧ 0 < d ∧ 0 < e ∧\n    89\t    ∀ (n : ℕ) (F : SAT.CNF (Fin n)), SAT.WidthAtMost k F →\n    90\t      ∃ (time : ℕ) (final : LocatedCfg machine),\n    91\t        Computation.Trace (locatedStep machine) time\n    92\t          (locatedInit machine (Encoding.encodeCNF F)) final ∧\n    93\t        final.config.tapes countTape =\n    94\t          Turing.Tape.mk₁ ((countWord (SAT.count F)).map some) ∧\n    95\t        final.config.tapes answerTape =\n    96\t          Turing.Tape.mk₁ [some (decide (0 < SAT.count F))] ∧\n    97\t        final.config.state = Sum.inl (decide (0 < SAT.count F)) ∧\n    98\t        BinaryArithmetic.toNat (countWord (SAT.count F)) = SAT.count F ∧\n    99\t        (countWord (SAT.count F)).length ≤ n + 1 ∧\n   100\t        (time : ℝ) ≤ OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLog.Statement.runtimeBound C A d\n   101\t          (Encoding.formulaBitLength F) n ∧\n   102\t        ∀ (prefixSteps : ℕ) (intermediate : LocatedCfg machine),\n   103\t          prefixSteps ≤ time →\n   104\t          Computation.Trace (locatedStep machine) prefixSteps\n   105\t            (locatedInit machine (Encoding.encodeCNF F)) intermediate →\n   106\t          (visitedSpace intermediate : ℝ) ≤\n   107\t            S * ((Encoding.formulaBitLength F : ℝ) + 1) ^ e\n   108\t\n   109\t/-- The comparator theorem in full: `SubexpCount k` for every `k ≥ 2`. A `Prop`, not asserted. -/\n   110\tdef ExactCountAndSat : Prop := ∀ k : ℕ, 2 ≤ k → SubexpCount k\n   111\t\n   112\t/-- A machine for width `k` is a machine for every smaller width: `WidthAtMost k' F → WidthAtMost k F`. -/\n   113\ttheorem SubexpCount.anti {k k' : ℕ} (h : SubexpCount k) (hk : k' ≤ k) : SubexpCount k' := by\n   114\t  obtain ⟨machine, countTape, answerTape, C, A, S, d, e, hC, hA, hS, hd, he, hmain⟩ := h\n   115\t  refine ⟨machine, countTape, answerTape, C, A, S, d, e, hC, hA, hS, hd, he, ?_⟩\n   116\t  intro n F hF\n   117\t  exact hmain n F (fun c hc => le_trans (hF c hc) hk)\n   118\t\n   119\ttheorem ExactCountAndSat.subexpCount (h : ExactCountAndSat) {k : ℕ} (hk : 2 ≤ k) :\n   120\t    SubexpCount k := h k hk\n   121\t\n   122\t/-! ### The runtime bound is `2^{o(n)} · poly(L)` -/\n   123\t\n   124\t/-- An exponent `f` with `2^(f n) = 2^{o(n)}`: `f = o(n)` as `n → ∞`. -/\n   125\tdef IsSubexpExponent (f : ℕ → ℝ) : Prop := f =o[atTop] (fun n : ℕ => (n : ℝ))\n   126\t\n   127\t/-- `runtimeBound C A d L n = C (L + 1)^d 2^(A · runtimeScale n)`, by definition. -/\n   128\ttheorem runtimeBound_eq (C A : ℝ) (d L n : ℕ) :\n   129\t    runtimeBound C A d L n = C * ((L : ℝ) + 1) ^ d * (2 : ℝ) ^ (A * runtimeScale n) := rfl\n   130\t\n   131\t/-- `(log y)² / √y → 0` as `y → ∞`. -/\n   132\ttheorem tendsto_log_sq_div_sqrt :\n   133\t    Tendsto (fun y : ℝ => (Real.log y) ^ 2 / Real.sqrt y) atTop (nhds 0) := by\n   134\t  have h := isLittleO_log_rpow_rpow_atTop (2 : ℝ) (by norm_num : (0 : ℝ) < 1 / 2)\n   135\t  have h' : (fun y : ℝ => (Real.log y) ^ 2) =o[atTop] (fun y : ℝ => Real.sqrt y) := by\n   136\t    refine h.congr' ?_ ?_\n   137\t    · filter_upwards with y\n   138\t      exact Real.rpow_two _\n   139\t    · filter_upwards [eventually_ge_atTop (0 : ℝ)] with y hy\n   140\t      rw [Real.sqrt_eq_rpow]\n   141\t  exact h'.tendsto_div_nhds_zero\n   142\t\n   143\t/-- The verified runtime scale `ρ(n) = n (log log(n + 16))² / √log(n + 16)` is `o(n)`. -/\n   144\ttheorem runtimeScale_isLittleO :\n   145\t    (fun n : ℕ => runtimeScale n) =o[atTop] (fun n : ℕ => (n : ℝ)) := by\n   146\t  have hlog : Tendsto (fun n : ℕ => Real.log ((n : ℝ) + 16)) atTop atTop :=\n   147\t    Real.tendsto_log_atTop.comp (tendsto_atTop_add_const_right _ _ tendsto_natCast_atTop_atTop)\n   148\t  have hq : Tendsto (fun n : ℕ => (Real.log (Real.log ((n : ℝ) + 16))) ^ 2 /\n   149\t      Real.sqrt (Real.log ((n : ℝ) + 16))) atTop (nhds 0) :=\n   150\t    tendsto_log_sq_div_sqrt.comp hlog\n   151\t  have h1 : (fun n : ℕ => (Real.log (Real.log ((n : ℝ) + 16))) ^ 2 /\n   152\t      Real.sqrt (Real.log ((n : ℝ) + 16))) =o[atTop] (fun _ : ℕ => (1 : ℝ)) :=\n   153\t    (isLittleO_one_iff ℝ).mpr hq\n   154\t  have h2 := h1.mul_isBigO (isBigO_refl (fun n : ℕ => (n : ℝ)) atTop)\n   155\t  refine h2.congr' ?_ ?_\n   156\t  · filter_upwards with n\n   157\t    simp only [runtimeScale]\n   158\t    ring\n   159\t  · filter_upwards with n\n   160\t    simp\n   161\t\n   162\t/-- The verified exponent `A · ρ(n)` is a subexponential exponent, for every `A`. -/\n   163\ttheorem isSubexpExponent_runtimeScale (A : ℝ) :\n   164\t    IsSubexpExponent (fun n : ℕ => A * runtimeScale n) :=\n   165\t  (runtimeScale_isLittleO.const_mul_left A)\n   166\t\n   167\t/-! ### Traces of the located step project to traces of the plain step -/\n   168\t\n   169\ttheorem LocatedCfg.monitor_config {machine : PrimitiveMachine} (c : LocatedCfg machine)\n   170\t    (x : machine.Cfg) : (c.monitor x).config = x := by\n   171\t  unfold LocatedCfg.monitor\n   172\t  rcases hs : c.config.state with b | q\n   173\t  · rfl\n   174\t  · simp only [Sum.elim_inr]\n   175\t    rcases machine.transition q (c.config.tapes (machine.scan q)).head with b' | ⟨q', a⟩\n   176\t    · rfl\n   177\t    · rfl\n   178\t\n   179\t/-- A trace of the located step is a trace of the plain step on the configurations. -/\n   180\ttheorem Trace.config_of_locatedStep {machine : PrimitiveMachine} {t : ℕ}\n   181\t    {c c' : LocatedCfg machine} (h : Trace (locatedStep machine) t c c') :\n   182\t    Trace machine.step t c.config c'.config := by\n   183\t  induction h with\n   184\t  | refl a => exact Trace.refl _\n   185\t  | cons hs _ ih =>\n   186\t    rename_i a b c''\n   187\t    simp only [locatedStep, Option.map_eq_some_iff] at hs\n   188\t    obtain ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩ := hs\n   189\t    rw [LocatedCfg.monitor_config] at ih\n   190\t    exact Trace.cons hx ih\n   191\t\n   192\t/-! ### The canonical input of a pathwidth decision instance -/\n   193\t\n   194\t/-- The canonical input of the instance `(G, k)`, `G` a graph on `Fin n`: `n` in the\n   195\tcollection's unary code (`encodeNat`), then `k`, then the `n²` adjacency bits row by row. -/\n   196\tnoncomputable def encodeGraph {n : ℕ} (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (k : ℕ) : List Bool :=\n   197\t  open Classical in\n   198\t  Encoding.encodeNat n ++ Encoding.encodeNat k ++\n   199\t    (List.finRange n).flatMap (fun i => (List.finRange n).map (fun j => decide (G.Adj i j)))\n   200\t\n   201\ttheorem encodeGraph_length {n : ℕ} (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (k : ℕ) :\n   202\t    (encodeGraph G k).length = (n + 1) + (k + 1) + n * n := by\n   203\t  simp [encodeGraph, Encoding.encodeNat, List.length_flatMap, List.map_const']\n   204\t  ring\n   205\t\n   206\t/-! ### The subexponential-pathwidth predicates -/\n   207\t\n   208\t/-- A class of graphs, one predicate per vertex count. -/\n   209\tabbrev GraphClass := ∀ n : ℕ, SimpleGraph (Fin n) → Prop\n   210\t\n   211\t/-- `PathwidthSubexpOn C`: some primitive machine decides `pathwidth G ≤ k` for every `G` in\n   212\tthe class `C` and every `k`, on the canonical input, in time `c (L + 1)^d 2^(f n)` with\n   213\t`f = o(n)` in the number of vertices `n`. -/\n   214\tdef PathwidthSubexpOn (C : GraphClass) : Prop :=\n   215\t  ∃ (machine : PrimitiveMachine) (c : ℝ) (d : ℕ) (f : ℕ → ℝ),\n   216\t    0 < c ∧ IsSubexpExponent f ∧\n   217\t    ∀ (n : ℕ) (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (k : ℕ), C n G →\n   218\t      ∃ (time : ℕ) (final : machine.Cfg),\n   219\t        Trace machine.step time (machine.init (encodeGraph G k)) final ∧\n   220\t        final.state = Sum.inl (@decide (pathwidth G ≤ k) (Classical.propDecidable _)) ∧\n   221\t        (time : ℝ) ≤ c * (((encodeGraph G k).length : ℝ) + 1) ^ d * (2 : ℝ) ^ (f n)\n   222\t\n   223\t/-- `PathwidthSubexp`: the same over every graph. -/\n   224\tdef PathwidthSubexp : Prop :=\n   225\t  ∃ (machine : PrimitiveMachine) (c : ℝ) (d : ℕ) (f : ℕ → ℝ),\n   226\t    0 < c ∧ IsSubexpExponent f ∧\n   227\t    ∀ (n : ℕ) (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (k : ℕ),\n   228\t      ∃ (time : ℕ) (final : machine.Cfg),\n   229\t        Trace machine.step time (machine.init (encodeGraph G k)) final ∧\n   230\t        final.state = Sum.inl (@decide (pathwidth G ≤ k) (Classical.propDecidable _)) ∧\n   231\t        (time : ℝ) ≤ c * (((encodeGraph G k).length : ℝ) + 1) ^ d * (2 : ℝ) ^ (f n)\n   232\t\n   233\t/-- Monotonicity in the class: a machine for `C` is a machine for every `C' ⊆ C`. -/\n   234\ttheorem PathwidthSubexpOn.mono {C C' : GraphClass} (h : PathwidthSubexpOn C)\n   235\t    (hsub : ∀ n G, C' n G → C n G) : PathwidthSubexpOn C' := by\n   236\t  obtain ⟨machine, c, d, f, hc, hf, hmain⟩ := h\n   237\t  exact ⟨machine, c, d, f, hc, hf, fun n G k hG => hmain n G k (hsub n G hG)⟩\n   238\t\n   239\t/-- `PathwidthSubexp` is the class-restricted notion on the class of all graphs. -/\n   240\ttheorem pathwidthSubexp_iff : PathwidthSubexp ↔ PathwidthSubexpOn (fun _ _ => True) := by\n   241\t  constructor\n   242\t  · rintro ⟨machine, c, d, f, hc, hf, hmain⟩\n   243\t    exact ⟨machine, c, d, f, hc, hf, fun n G k _ => hmain n G k⟩\n   244\t  · rintro ⟨machine, c, d, f, hc, hf, hmain⟩\n   245\t    exact ⟨machine, c, d, f, hc, hf, fun n G k => hmain n G k trivial⟩\n   246\t\n   247\topen Classical in\n   248\t/-- The decision bit is the same for vertex separation (Kinnersley). -/\n   249\ttheorem decide_pathwidth_le_eq {n : ℕ} (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (k : ℕ) :\n   250\t    @decide (pathwidth G ≤ k) (Classical.propDecidable _) =\n   251\t      @decide (vertexSeparation G ≤ k) (Classical.propDecidable _) :=\n   252\t  decide_eq_decide.mpr (by rw [vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth])\n   253\t\n   254\topen Classical in\n   255\t/-- `PathwidthSubexpOn C`, stated with `vertexSeparation` in place of `pathwidth`, for the\n   256\tstatements of item 08 that are made against `VertexSeparation.lean`. -/\n   257\ttheorem pathwidthSubexpOn_iff_vertexSeparation (C : GraphClass) :\n   258\t    PathwidthSubexpOn C ↔\n   259\t    ∃ (machine : PrimitiveMachine) (c : ℝ) (d : ℕ) (f : ℕ → ℝ),\n   260\t      0 < c ∧ IsSubexpExponent f ∧\n   261\t      ∀ (n : ℕ) (G : SimpleGraph (Fin n)) (k : ℕ), C n G →\n   262\t        ∃ (time : ℕ) (final : machine.Cfg),\n   263\t          Trace machine.step time (machine.init (encodeGraph G k)) final ∧\n   264\t          final.state = Sum.inl (@decide (vertexSeparation G ≤ k) (Classical.propDecidable _)) ∧\n   265\t          (time : ℝ) ≤ c * (((encodeGraph G k).length : ℝ) + 1) ^ d * (2 : ℝ) ^ (f n) := by\n   266\t  simp only [PathwidthSubexpOn, decide_pathwidth_le_eq]\n   267\t\n   268\tend MOSPFormalization.SubexpPathwidth","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0164ZPMHw4itNiUbhdFh8VX2","agent_id":"a97cfc7d4ba482ba5","session_id":"e33d18ed-7e5b-4d5f-8bbe-6467cd7c6364","uuid":"bf68cedb-63b1-49c4-90fd-e0bb158476c5","timestamp":"2026-11-05T07:57:44.731Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FfeHoNq6rfHKZ8Sok23HTM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Refuter pass over paper 3 PDF"}
