ays \"0 `sorry`, 0 `axiom` declaration(s) **outside the header comment**\"; the paper's sentence drops the qualifier.\n\n**R15 (F).** Terms used before their gloss: `lean4export` and `nanoda_lib` appear in Table 4.2 rows (iv) and (v) and §4.8 (p. 25) and are glossed only in §5.3 (p. 27); `tmpfs` (p. 26) and `SHA-256` (pp. 27, 28, 44) are never glossed; the notation `m = ⌊4.26 n⌉` (mixed floor/ceiling brackets, pp. 36–38) is not explained (the data show rounding to nearest: 85 at n = 20). Everything else the brief lists (kernel, comparator, nanoda, sorry, CDCL, O_k(1), Prop/def/theorem/axiom, k-DNF, E^NP) is glossed at or before first use.\n\n**R16 (F).** Sentences over 45 words (word counts from the extracted text, single sentences only; quotations excluded): §3.2 p. 13 \"The proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript's Section 6.4 ...\" (**98**); §7.2 p. 36 \"Tools. CaDiCaL 1.9.5 ... on the host.\" (**151**, one sentence with semicolons); §5.1 p. 26 \"Inside it, the system directories are read-only; ...\" (**87**); §6.3 p. 34 \"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting ...\" (**82**); §10.1 p. 49 \"In one sentence each: ...\" (**82**); §3.3 p. 15 \"The encodings are explicit: ...\" (**79**); §3.3 p. 15 \"For a graph on v vertices and e edges: ...\" (**74**); §9.3 p. 46 \"That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries ...\" (**73**); §7.3 p. 39 \"CaDiCaL's median is under a millisecond ...\" (**71**); §4 p. 20 \"There are known ways for that to happen: ...\" (**68**); §6.1 p. 29 \"For each fixed width k ≥ 2 there is one deterministic machine ...\" (**66**); §4.5 p. 23 \"The lemmas that connect the program to the machine ...\" (**66**); §9.6 p. 48 \"In the manuscript, \"practical\" occurs once ...\" (**66**); §3.3 p. 15 (Cor. 1.4 sentence with the quotation, **66**); §8.2 p. 41 \"It takes the 23 lines after def SubexpCount ...\" (**64**); §3.4 p. 16 \"The fixed-density proof ...\" (**64**); §6.3 p. 35 \"What such a transfer inherits