{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjSJ4wmURHEx8aa8KmNj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Refuter report on paper3/latex/main.pdf (62 pages, built 2026-11-05 07:50 WET)\n\nWritten to `/tmp/paper3_refuter/REPORT.md`; the same text follows.\n\n**Summary.** I read the PDF text in full and checked every citable claim against the clone at 4eaee875, the manuscript extract, the two audits, the replay logs, the fetched related-work records, the companion Lean files and logs, the practitioner data files, and my own arithmetic. The paper's central numbers (every value of rho(n)/n, the peak, the crossing at 50, the return below 1 near e^5504 = 10^2390.36 with 2,391 digits, the clause-count bound 2,604,376, the constants 256+At / C+28700 / A+1 / S+5022741 / d+2 / e+2, the cutoff, every comparator line number, every manuscript page/line but one, every replay and nanoda number, every related-work quotation, the Lean companion claims, and all of Table 7.1) agree with the sources. Findings: **A: 6** (one false sentence about where \"ETH\" occurs in the Lean, one wrong manuscript page, a line-count artefact of +1 per file, a 41-vs-42 dependency count, two imprecise line citations); **B: 4**; **C: 1**; **D: 0**; **E: 0**; **F: 5**; **G: 5**. No finding touches the paper's stance or its verdict on the proof.\n\nCounting rule used for manuscript pages: `page(L) = 1 + (number of lines before line L that contain a form feed)`; form feeds sit at the start of lines 53, 104, 155, 208, 260, ... (67 in all, 3,815 lines). Under the alternative rule (count form feeds in lines up to and including L) every cited page comes out the same, including the one disagreement below.\n\n---\n\n## A — factual disagreements, wrong line/page numbers, wrong numbers\n\n**R1 (A).** p. 16, §3.5: *\"The hypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings \"ETH\" and \"SETH\" occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of a different family, lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/, ...).\"* Checked with `grep -rnw ETH` over `lean/**/*.lean`. \"ETH\" occurs, besides the two ParityLifts files, in **six files of this family**, all inside comments: `SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean:6` (\"the unchanged ETH square-root-log time scale\"), `SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean:147`, `SqrtLogSpace/NativeQueryTimeBounds.lean:49`, `SqrtLogSpace/BooleanDifferences.lean:7` and `:228`, `SqrtLogSpace/NativeHashQueries.lean:377`; and the theorem name `exact_count_eth` (Final.lean:104) contains it in lower case. \"SETH\" indeed occurs nowhere as a word. The substantive point (no hypothesis is defined in the library) stands; the sentence as written is false.\n\n**R2 (A).** p. 48, §9.6: *\"In the manuscript, \"practical\" occurs once (page 4, line 153) ...\"*. Line 153 of the extract (\"Experiments on formulas from circuit synthesis and diagnosis demonstrated the practical value of\") is on **page 3**: form feeds precede it at lines 53 and 104 only; page 4 begins at line 155. The count \"once\" and the eleven occurrences of \"exhaustive\" (lines 170, 311, 395, 411, 705, 2210, 2324, 2480, 2487, 2509, 3804) agree.\n\n**R3 (A).** p. 20, §4.2 and Table 4.1 p. 22: *\"... totalling 550,551 lines; the largest file is 2,873 lines (SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean).\"* Recounted with `wc -l` and Python `splitlines()` over the same 3,091 modules (my own import-closure script reproduces the 3,091 names, 50 Mathlib modules, 1 core module and 886 outside files exactly): **547,460 lines and 2,872 lines**. The paper's figures are exactly 3,091 more in total and 1 more for the file, i.e. the audit's `closure.py` counts `len(text.split('\\n'))`, one more than `wc -l` for every file ending in a newline. `proof_audit.md` carries the same 550,551 and 2,873. State the convention or use the `wc -l` figures.\n\n**R4 (A).** p. 26, §5.1: *\"... (its lakefile) clones its 41 dependencies at build time\"*. p. 23, §4.6 says *\"The manifest ... lists 42 packages\"* and Table 4.1 says 42. `lake-manifest.json` has 42 packages and the replay copy's `.lake/packages/` holds 42 directories (mathlib included); nothing is 41. The \"41\" comes from `paper3/replay/README.md` (\"41 packages under .lake/packages\"), wrong by the same one. Internal inconsistency 41 vs 42.\n\n**R5 (A).** p. 7, §2.3: *\"... a program that uses 80 one-bit memory cells (\"registers\") and 1,220 tapes (SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 98–101)\"*, and p. 58, B.2 likewise. Lines 98–101 of Final.lean contain `1220`, `256` and `1216` but not `80`; the `80` is in `BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels` at line 11 (also 105, 150). B.3 (p. 59) cites line 11 correctly; §2.3 and B.2 do not.\n\n**R6 (A).** p. 59, B.3: *\"GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution (SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean, lines 158–162) ...\"*. `primitive_execution` is declared at **lines 160–162**; line 158 is the last line of `compiler_overhead`'s proof. §4.5's \"lines 152–162\" (spanning both lemmas) is fine.\n\n## B — numbers or quotations with no stated source or regenerate command\n\n**R7 (B).** p. 6, §2.2 (and p. 56, B.1): *\"A formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes 1,006,223 bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width 3 becomes 47.9 million bits.\"* The encoded length depends on which literals the formula contains (unary codes `2·var+sign`), not on (n, m, k) alone: emulating the comparator's definitions on five random width-4 formulas with n = 125, m = 500 gives 990,775; 1,013,703; 1,005,403; 1,017,823; 1,021,583 bits. `check_encoding.py` is named but the formula/seed giving 1,006,223 is not, so the sentence reads as if the three parameters determined the number. Say \"one random formula with ...\" and name the seed, or quote only the bound 2,006,127 (which I reproduce).\n\n**R8 (B).** p. 26, §5.1: *\"... the collection's default target, which would build all 721 families\"*. No source or regenerate command. Cheap recounts give none of 721: `lean/OAI.lean` has 928 import lines, `lean/ComparatorChallenges/` has 418 JSONs and 418 `.lean` files, `lean/docs/` has 245 files. The replay README states 721 without derivation.\n\n**R9 (B).** p. 36, §7.1: *\"... counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0.\"* A literal substring search for `def main` hits 6 declarations in 3 family files (`SubexpQuarticSelectorAdapter.lean:109 def mainProgram`, `SubexpSetupProcedure.lean:10,30,48 def mainExtraStacks/mainStack/mainProcedure`, `SubexpMainSparsify.lean:333,351`); the count is 0 only with a word boundary. The conclusion (no entry point) is right; the pattern should be stated.\n\n**R10 (B, minor).** p. 61, B.4: *\"The 14,034 lines with decide ...\"*. My recount with a different comment-stripper gives 14,078. Method-dependent; no definition of \"line with decide\" beyond naming `closure.py`.\n\n## C — sentences that read as overclaims beyond the Lean statement\n\n**R11 (C).** Title (p. 1): *\"...the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\"* versus p. 4, §1.3, Not claimed 1: *\"No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. ... the title compares two guarantees.\"* and p. 36, §7.1: *\"the verified algorithm's actual running time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says it is slow\"*. Taken literally, \"the algorithm is slower\" is a running-time claim the body expressly disowns; the body makes it true only through §2.5's gloss. The title is fixed and not to be altered; this is filed so the owner knows the paper's own non-claim list contradicts the title's literal wording and the reconciliation rests entirely on §2.5 and §7.4.\n\n## D — out of scope\n\nNone found. The only graph vocabulary (independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings, p. 15; \"circuits, graphs, maps\", p. 14) is the manuscript's own quoted corollary objects; `SubexpPathwidth` appears only as the directory name; no sentence mentions treewidth, pathwidth, width parameters, crossovers or MOSP. The quoted §1.1.3 list is elided as \"[...]\".\n\n## E — title altered\n\nNone. Page 1 carries the title with the ellipsis typeset as three spaced dots and 2^n as a superscript (recorded as S2); `pdfinfo` Title is exactly `Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.`; there are no running headers; `paper3/lean/README.md` quotes the title verbatim.\n\n## F — plain-English stalls and internal inconsistencies\n\n**R12 (F).** Table 2.1 (p. 8) gives rho(50) = **50.14**; B.6 (p. 62) says *\"up to the rounding of rho(50) = 50.13\"*. rho(50) = 50.1351. Also \"equal to 1 up to rounding\" is generous: 2^(rho(50)−50) = 1.098.\n\n**R13 (F).** Table 7.1 (p. 37), column \"sat\", caption *\"the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL finished\"*; rows n = 400 and n = 500 read **\"10/20\"** and **\"8/20\"** while only 10 and 8 instances finished (the CSV confirms 10/10 and 8/8). Under the caption's definition the cells should read 10/10 and 8/8, or the caption should say \"of the twenty\".\n\n**R14 (F).** p. 41, §8.1: *\"our file has no sorry and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script counts them: zero and zero in each of our two files)\"*. `sorry` occurs in `PrimitiveMachine.lean` line 10 (header comment) and `axiom` in `Frame.lean` lines 13 and 83 (comments). `rediff.log` says \"outside the header comment\"; the paper drops the qualifier.\n\n**R15 (F).** Terms used before their gloss: `lean4export` and `nanoda_lib` in Table 4.2 rows (iv)/(v) and §4.8 (p. 25), glossed only in §5.3 (p. 27); `tmpfs` (p. 26) and `SHA-256` (pp. 27, 28, 44) never glossed; the notation `m = ⌊4.26 n⌉` (mixed brackets, pp. 36–38) unexplained (the data show rounding to nearest: 85 at n = 20). Everything else the brief lists is glossed at or before first use.\n\n**R16 (F).** Sentences over 45 words (single sentences, quotations excluded): §3.2 p. 13 \"The proof is built around the parameters ...\" (**98**); §7.2 p. 36 \"Tools. CaDiCaL 1.9.5 ... on the host.\" (**151**); §5.1 p. 26 \"Inside it, the system directories are read-only; ...\" (**87**); §6.3 p. 34 \"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting ...\" (**82**); §10.1 p. 49 \"In one sentence each: ...\" (**82**); §3.3 p. 15 \"The encodings are explicit: ...\" (**79**); §3.3 p. 15 \"For a graph on v vertices ...\" (**74**); §9.3 p. 46 \"That is why every quotation ...\" (**73**); §7.3 p. 39 \"CaDiCaL's median is under a millisecond ...\" (**71**); §4 p. 20 \"There are known ways for that to happen: ...\" (**68**); §6.1 p. 29 \"For each fixed width k ≥ 2 there is one deterministic machine ...\" (**66**); §4.5 p. 23 \"The lemmas that connect the program ...\" (**66**); §9.6 p. 48 \"In the manuscript, \"practical\" occurs once ...\" (**66**); §3.3 p. 15 (Cor. 1.4 sentence, **66**); §8.2 p. 41 \"It takes the 23 lines ...\" (**64**); §3.4 p. 16 \"The fixed-density proof ...\" (**64**); §6.3 p. 35 \"What such a transfer inherits ...\" (**63**); §4.8 p. 25 \"After the replay, then, ...\" (**62**); §7.3 p. 37 \"On every instance where both finished, ...\" (**60**); §6.3 p. 34 \"For shape (S): ...\" (**60**); §6.2 p. 33 \"Two sentences follow ...\" (**59**); and about forty more between 46 and 58 words, concentrated in §3.3, §4.3–4.7, §5, §6.2–6.3, §7.2–7.4, §9 and the entry lists of §10.2.\n\n## G — anything else\n\n**R17 (G).** p. 51: *\"Data, code and proofs. Placeholder. Item 11 completes this statement ...\"* and *\"Acknowledgements. Placeholder. ...\"* Placeholder text in the built PDF; §10.2 entry 09d refers to \"the assistant the acknowledgements disclose\", which do not yet exist.\n\n**R18 (G).** p. 13, §3.2: *\"the manuscript's exponents are undefined below n = e\"*. ln ln n is undefined only for n ≤ 1; for 1 < n < e it is defined and negative.\n\n**R19 (G).** The records the paper cites as sources disagree with the paper on counts the paper got right: `paper3/replay/README.md` says the 19 in-build axiom lines come \"from the 17 source files of the closure\" (paper and log: 8); `proof_audit.md` says \"41 manifest packages\" (42), \"33 definitions\" (35/33/2), and places the challenge theorem at \"SubexponentialSAT.lean:181\" (194); `statement_audit.md` says `enable_nanoda: false` \"on 416 of the 418\" (413/2/3). The paper corrects these silently except §10.2's 04a/04b/04d/04e.\n\n**R20 (G).** Figure 7.1 caption (p. 38) opens *\"The same data on a logarithmic time axis\"* with no antecedent in the figure; it means Table 7.1.\n\n**R21 (G).** p. 5, §2.2: the quotation *\"Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k literals\" (p. 1, line 44)* spans lines 44–45; trivial.\n\n---\n\n## What was checked and agreed (coverage)\n\n- **Title**: verbatim on p. 1 and in the metadata; no running headers; 62 pages.\n- **Comparator file, every cited line**: 220 lines; SAT 13–51 (Literal 19–22, Clause/CNF 24–25, Assignment 17, evals 29–36, WidthAtMost 38, satisfyingAssignments 44–45, count 47); encoding 53–81 (55, 57–58, 60–62, 64–65, 67–71, 73–77, 79); toNat 7–9; Trace 87–90; PrimitiveMachine 92–99; Cfg 103–105; step 107–119; init 121–124; namespace 83–128; runtimeScale 132–134; runtimeBound 136–139; countWord 147; LocatedCfg 149–153, locatedInit 155–159, headDisplacement 161–164, monitor 166–182, locatedStep 184–186, visitedSpace 188–189; theorem 194–217 with every conjunct at the cited line; `sorry` 218; end 220; `∃ machine` 195; SHA-256 4183b205.\n- **Solution files**: Final.lean 201 lines; theorems at 10, 74, 104–147 (20 ≤ n at 109; `4*A, 2*d, 2*e` at 131–132), 149–183 (3 ≤ k; `↔ Satisfiable` 164), 184; witnesses at **line 41**; program at 40; 256/1216 at 100; `#print axioms` ×5 at 197–201. NativeLargeEntry.lean: `1 ≤ C ∧ 1 ≤ A` at 12, 27, 39, 46, 48, cutoff at **50**, `256 + At` and the degree at **51**. NativeLeafContract.lean `1 ≤ At` at **793**. NativeEntry.lean 81–100, 28700 at 92. NativeOuterBudgets.lean 297, 317. NativeOuter.lean 50–51, 53–56, 73. CompileSpace.lean 125–143. GuardedCompiler.lean 14–17, 30–47, 131, 132, 152–153, 600. Asymptotics.lean 10–11, 14–20, 190–191. BitProgram.lean 12 kinds. StatementScale.lean 15; Statement.lean 9–14; EncodingBounds.lean 17; StatementCanonical.lean 37; FiniteFallback.lean:14 and SparseCharging.lean:5; Main.lean 16 lines; the seven re-declaring files; 35 statement definitions.\n- **Closure recount (own script)**: 3,091 modules, 50 Mathlib, 1 core; 886 of 3,977 outside, clean; all keyword counts of Table 4.1 reproduced (0s, set_option 2, opaque 9 at the cited lines, noncomputable 1,048, Classical.choice 4, propext 4, open Classical 8, instance 148, local instance 40, termination_by 9, decreasing_by 5, `#print axioms` 19 in 8 closure files, 35 in 17 family files, 9 outside); identifier search all absent; `#eval` 0; word `IO` 0.\n- **Repository facts**: toolchain v4.34.1; lakefile lines 8, 9, 89, 290–304 (296 `unsafe`), 188–191 eleven patch names, 306; OAI.lean line 49; 23 patches, none for Mathlib; 42 manifest packages; Mathlib d13f23b7; formalization.yaml 1683–1685; workflows = lock-prs.yml; no \"depends on axioms\" in the clone; 418 JSONs 413/2/3; the JSON's six fields; ComparatorChallenges/README.md 10 lines; docs/378.md 17 lines, lines 9, 11, 17 verbatim; history.md 56 lines, lines 7, 11, 18; tex line 25; manuscript 67 pages; Mathlib lines 118–120, 408–414, 421–423, 494 at d13f23b7.\n- **Manuscript quotations**: every cited (page, line) pair other than R2 — p.1 44, 48–49; p.7 326–327; p.2 56–103; p.3 107–127; p.4 165–205; p.5 211–243; p.43 2474–2487; p.51 2912–2925; p.52 2949–2988; p.53 2994–3028; p.54 3045–3069; p.57 3222–3252; p.65–67 reference entries; abstract 6–11 — fragments verbatim at the cited lines.\n- **Arithmetic**: all Table 2.1 and Table 3.1 rows; peak 16/e² = 2.1654 at n = 10^23.71; return to 1 at y = 5503.66 (10^2390.2, 2,391 digits); 2^(rho−n) = 1.10 / 2820 / 4.01×10^51 at 50/100/500; 2^2.79; 2,604,376; 2,006,127; 2/e 