 p. 26, §5.1: *\"... the collection's default target, which would build all 721 families\"*. No source or regenerate command. Cheap recounts give none of 721: `lean/OAI.lean` has 928 import lines, `lean/ComparatorChallenges/` has 418 JSONs and 418 `.lean` files, `lean/docs/` has 245 files. The replay README states 721 without derivation.\n\n**R9 (B).** p. 36, §7.1: *\"... counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0.\"* A literal substring search for `def main` hits 6 declarations in 3 family files (`SubexpQuarticSelectorAdapter.lean:109 def mainProgram`, `SubexpSetupProcedure.lean:10,30,48 def mainExtraStacks/mainStack/mainProcedure`, `SubexpMainSparsify.lean:333,351`); the count is 0 only with a word boundary. The conclusion (no entry point) is right; the pattern should be stated.\n\n**R10 (B, minor).** p. 61, B.4: *\"The 14,034 lines with decide ...\"*. My recount with a different comment-stripper gives 14,078. Method-dependent; no definition of \"line with decide\" beyond naming `closure.py`.\n\n## C — sentences that read as overclaims beyond the Lean statement\n\n**R11 (C).** Title (p. 1): *\"...the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\"* versus p. 4, §1.3, Not claimed 1: *\"No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. ... the title compares two guarantees.\"* and p. 36, §7.1: *\"the verified algorithm's actual running time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says it is slow\"*. Taken literally, \"the algorithm is slower\" is a running-time claim the body expressly disowns; the body makes it true only through §2.5's gloss. The title is fixed and not to be altered; this is filed so the owner knows the paper's own non-claim list contradicts the title's literal wording and the reconciliation rests entirely on §2.5 and §7.4.\n\n## D — out of scope\n\nNone found. The only graph vocabulary (independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings, p. 15; \"circuits, graphs, maps\", p. 14) is the manuscript's own quoted corollary objects; `SubexpPathwidth` appears only as the directory name; no sentence mentions treewidth, pathwidth, width parameters, crossovers or MOSP. The quoted §1.1.3 list is elided as \"[...]\".\n\n## E — title altered\n\nNone. Page 1 carries the title with the ellipsis typeset as three spaced dots and 2^n as a superscript (recorded as S2); `pdfinfo` Title is exactly `Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.`; there are no running headers; `paper3/lean/README.md` quotes the title verbatim.\n\n## F — plain-English stalls and internal inconsistencies\n\n**R12 (F).** Table 2.1 (p. 8) gives rho(50) = **50.14**; B.6 (p. 62) says *\"up to the rounding of rho(50) = 50.13\"*. rho(50) = 50.1351. Also \"equal to 1 up to rounding\" is generous: 2^(rho(50)−50) = 1.098.\n\n**R13 (F).** Table 7.1 (p. 37), column \"sat\", caption *\"the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL finished\"*; rows n = 400 and n = 500 read **\"10/20\"** and **\"8/20\"** while only 10 and 8 instances finished (the CSV confirms 10/10 and 8/8). Under the caption's definition the cells should read 10/10 and 8/8, or the caption should say \"of the twenty\".\n\n**R14 (F).** p. 41, §8.1: *\"our file has no sorry and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script counts them: zero and zero in each of our two files)\"*. `sorry` occurs in `PrimitiveMachine.lean` line 10 (header comment) and `axiom` in `Frame.lean` lines 13 and 83 (comments). `rediff.log` says \"outside the header comment\"; the paper drops the qualifier.\n\n**R15 (F).** Terms used before their gloss: `lean4export` and `nanoda_lib` in Table 4.2 rows (iv)/(v) and §4.8 (p. 25), glossed only in §5.3 (p. 27); `tmpfs` (p. 26) and `SHA-256` (pp. 27, 28, 44) never glossed; the notation `m = ⌊4.26 n⌉` (mixed brackets, pp. 36–38) unexplained (the data show rounding to nearest: 85 at n = 20). Everything else the brief lists is glossed at or before first use.\n\n**R16 (F).** Sentences over 45 words (single sentences, quotations excluded): §3.2 p. 13 \"The proof is built around the parameters ...\" (**98**); §7.2 p. 36 \"Tools. CaDiCaL 1.9.5 ... on the host.\" (**151**); §5.1 p. 26 \"Inside it, the system directories are read-only; ...\" (**87**); §6.3 p. 34 \"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting ...\" (**82**); §10.1 p. 49 \"In one sentence each: ...\" (**82**); §3.3 p. 15 \"The encodings are explicit: ...\" (**79**); §3.3 p. 15 \"For a graph on v vertices ...\" (**74**); §9.3 p. 46 \"That is why every quotation ...\" (**73**); §7.3 p. 39 \"CaDiCaL's median is under a millisecond ...\" (**71**); §4 p. 20 \"There are known ways for that to happen: ...\" (**68**); §6.1 p. 29 \"For each fixed width k ≥ 2 there is one deterministic machine ...\" (**66**); §4.5 p. 23 \"The lemmas that connect the program ...\" (**66**); §9.6 p. 48 \"In the manuscript, \"practical\" occurs once ...\" (**66**); §3.3 p. 15 (Cor. 1.4 sentence, **66**); §8.2 p. 41 \"It takes the 23 lines ...\" (**64**); §3.4 p. 16 \"The fixed-density proof ...\" (**64**); §6.3 p. 35 \"What such a transfer inherits ...\" (**63**); §4.8 p. 25 \"After the replay, then, ...\" (**62**); §7.3 p. 37 \"On every instance where both finished, ...\" (**60**); §6.3 p. 34 \"For shape (S): ...\" (**60**); §6.2 p. 33 \"Two sentences follow ...\" (**59**); and about forty more between 46 and 58 words, concentrated in §3.3, §4.3–4.7, §5, §6.2–6.3, §7.2–7.4, §9 and the entry lists of §10.2.\n\n## G — anything else\n\n**R17 (G).** p. 51: *\"Data, code and proofs. Placeholder. Item 11 completes this statement ...\"* and *\"Acknowledgements. Placeholder. ...\"* Placeholder text in the built PDF; §10.2 entry 09d refers to \"the assistant the acknowledgements disclose\", which do not yet exist.\n\n**R18 (G).** p. 13, §3.2: *\"the manuscript's exponents are undefined below n = e\"*. ln ln n is undefined only for n ≤ 1; for 1 < n < e it is defined and negative.\n\n**R19 (G).** The records the paper cites as sources disagree with the paper on counts the paper got right: `paper3/replay/README.md` says the 19 in-build axiom lines come \"from the 17 source files of the closure\" (paper and log: 8); `proof_audit.md` says \"41 manifest packages\" (42), \"33 definitions\" (35/33/2), and places the challenge theorem at \"SubexponentialSAT.lean:181\" (194); `statement_audit.md` says `enable_nanoda: false` \"on 416 of the 418\" (413/2/3). The paper corrects these silently except §10.2's 04a/04b/04d/04e.\n\n**R20 (G).** Figure 7.1 caption (p. 38) opens *\"The same data on a logarithmic time axis\"* with no antecedent in the figure; it means Table 7.1.\n\n**R21 (G).** p. 5, §2.2: the quotation *\"Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k literals\" (p. 1, line 44)* spans lines 44–45; trivial.\n\n---\n\n## What was checked and agreed (coverage)\n\n- **Title**: verbatim on p. 1 and in the metadata; no running headers; 62 pages.\n- **Comparator file, every cited line**: 220 lines; SAT 13–51 (Literal 19–22, Clause/CNF 24–25, Assignment 17, evals 29–36, WidthAtMost 38, satisfyingAssignments 44–45, count 47); encoding 53–81 (55, 57–58, 60–62, 64–65, 67–71, 73–77, 79); toNat 7–9; Trace 87–90; PrimitiveMachine 92–99; Cfg 103–105; step 107–119; init 121–124; namespace 83–128; runtimeScale 132–134; runtimeBound 136–139; countWord 147; LocatedCfg 149–153, locatedInit 155–159, headDisplacement 161–164, monitor 166–182, locatedStep 184–186, visitedSpace 188–189; theorem 194–217 with every conjunct at the cited line; `sorry` 218; end 220; `∃ machine` 195; SHA-256 4183b205.\n- **Solution files**: Final.lean 201 lines; theorems at 10, 74, 104–147 (20 ≤ n at 109; `4*A, 2*d, 2*e` at 131–132), 149–183 (3 ≤ k; `↔ Satisfiable` 164), 184; witnesses at **line 41**; program at 40; 256/1216 at 100; `#print axioms` ×5 at 197–201. NativeLargeEntry.lean: `1 ≤ C ∧ 1 ≤ A` at 12, 27, 39, 46, 48, cutoff at **50**, `256 + At` and