{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MzQr63k8YCk6BgEQd2grAN","type":"tool_result","content":"=== sec3_beyond.tex 294-301\nwithin a subexponential bound, for each fixed $k \\ge 2$; each hypothesis\nis the denial of that. The hypotheses themselves are not defined in the\nlibrary (the strings ``ETH'' and ``SETH'' occur in the collection's Lean\nonly in two files of a different family,\n\\clonefile{lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/}, which take an ETH-like\nproposition as a hypothesis and are not among the files the proof depends on, its closure, Section~\\ref{sec:proof}).\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences} says exactly how the verified statement\nnegates each one. The two sentences the manuscript adds at lines\n=== sec9_related.tex 266-271\na statement would be likely to use (``practical'', ``brute'', ``trying\nall'', ``exhaustive'', ``$2^n$'', ``constants are'', ``concrete''). Outside\nthe manuscript, none occurs. In the manuscript, ``practical'' occurs once\n(page 4, line 153), in a sentence about the practical value of SAT\nsolvers on industrial formulas, a different sense; ``exhaustive'' occurs\neleven times, as the motivation of SETH quoted above (line 170) and as the\n=== sec4_proof.tex 60-66\nsixteen-line file that imports \\path{SqrtLog.ExecutionNativeFinal} and\n\\path{SqrtLogSpace.Final} and declares nothing (\\clonefile{Main.lean}). Its closure inside the repository is\n\\textbf{3,091 modules}, every one in the family's own directory,\ntotalling \\textbf{550,551} lines; the largest file is 2,873 lines\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean}).\nOutside the family, the closure reaches \\textbf{50} modules of Mathlib\nand one module of Lean's own library, \\path{Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega},\n=== sec5_replay.tex 27-31\neight cores (\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling\npriority; and the network is on, because the repository's build file\n(its \\texttt{lakefile}) clones its 41 dependencies at build time and the compiled Mathlib comes\nfrom a download cache. Inside the sandbox, the Lean installer\n\\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home and fetched the\n=== sec5_replay.tex 35-38\n\\texttt{lake build OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, which builds\nthe solution's 3,091 files and not the collection's default target,\nwhich would build all 721 families; and \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the\none theorem the comparator names,\n=== sec2_statement.tex 180-185\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}, $A \\ge 258$). No closed value of any of the\nfive constants exists anywhere. The machine is explicit in one respect:\nit is produced mechanically (``compiled'') from a program that uses 80 one-bit memory cells (``registers'') and 1,220 tapes\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101). How many states it\nhas is never computed.\n\n=== sec2_statement.tex 83-89\n\\]\nwhere $O(k\\,n\\,m)$ is read ``at most a fixed multiple of $k\\,n\\,m$''.\nA formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes\n$1{,}006{,}223$ bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width\n3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that\nimitates the definitions,\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}. Nothing in the\n=== secB_appendix_technical.tex 200-205\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12). The witness\nmachine is compiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220\ntapes, with \\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 98--101); its state count is\na finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. The solution's\ninternal \\(\\rho\\) (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Asymptotics.lean}, lines\n=== secB_appendix_technical.tex 278-282\nThe compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary: \\path{GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 158--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n=== secB_appendix_technical.tex 66-71\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\ndefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}),\na formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\), \\(k = 4\\) encodes to\n\\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with \\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\),\n\\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n=== secB_appendix_technical.tex 360-366\nreasoning the comparator permits. The 148 \\texttt{instance}s and 40\n\\texttt{local instance}s are \\texttt{Fintype}, \\texttt{Decidable} and\nalgebraic instances; an instance cannot be ``inconsistent'' without an\naxiom, and there is none. The 14,034 lines with \\texttt{decide} are\nkernel-evaluated decision procedures, not the native evaluator. Nine\n\\texttt{termination\\_by} and five \\texttt{decreasing\\_by} clauses are the\nrecursions the kernel checks.\n=== secB_appendix_technical.tex 476-481\nmedian at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves, so their\nratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves, $2^{\\rhon - n}$: below\n1 for $n < 50$, equal to 1 at $n = 50$ up to the rounding of\n$\\rho(50) = 50.13$, and above 1 from there ($2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers\neach curve scaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply\n=== sec7_practitioner.tex 36-42\nsolution's 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no\n\\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no input-output (\\texttt{IO})\naction. The\nscript of this section checks that, reading every file of the family\nwith \\texttt{open()} and counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0. The\nlibrary proves that a machine with the stated bound exists and exhibits\none as a mathematical term, inside a development with 1,048\n=== sec7_practitioner.tex 108-114\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. Columns: $n$ variables; $m$ clauses;\n``sat'', the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL\nfinished; for each tool, ``fin'', the number of instances it finished\nwithin budget, and ``med'' and ``max'', its median and maximum seconds\n=== sec7_practitioner.tex 128-132\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{The same data on a logarithmic time axis, where each grid line\nis ten times the one below. Solid lines with markers are medians, dotted\nlines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool whose median is censored\n=== sec8_lean.tex 62-67\nSo the copy holds the theorem's \\emph{vocabulary} and not the theorem.\nThe \\texttt{sorry} at line 218 is outside the copied range, and our file\nhas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script\ncounts them: zero and zero in each of our two files). Everything in the\ncopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\nfalse; what the definitions mean is Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s\n=== sec5_replay.tex 23-26\nsandbox on the project's machine. The sandbox tool is \\texttt{bwrap}\n(bubblewrap). Inside it, the system directories are read-only; the home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\texttt{tmpfs}), so nothing of the real home or of\nthis repository is visible; processes, inter-process communication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate ``namespaces''); the whole sandbox is confined to\n=== sec5_replay.tex 88-92\nclone, and the comparator file and the solution's files have the same\nhashes in both (\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/hashes.txt}; the\ncomparator statement's SHA-256 begins \\texttt{4183b205}). The full build\nlog is kept out of the repository by size; its SHA-256\n(\\texttt{d313f594…}) and its last 2