{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0154nnb7x9EkoLRGgHQeT7YA","type":"tool_result","content":"ALL CHECKS PASSED\n=== sec3_beyond.tex: The proof is built around the parameters\n104:comparator's exponent by four times it for $n \\ge 20$. The proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript's Section~6.4, the three numbers that set how the proof thins the formula before counting (its ``sparsification''), to the letter: the manuscript selects $\\lambda_n = \\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil$,\n105-$\\nu_n = \\lceil\\log_2\\lambda_n\\rceil$ and\n106-$s = \\lfloor\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}/\\nu_n^2\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\n107-integer satisfying $s^2\\nu_n^4 \\le \\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486; $\\lceil x\\rceil$ is the smallest whole number at least $x$ and $\\lfloor x\\rfloor$ the largest at most $x$), and \\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define\n108-\\texttt{sparsifyLambda}, \\texttt{sparsifyNu} and\n109-\\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three quantities. What\n110-Appendix~D of the manuscript proves is not in the library in any form we\n111-could find (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond-lean}). So the general exponent\n112-$n\\log\\log n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ and the fixed-density exponent\n113-$n/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ are \\textbf{paper-only}: proved at p.~57 (lines\n=== sec7_practitioner.tex: paragraph{Tools\n72:\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\n73-through python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one\n74-\\texttt{solve()} call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0\n75-\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, through the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called Arjun and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\n76-Soos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, at its defaults $\\varepsilon =\n77-0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\n78-probability $0.8$), with the count taken ov