{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjSfMkQ8wsxZMCETdTGt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Jkht1E4qtwzFfVesL3KHB2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfails=[]\ndef sub(path, old, new):\n    s=open(path).read()\n    if old not in s:\n        fails.append((path, old[:70])); return\n    open(path,'w').write(s.replace(old,new,1))\n\n# 1\nsub('sec3_beyond.tex', \"\"\"The proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript's Section~6.4, the three numbers that set how the proof thins the formula before counting (its ``sparsification''), to the letter: the manuscript selects $\\\\lambda_n = \\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil$,\n$\\\\nu_n = \\\\lceil\\\\log_2\\\\lambda_n\\\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\\\lfloor\\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda_n}/\\\\nu_n^2\\\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\\\nu_n^4 \\\\le \\\\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines 2480--2486; $\\\\lceil x\\\\rceil$ is the smallest whole number at least $x$ and $\\\\lfloor x\\\\rfloor$ the largest at most $x$), and \\\\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define\n\\\\texttt{sparsifyLambda}, \\\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and\n\\\\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three quantities.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"The proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript's\nSection~6.4 to the letter. Those are the three numbers that set how the\nproof thins the formula before counting (its ``sparsification''). The\nmanuscript selects $\\\\lambda_n = \\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil$,\n$\\\\nu_n = \\\\lceil\\\\log_2\\\\lambda_n\\\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\\\lfloor\\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda_n}/\\\\nu_n^2\\\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\\\nu_n^4 \\\\le \\\\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines\n2480--2486). Here $\\\\lceil x\\\\rceil$ is the smallest whole number at least\n$x$ and $\\\\lfloor x\\\\rfloor$ the largest at most $x$. And\n\\\\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define \\\\texttt{sparsifyLambda},\n\\\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and \\\\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three\nquantities.\"\"\")\n# 2\nsub('sec7_practitioner.tex', \"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\\\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\nthrough python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\\\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one\n\\\\texttt{solve()} call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0\n\\\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, through the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called Arjun and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\nSoos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, at its defaults $\\\\varepsilon =\n0.8$, $\\\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\nprobability $0.8$), with the count taken over all variables (its ``projection set''); and,\nat $n \\\\le 30$ only, exact counting by listing every solution with\nCaDiCaL, one at a time, which is not a counter at any larger size (its\ntime grows with the count, and the count grows exponentially in $n$)\nand is here as the independent check of the two counters where it can\nreach.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\\\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\ngives the yes-or-no answer, through python-sat 1.9.dev15\n\\\\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT} in one \\\\texttt{solve()} call. Ganak 2.8.0\n\\\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, gives the exact count,\nthrough the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called\nArjun and then the counter). ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\nSoos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, runs at its defaults $\\\\varepsilon =\n0.8$, $\\\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\nprobability $0.8$), with the count taken over all variables (its\n``projection set''). And at $n \\\\le 30$ only, the exact count is also\nfound by listing every solution with CaDiCaL, one at a time. That is not\na counter at any larger size, since its time grows with the count and the\ncount grows exponentially in $n$; it is here as the independent check of\nthe two counters where it can reach.\"\"\")\n# 3\nsub('sec5_replay.tex', \"\"\"Inside it, the system directories are read-only; the home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\\\texttt{tmpfs}, a scratch file system held in memory and gone when the sandbox ends), so nothing of the real home or of\nthis repository is visible; processes, inter-process communication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate ``namespaces''); the whole sandbox is confined to\neight cores (\\\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling\npriority; and the network is on, because\"\"\",\n\"\"\"Inside it, the system directories are read-only. The home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\\\texttt{tmpfs}, a scratch file\nsystem held in memory and gone when the sandbox ends), so nothing of the\nreal home or of this repository is visible. Processes, inter-process\ncommunication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate\n``namespaces''). The whole sandbox is confined to eight cores\n(\\\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling priority. And the\nnetwork is on, because\"\"\")\n# 4\nsub('sec6_consequences.tex', \"\"\"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$ (given small sets over a universe of elements, colour the elements with two colours so that no set is all one colour; p.~5, lines 239--243): one variable per element and\ntwo clauses per set give\"\"\",\n\"\"\"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of\nsize at most $k$ (p.~5, lines 239--243). The problem: given small sets\nover a universe of elements, colour the elements with two colours so that\nno set is all one colour. One variable per element and two clauses per\nset give\"\"\")\n# 5\nsub('sec10_conclusion.tex', \"\"\"In one sentence each: the verified\nstatement is a guarantee with uncomputed constants, above $2^n$ at every\npractical size with the constants set to one, and refuting ETH and its\nrelatives nonetheless; the proof shows no flaw by inspection, builds\nfrom source, and passes two checkers; the sharper exponents are\npaper-only; and no running time\"\"\",\n\"\"\"In one sentence each. The verified\nstatement is a guarantee with uncomputed constants, above $2^n$ at every\npractical size with the constants set to one, and it refutes ETH and its\nrelatives nonetheless. The proof shows no flaw by inspection, builds\nfrom source, and passes two checkers. The sharper exponents are\npaper-only. And no running time\"\"\")\n# 6+7\nsub('sec3_beyond.tex', \"\"\"For a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges: the number of independent sets (sets of vertices no two of which are joined by an edge) and, for fixed $q$, of proper $q$-colourings (colourings with $q$ colours such that no edge joins two vertices of the same colour), in time $2^{o(v)}$, and the number of perfect matchings (sets of edges touching every vertex exactly once) in time $2^{o(v+e)}$, all in polynomial space. The encodings are explicit: one variable per\nvertex and a clause of two literals per edge (lines 107--112); $q$\nvariables per vertex with ``exactly one colour'' and ``no equal\ncolours'' clauses, a $q$-CNF on $qv$ variables (lines 112--115); one\nvariable per edge and a linear-size circuit for ``exactly one selected\nedge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with $O(v+e)$ variables, with\nthe extra variables fixed by the edge bits so that the count is\npreserved (lines 116--119).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"For a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges, three counts. The number of\nindependent sets (sets of vertices no two of which are joined by an\nedge), in time $2^{o(v)}$. For fixed $q$, the number of proper\n$q$-colourings (colourings with $q$ colours such that no edge joins two\nvertices of the same colour), in time $2^{o(v)}$. And the number of\nperfect matchings (sets of edges touching every vertex exactly once), in\ntime $2^{o(v+e)}$. All three in polynomial space. The encodings are\nexplicit. For independent sets: one variable per vertex and a clause of\ntwo literals per edge (lines 107--112). For colourings: $q$ variables per\nvertex with ``exactly one colour'' and ``no equal colours'' clauses, a\n$q$-CNF on $qv$ variables (lines 112--115). For matchings: one variable\nper edge and a linear-size circuit for ``exactly one selected edge meets\neach vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with $O(v+e)$ variables, with the extra\nvariables fixed by the edge bits so that the count is preserved (lines\n116--119).\"\"\")\n# 8\nsub('sec9_related.tex', \"\"\"That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries\nthe page of the PDF beside the line of the extract, why the Lean is read\nfrom its source files and never from a rendering, and why no claim in\nthis paper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the formulas\"\"\",\n\"\"\"That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries\nthe page of the PDF beside the line of the extract. It is why the Lean is\nread from its source files and never from a rendering. And it is why no\nclaim in this paper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the\nformulas\"\"\")\n# 9\nsub('sec7_practitioner.tex', \"\"\"and 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"and 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s). At 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable. That is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold: the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half.\"\"\")\n# 10\nsub('sec4_proof.tex', \"\"\"There are known ways for\nthat to happen: an added axiom; a \\\\texttt{sorry}, which is Lean's word\nfor a proof left out; a step proved by trusting a compiled program\nrather than the kernel; a patched copy of the mathematics library; a\nstatement in the solution that differs from the statement in the\nchallenge; or a running-time bound proved against a cost model of the\nproof's own choosing. We found none of these.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"There are known ways for\nthat to happen. An added axiom. A \\\\texttt{sorry}, which is Lean's word\nfor a proof left out. A step proved by trusting a compiled program\nrather than the kernel. A patched copy of the mathematics library. A\nstatement in the solution that differs from the statement in the\nchallenge. Or a running-time bound proved against a cost model of the\nproof's own choosing. We found none of these.\"\"\")\n# 11\nsub('sec6_consequences.tex', \"\"\"For each\nfixed width $k \\\\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine which, given\nany formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables, halts with the number\nof satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines 203--204), the\nanswer ``is that number positive'' on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205--207), within the bound $C\\\\,(L+1)^d\\\\,2^{A\\\\rhon}$\n(lines 210--211).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"For each\nfixed width $k \\\\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine with three\nproperties, given any formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables. It\nhalts with the number of satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines\n203--204). It has the answer ``is that number positive'' on its answer\ntape and in its halting state (lines 205--207). And it does so within the\nbound $C\\\\,(L+1)^d\\\\,2^{A\\\\rhon}$ (lines 210--211).\"\"\")\n# 12\nsub('sec4_proof.tex', \"\"\"The lemmas that connect the\nprogram to the machine conclude in the challenge's own words: a source\nrun of $n$ steps becomes a run of the compiled machine of $7n$ steps,\nwith the overhead $7$ proved as a theorem\n(\\\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 152--162), and the\nspace lemma\"\"\",\n\"\"\"The lemmas that connect the\nprogram to the machine conclude in the challenge's own words. A source\nrun of $n$ steps becomes a run of t