on~6.4 to the letter. Those are the three numbers that set how the\nproof thins the formula before counting (its ``sparsification''). The\nmanuscript selects $\\\\lambda_n = \\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil$,\n$\\\\nu_n = \\\\lceil\\\\log_2\\\\lambda_n\\\\rceil$ and\n$s = \\\\lfloor\\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda_n}/\\\\nu_n^2\\\\rfloor$, ``the largest nonnegative\ninteger satisfying $s^2\\\\nu_n^4 \\\\le \\\\lambda_n$'' (p.~43, lines\n2480--2486). Here $\\\\lceil x\\\\rceil$ is the smallest whole number at least\n$x$ and $\\\\lfloor x\\\\rfloor$ the largest at most $x$. And\n\\\\path{Asymptotics.lean} lines 14--20 define \\\\texttt{sparsifyLambda},\n\\\\texttt{sparsifyNu} and \\\\texttt{sparsifySchedule} as exactly those three\nquantities.\"\"\")\n# 2\nsub('sec7_practitioner.tex', \"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\\\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\nthrough python-sat 1.9.dev15 \\\\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, one\n\\\\texttt{solve()} call, for the yes-or-no answer; Ganak 2.8.0\n\\\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, through the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called Arjun and then the counter), for the exact count; ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\nSoos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, at its defaults $\\\\varepsilon =\n0.8$, $\\\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\nprobability $0.8$), with the count taken over all variables (its ``projection set''); and,\nat $n \\\\le 30$ only, exact counting by listing every solution with\nCaDiCaL, one at a time, which is not a counter at any larger size (its\ntime grows with the count, and the count grows exponentially in $n$)\nand is here as the independent check of the two counters where it can\nreach.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\\\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\ngives the yes-or-no answer, through python-sat 1.9.dev15\n\\\\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT} in one \\\\texttt{solve()} call. Ganak 2.8.0\n\\\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, gives the exact count,\nthrough the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called\nArjun and then the counter). ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\\\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\nSoos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, runs at its defaults $\\\\varepsilon =\n0.8$, $\\\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\nprobability $0.8$), with the count taken over all variables (its\n``projection set''). And at $n \\\\le 30$ only, the exact count is also\nfound by listing every solution with CaDiCaL, one at a time. That is not\na counter at any larger size, since its time grows with the count and the\ncount grows exponentially in $n$; it is here as the independent check of\nthe two counters where it can reach.\"\"\")\n# 3\nsub('sec5_replay.tex', \"\"\"Inside it, the system directories are read-only; the home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\\\texttt{tmpfs}, a scratch file system held in memory and gone when the sandbox ends), so nothing of the real home or of\nthis repository is visible; processes, inter-process communication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate ``namespaces''); the whole sandbox is confined to\neight cores (\\\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling\npriority; and the network is on, because\"\"\",\n\"\"\"Inside it, the system directories are read-only. The home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\\\texttt{tmpfs}, a scratch file\nsystem held in memory and gone when the sandbox ends), so nothing of the\nreal home or of this repository is visible. Processes, inter-process\ncommunication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate\n``namespaces''). The whole sandbox is confined to eight cores\n(\\\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling priority. And the\nnetwork is on, because\"\"\")\n# 4\nsub('sec6_consequences.tex', \"\"\"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting with sets of size at most $k$ (given small sets over a universe of elements, 