ed\nedge meets each vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with $O(v+e)$ variables, with\nthe extra variables fixed by the edge bits so that the count is\npreserved (lines 116--119).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"For a graph on $v$ vertices and $e$ edges, three counts. The number of\nindependent sets (sets of vertices no two of which are joined by an\nedge), in time $2^{o(v)}$. For fixed $q$, the number of proper\n$q$-colourings (colourings with $q$ colours such that no edge joins two\nvertices of the same colour), in time $2^{o(v)}$. And the number of\nperfect matchings (sets of edges touching every vertex exactly once), in\ntime $2^{o(v+e)}$. All three in polynomial space. The encodings are\nexplicit. For independent sets: one variable per vertex and a clause of\ntwo literals per edge (lines 107--112). For colourings: $q$ variables per\nvertex with ``exactly one colour'' and ``no equal colours'' clauses, a\n$q$-CNF on $qv$ variables (lines 112--115). For matchings: one variable\nper edge and a linear-size circuit for ``exactly one selected edge meets\neach vertex'', reduced to 3-CNF with $O(v+e)$ variables, with the extra\nvariables fixed by the edge bits so that the count is preserved (lines\n116--119).\"\"\")\n# 8\nsub('sec9_related.tex', \"\"\"That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries\nthe page of the PDF beside the line of the extract, why the Lean is read\nfrom its source files and never from a rendering, and why no claim in\nthis paper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the formulas\"\"\",\n\"\"\"That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries\nthe page of the PDF beside the line of the extract. It is why the Lean is\nread from its source files and never from a rendering. And it is why no\nclaim in this paper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the\nformulas\"\"\")\n# 9\nsub('sec7_practitioner.tex', \"\"\"and 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s); at 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable, which is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold (the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"and 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s). At 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable. That is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold: the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half.\"\"\")\n# 10\nsub('sec4_proof.tex', \"\"\"There are known ways for\nthat to happen: an added axiom; a \\\\texttt{sorry}, which is Lean's word\nfor a proof left out; a step proved by trusting a compiled program\nrather than the kernel; a patched copy of the mathematics library; a\nstatement in the solution that differs from the statement in the\nchallenge; or a running-time bound proved against a cost model of the\nproof's own choosing. We found none of these.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"There are known ways for\nthat to happen. An added axiom. A \\\\texttt{sorry}, which is Lean's word\nfor a proof left out. A step proved by trusting a compiled program\nrather than the kernel. A patched copy of the mathematics library. A\nstatement in the solution that differs from the statement in the\nchallenge. Or a running-time bound proved against a cost model of the\nproof's own choosing. We found none of these.\"\"\")\n# 11\nsub('sec6_consequences.tex', \"\"\"For each\nfixed width $k \\\\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine which, given\nany formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables, halts with the number\nof satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines 203--204), the\nanswer ``is that number positive'' on its answer tape and in its\nhalting state (lines 205--207), within the bound $C\\\\,(L+1)^d\\\\,2^{A\\\\rhon}$\n(lines 210--211).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"For each\nfixed width $k \\\\ge 2$ there is one deterministic machine with three\nproperties, given any formula of width at most $k$ on $n$ variables. It\nhalts with the number of satisfying assignments on its count tape (lines\n203--204). It has the answer ``is