"+and were re-read by item 10.","+","+## What this does not claim","+","+The refuter read one build of one PDF on one day; a finding it did not","+file is not a claim that none exists. Its checks of the clone were by","+`grep`, `sed` and its own small scripts, never by building anything.","+\"Agreed\" means the quoted text or number matched the source at the cited","+place; it does not mean the source is right."]}],"created":true},{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/refuter/REPORT.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":0,"newStart":1,"newLines":86,"lines":["+# Refuter report on paper3/latex/main.pdf (62 pages, built 2026-11-05 07:50 WET)","+","+**Summary.** I read the PDF text in full and checked every citable claim against the clone at 4eaee875, the manuscript extract, the two audits, the replay logs, the fetched related-work records, the companion Lean files and logs, the practitioner data files, and my own arithmetic. The paper's central numbers (every value of rho(n)/n, the peak, the crossing at 50, the return below 1 near e^5504 = 10^2390.36 with 2,391 digits, the clause-count bound 2,604,376, the constants 256+At / C+28700 / A+1 / S+5022741 / d+2 / e+2, the cutoff, every comparator line number, every manuscript page/line but one, every replay and nanoda number, every related-work quotation, the Lean companion claims, and all of Table 7.1) agree with the sources. Findings: **A: 6** (one false sentence about where \"ETH\" occurs in the Lean, one wrong manuscript page, a line-count artefact of +1 per file, a 41-vs-42 dependency count, two imprecise line citations); **B: 4**; **C: 1**; **D: 0**; **E: 0**; **F: 5**; **G: 5**. No finding touches the paper's stance or its verdict on the proof.","+","+Counting rule used for manuscript pages: `page(L) = 1 + (number of lines before line L that contain a form feed)`; form feeds sit at the start of lines 53, 104, 155, 208, 260, ... (67 in all, 3,815 lines). Under the alternative rule (count form feeds in lines up to and including L) every cited page comes out the same, including the one disagreement below.","+","+---","+","+## A — factual disagreements, wrong line/page numbers, wrong numbers","+","+**R1 (A).** p. 16, §3.5: *\"The hypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings \"ETH\" and \"SETH\" occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of a different family, lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/, which take an ETH-like proposition as a hypothesis and are not among the files the proof depends on, its closure, Section 4).\"* Checked with `grep -rnw ETH` over `lean/**/*.lean`. The string \"ETH\" occurs, besides the two ParityLifts files, in **six files of this family**, all inside comments: `SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean:6` (\"the unchanged ETH square-root-log time scale\"), `SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean:147`, `SqrtLogSpace/NativeQueryTimeBounds.lean:49`, `SqrtLogSpace/BooleanDifferences.lean:7` and `:228`, `SqrtLogSpace/NativeHashQueries.lean:377`; and the theorem name `exact_count_eth` (Final.lean:104) contains it in lower case. \"SETH\" indeed occurs nowhere as a word. The substantive point (no hypothesis is defined in the library) stands; the sentence as written is false.","+","+**R2 (A).** p. 48, §9.6: *\"In the manuscript, \"practical\" occurs once (page 4, line 153), in a sentence about the practical value of SAT solvers on industrial formulas\"*. Line 153 of the extract (\"Experiments on formulas from circuit synthesis and diagnosis demonstrated the practical value of\") is on **page 3**: form feeds precede it at lines 53 and 104 only; page 4 begins at line 155. (The paper's other page-4 citations, lines 165–205, are correct.) The count \"once\" and the eleven occurrences of \"exhaustive\" (lines 170, 311, 395, 411, 705, 2210, 2324, 2480, 2487, 2509, 3804) agree.","+","+**R3 (A).** p. 20, §4.2 and Table 4.1 p. 22: *\"Its closure inside the repository is 3,091 modules, ... totalling 550,551 lines; the largest file is 2,873 lines (SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean).\"* Recounted with `wc -l` and Python `splitlines()` over the same 3,091 modules (my own import-closure script reproduces the 3,091 names, 50 Mathlib modules, 1 core module and 886 outside files exactly): **547,460 lines and 2,872 lines**. The paper's figures are exactly 3,091 more in total and 1 more for the file, i.e. the audit's `closure.py` counts `len(text.split('\\n'))`, one more than `wc -l` for every file that ends in a newline. `reports/scratch/eth_audit/proof_audit.md` carries the same 550,551 and 2,873. Either state the counting convention or use the `wc -l` figures.","+","+**R4 (A).** p. 26, §5.1: *\"the network is on, because the repository's build file (its lakefile) clones its 41 dependencies at build time\"*. p. 23, §4.6 says *\"The manifest ... lists 42 packages\"* and Table 4.1 says \"manifest packages ... 42\". `lake-manifest.json` has 42 packages and the replay copy's `.lake/packages/` holds 42 directories (mathlib included); nothing is 41. The \"41\" comes from `paper3/replay/README.md` (\"41 packages under .lake/packages\"), which is wrong by the same one. Internal inconsistency 41 vs 42 within the paper.","+","+**R5 (A).** p. 7, §2.3: *\"it is produced mechanically (\"compiled\") from a program that uses 80 one-bit memory cells (\"registers\") and 1,220 tapes (SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 98–101)\"*, and p. 58, B.2: *\"compiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220 tapes, with countTape = 256 and answerTape = 1216 (SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 98–101)\"*. Lines 98–101 of Final.lean contain `1220`, `256` and `1216` but not `80`; the `80` is in `BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels` at line 11 (also 105, 150). B.3 (p. 59) cites line 11 correctly; §2.3 and B.2 do not.","+","+**R6 (A).** p. 59, B.3: *\"GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution (SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean, lines 158–162) turns a source run of n steps into RunsIn (primitive p).step (7 * n)\"*. `primitive_execution` is declared at **lines 160–162**; line 158 is the last line of `compiler_overhead`'s proof (`exact localCost_le_overhead ...`). §4.5's \"lines 152–162\" (spanning both lemmas) is fine.","+","+## B — numbers or quotations with no stated source or regenerate command","+","+**R7 (B).** p. 6, §2.2 (and p. 56, B.1): *\"A formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes 1,006,223 bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width 3 becomes 47.9 million bits.\"* The encoded length depends on which literals the formula contains (unary codes `2·var+sign`), not on (n, m, k) alone: emulating the comparator's definitions on five random width-4 formulas with n = 125, m = 500 gives 990,775; 1,013,703; 1,005,403; 1,017,823; 1,021,583 bits. The script `check_encoding.py` is named, but the formula (seed) that gives 1,006,223 is not, so the sentence reads as if the number were determined by the three parameters. Say \"one random formula with ...\" and name the seed, or quote only the bound 2,006,127 (which I reproduce).","+","+**R8 (B).** p. 26, §5.1: *\"not the collection's default target, which would build all 721 families\"*. No source or regenerate command. Cheap recounts give none of 721: `lean/OAI.lean` has 928 import lines, `lean/ComparatorChallenges/` has 418 JSONs and 418 `.lean` files, `lean/docs/` has 245 files. The replay README states 721 without derivation.","+","+**R9 (B).** p. 36, §7.1: *\"The script of this section checks that, reading every file of the family with open() and counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0.\"* The patterns are `#eval`, `def main`, \"IO\". A literal substring search for `def main` hits 6 declarations in 3 family files (`SubexpQuarticSelectorAdapter.lean:109 def mainProgram`, `SubexpSetupProcedure.lean:10,30,48 def mainExtraStacks/mainStack/mainProcedure`, `SubexpMainSparsify.lean:333,351`); the count is 0 only with a word boundary. The conclusion (no entry point) is right; the paper should state the pattern.","+","+**R10 (B, minor).** p. 61, B.4: *\"The 14,034 lines with decide are kernel-evaluated decision procedures\"*. My recount with a different (nested-comment-aware) stripper gives 14,078 lines containing the word `decide`. Method-dependent; the paper gives no definition of \"line with decide\" beyond naming `closure.py`.","+","+## C — sentences that read as overclaims beyond the Lean statement","+","+**R11 (C).** Title (p. 1): *\"...the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.\"* versus p. 4, §1.3, Not claimed 1: *\"No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. No implementation of it exists; the title compares two guarantees.\"* and p. 36, §7.1: *\"the verified algorithm's actual running time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says it is slow\"*. Taken literally, \"the algorithm is slower\" is a running-time claim the body expressly disowns; the body makes it true only through §2.5's gloss (\"that means the following four things, and nothing more\"). The title is fixed by the owner and is not to be altered (E); this is filed so the owner knows that the paper's own non-claim list contradicts the title's literal wording and that the reconciliation rests entirely on §2.5 and §7.4.","+","+## D — out of scope","+","+None found. The only graph vocabulary (independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings, p. 15; \"circuits, graphs, maps\", p. 14) is the manuscript's own quoted corollary objects, allowed by the brief; `SubexpPathwidth` appears only as the directory name; no sentence mentions treewidth, pathwidth, width parameters, crossovers or MOSP. The quoted §1.1.3 list (which mentions treewidth in the manuscript) is elided as \"[...]\".","+","+## E — title altered","+","+None. Page 1 carries the title with the ellipsis typeset as three spaced dots and 2^n as a superscript (the paper records this as S2); `pdfinfo` Title is exactly `Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.`; there are no running headers (the first line of every page differs); `paper3/lean/README.md` quotes the title verbatim.","+","+## F — plain-English stalls and internal inconsistencies","+","+**R12 (F).** Table 2.1 (p. 8) gives rho(50) = **50.14**; B.6 (p. 62) says *\"equal to 1 at n = 50 up to the rounding of rho(50) = 50.13\"*. rho(50) = 50.1351, so 50.14 is the rounding and 50.13 the truncation. Also \"equal to 1 up to rounding\" is generous: 2^(rho(50)-50) = 1.098.","+","+**R13 (F).** Table 7.1 (p. 37), column \"sat\", caption: *\"\"sat\", the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL finished\"*; rows n = 400 and n = 500 read **\"10/20\"** and **\"8/20\"** while only 10 and 8 instances finished (the raw CSV confirms 10/10 and 8/8 finished satisfiable). Under the caption's definition the cells should read 10/10 and 8/8, or the caption should say \"of the twenty\".","+","+**R14 (F).** p. 41, §8.1: *\"our file has no sorry and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script counts them: zero and zero in each of our two files)\"*. The word `sorry` occurs in `PrimitiveMachine.lean` line 10 (header comment: \"with its `sorry`\") and `axiom` in `Frame.lean` lines 13 and 83 (comments). `rediff.log` says \"0 `sorry`, 0 `axiom` declaration(s) **outside the header comment**\"; the paper's sentence drops the qualifier.","+","+**R15 (F).** Terms used before their gloss: `lean4export` and `nanoda_lib` appear in Table 4.2 rows (iv) and (v) and §4.8 (p. 25) and are glossed only in §5.3 (p. 27); `tmpfs` (p. 26) and `SHA-256` (pp. 27, 28, 44) are never glossed; the notation `m = ⌊4.26 n⌉` (mixed floor/ceiling brackets, pp. 36–38) is not explained (the data show rounding to nearest: 85 at n = 20). Everything else the brief lists (kernel, comparator, nanoda, sorry, CDCL, O_k(1), Prop/def/theorem/axiom, k-DNF, E^NP) is glossed at or before first use.","+","+**R16 (F).** Sentences over 45 words (word counts from the extracted text, single sentences only; quotations excluded): §3.2 p. 13 \"The proof is built around the parameters of the manuscript's Section 6.4 ...\" (**98**); §7.2 p. 36 \"Tools. CaDiCaL 1.9.5 ... on the host.\" (**151**, one sentence with semicolons); §5.1 p. 26 \"Inside it, the system directories are read-only; ...\" (**87**); §6.3 p. 34 \"The manuscript's one positive transfer is Set Splitting ...\" (**82**); §10.1 p. 49 \"In one sentence each: ...\" (**82**); §3.3 p. 15 \"The encodings are explicit: ...\" (**79**); §3.3 p. 15 \"For a graph on v vertices and e edges: ...\" (**74**); §9.3 p. 46 \"That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries ...\" (**73**); §7.3 p. 39 \"CaDiCaL's median is under a millisecond ...\" (**71**); §4 p. 20 \"There are known ways for that to happen: ...\" (**68**); §6.1 p. 29 \"For each fixed width k ≥ 2 there is one deterministic machine ...\" (**66**); §4.5 p. 23 \"The lemmas that connect the program to the machine ...\" (**66**); §9.6 p. 48 \"In the manuscript, \"practical\" occurs once ...\" (**66**); §3.3 p. 15 (Cor. 1.4 sentence with the quotation, **66**); §8.2 p. 41 \"It takes the 23 lines after def SubexpCount ...\" (**64**); §3.4 p. 16 \"The fixed-density proof ...\" (**64**); §6.3 p. 35 \"What such a transfer inherits ...\" (**63**); §4.8 p. 25 \"After the replay, then, the proof's trust rests on four things: ...\" (**62**); §7.3 p. 37 \"On every instance where both finished, ...\" (**60**); §6.3 p. 34 \"For shape (S): ...\" (**60**); §6.2 p. 33 \"Two sentences follow the refutation paragraph ...\" (**59**); and about forty more between 46 and 58 words, concentrated in §3.3, §4.3–4.7, §5, §6.2–6.3, §7.2–7.4, §9 and the entry lists of §10.2. The paper's plain-English rule is not met by these.","+","+## G — anything else","+","+**R17 (G).** p. 51: *\"Data, code and proofs. Placeholder. Item 11 completes this statement ...\"* and *\"Acknowledgements. Placeholder. Item 11 writes the acknowledgements, with the disclosure of AI use in the form of paper1/latex/sec8_ack.tex.\"* Placeholder text in the built PDF; §10.2 entry 09d (p. 51) refers to \"the assistant the acknowledgements disclose\", which do not yet exist.","+","+**R18 (G).** p. 13, §3.2: *\"the manuscript's exponents are undefined below n = e and are stated for n ≥ 20\"*. ln ln n is undefined only for n ≤ 1; for 1 < n < e it is defined and negative (so the ratios are negative, not undefined).","+","+**R19 (G).** The records the paper cites as its sources disagree with the paper on counts the paper got right: `paper3/replay/README.md` says the 19 in-build axiom lines come \"from the 17 source files of the closure\" (the paper and the log: 8 files in the closure, 17 in the family); `proof_audit.md` says \"41 manifest packages\" (42), \"33 definitions\" (the paper: 35 compared, 33 identical, 2 up to `noncomputable`), and places the challenge theorem at \"SubexponentialSAT.lean:181\" (line 194); `statement_audit.md` says `enable_nanoda: false` \"on 416 of the 418\" (413, with 2 true and 3 absent). The paper corrects these silently except for §10.2's 04a/04b/04d/04e; a reader who opens the cited audit will find different numbers.","+","+**R20 (G).** Figure 7.1 caption (p. 38) opens *\"The same data on a logarithmic time axis\"* with no antecedent in the figure; it means Table 7.1's data (the body says \"Figure 7.1 draws the same\" one page earlier).","+","+**R21 (G).** p. 5, §2.2: *\"The manuscript says the same thing in its own words: \"Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than k literals\" (p. 1, line 44).\"* The quotation spans lines 44–45 (\"...fewer than k\" / \"literals\"); trivial.","+","+---","+","+## What was checked and agreed (coverage)","+","+- **Title**: verbatim on p. 1 (typographic ellipsis/superscript only) and in the PDF metadata; no running headers; 62 pages.","+- **Comparator file, every cited line**: 220 lines; SAT 13–51 (Literal 19–22, Clause/CNF 24–25, Assignment 17, evals 29–36, WidthAtMost 38, satisfyingAssignments 44–45, count 47); encoding 53–81 (encodeNat 55, frame 57–58, encodeList 60–62, literalCode 64–65, clauseNumbers/cnfNumbers 67–71, encodeCNF 73–77, formulaBitLength 79); toNat 7–9; Trace 87–90; PrimitiveMachine 92–99; Cfg 103–105; step 107–119; init 121–124; machine namespace 83–128; runtimeScale 132–134 (natural log, +16); runtimeBound 136–139; countWord 147; LocatedCfg 149–153, locatedInit 155–159, headDisplacement 161–164, monitor 166–182, locatedStep 184–186, visitedSpace 188–189; theorem 194–217 with every conjunct at the cited line (201–202, 203–204, 205–206, 207, 208, 209, 210–211, 212–217); `sorry` at 218; namespace end 220; `∃ machine` at 195; comparator statement SHA-256 4183b205...","+- **Solution files**: Final.lean 201 lines; theorems at 10, 74, 104–147 (20 ≤ n at 109; constants `C*(8k+7)^d, 4*A, S*(8k+7)^e, 2*d, 2*e` at 131–132), 149–183 (3 ≤ k; `answer = true ↔ Satisfiable` at 164), 184; witnesses `C + 28700, A + 1, (S : ℝ) + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2` at **line 41**; program at 40; `(256 : Fin 1220), (1216 : Fin 1220)` at 100; `#print axioms` ×5 at 197–201; imports 1–3. NativeLargeEntry.lean: `1 ≤ C ∧ 1 ≤ A` at 12, runtimeBound at 27, uniform_contract at 39, runtime_of_tree_and_leaf 46, `Filter.eventually_atTop` 48, `cutoff := max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)` at **50**, `256 + At, db + (4 * (k - 2) + 2) + df` at **51**. NativeLeafContract.lean `1 ≤ At` at **793** (theorem at 791–793). NativeEntry.lean 81–100, `28700 * (L + 1)^2` at 92. NativeOuterBudgets.lean 297, 317. NativeOuter.lean 50–51, 53–56, 73. CompileSpace.lean 125–143 (134, 135, 143). GuardedCompiler.lean: selected 14–17, transition 30–47, primitive 131, equivFin 132, compiler_overhead 152–153, primitive_located_step 600. Asymptotics.lean rho 10–11 (base 2), sparsifyLambda/Nu/Schedule 14–20, runtimeScale_le_four_rho 190–191. BitProgram.lean 12 instruction kinds at 11–23, Program 26–30. SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean `noncomputable section` at 15; SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean 9–14; EncodingBounds.lean countWord at 17; StatementCanonical.lean MainTheorem at 37 (3 ≤ width); FiniteFallback.lean:14 and SparseCharging.lean:5 import `Lean.Elab.Tactic.Omega`; Main.lean 16 lines, declares nothing; the seven re-declaring files exist with the named definitions; 35 statement definitions enumerated.","+- **Closure recount (own script)**: 3,091 modules, all in the family, 50 Mathlib, 1 core; 886 of 3,977 outside, with no sorry/axiom/native_decide/admit; keyword counts with comments stripped: axiom/sorry/admit/native_decide/ofReduceBool 0; implemented_by/extern/unsafe/partial 0; elab/macro/syntax/simproc 0; set_option 2 (both `quotPrecheck false`, RecoveryQuery:15, RecoveryWitness:14); opaque 9 (RecoveryQuery:59 with `:= by` at 86, RecoveryWitness:178,198, QueryCleanup:22 + five); noncomputable 1,048; Classical.choice 4; propext 4; open Classical 8; instance 148; local instance 40; termination_by 9; decreasing_by 5; `#print axioms` 19 in 8 closure files, 35 in 17 family files, 9 outside. Identifier search: SimpleGraph, PerfectMatching, Coloring, IndependentSet, CircuitSize, OrthogonalVectors, MaxSat, TensorNetwork, HermiteInterp, ENP, SIZE, SetSplitting, GapETH, NSETH all absent; `#eval` 0; word `IO` 0.","+- **Repository facts**: lean-toolchain v4.34.1; lakefile line 8 `fixedToolchain := true`, line 9 `autoImplicit false`, line 89 `@[default_target] lean_lib OAI`, run_cmd 290–304 with `unsafe env.evalConstCheck` (296) and eleven `preResolutionPatchNames` (188–191), post_update 306; OAI.lean imports the family only at line 49; 23 `.patch` files (none for Mathlib); 42 manifest packages; Mathlib rev d13f23b7...; formalization.yaml 1683–1685; `.github/workflows/` = lock-prs.yml only; no \"depends on axioms\" anywhere in the clone; 418 JSONs: 413 false / 2 true / 3 absent; the family JSON's six fields; ComparatorChallenges/README.md 10 lines (3, 7–9, QuasiRiemannHypothesis example); docs/378.md 17 lines, lines 9, 11, 17 quoted verbatim; history.md 56 lines, line 11 heading, line 18